window.SYLVA_EN = window.SYLVA_EN || { ui: {}, content: {} };
window.SYLVA_EN.content = {"guide":{"mental":{"titre":"Mindset and priorities","resume":"Keep a cool head, know what to do first, decide whether to stay or go.","sections":[{"titre":"Survival starts in your head","p":["In most survival situations, it is neither cold nor hunger that tips things over, but panic: you rush, you scatter your efforts, you take a wrong direction, you forget to eat or to shelter.","So the first reflex to learn is to slow down. A situation that seems catastrophic often becomes manageable as soon as you break it into small, simple problems, dealt with in the right order."]},{"titre":"The STOP method","etapes":["Stop: don't move, sit down, take a sip of water, breathe slowly.","Think: what happened? What is really urgent? Who knows where I am?","Observe: the surroundings (shelter, water, wood, dangers), the weather, the time of sunset, your gear, your physical state.","Plan: decide only the next 2 or 3 actions, then act calmly."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"Stopping for five minutes never wastes time. Setting off in the wrong direction does."}},{"titre":"The order of priorities","p":["The rule of 3 gives the logical order: you die in 3 minutes without air, in 3 hours without protection in cold, wet weather, in 3 days without water and in 3 weeks without food. In practice, in our climates:"],"etapes":["Life-threatening emergencies: bleeding, serious injury, unconscious person (see First aid).","Protect yourself from cold and wet: dry clothes, shelter, insulation from the ground.","Signal and alert the emergency services if needed.","Water: find it and make it safe to drink.","Fire: warmth, morale, boiled water, signal.","Food, last: it is almost never the emergency."]},{"titre":"Stay or go?","p":["If someone knows your route and your return time, staying put is almost always the best decision: rescuers search around the planned route first, and a person who is still and visible is found much faster.","Leaving is justified if the place is dangerous (flood, falling rocks, extreme cold with no possible shelter), if nobody will look for you, or if you know exactly where to go and can get there before nightfall."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"If you leave, leave a clearly visible message (branches forming an arrow, a note under a stone) showing your direction and the time."}},{"titre":"Assessing a risk before acting","p":["Crossing this stream, going down this steep slope, walking another hour before dark: each decision is weighed with two simple questions. How likely is it to go wrong? And if it goes wrong, how serious is it?"],"liste":["Likely AND serious (crossing a river in flood): give up.","Unlikely but serious (camping under a dead tree in strong wind): choose another option, it is often easy.","Likely but minor (getting your feet wet): accept it, with a plan to deal with it (dry socks).","Every precaution has a cost (time, energy, weight): choose it according to the real risk, not fear."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"What distorts judgement","texte":"Fatigue, lack of sleep, cold, hunger, dehydration and the urge to “finish anyway” (especially in a group) lead to bad decisions. If you recognise one of them, slow down and choose the most cautious option."}},{"titre":"Keeping your spirits up","liste":["Set small, achievable goals: gather wood, then put up the shelter, then boil the water.","Keep a routine: eat at set times, tidy your camp, tend the fire.","Talk (out loud if need be), sing, write down what you do: it keeps the mind busy and helps you think.","Look after yourself: a washed face, dry feet, a hot drink change everything.","In a group: share out roles, make decisions together, watch over the most vulnerable."]}]},"crises":{"titre":"Crises: holding out for 72 hours","sections":[{"titre":"Why 72 hours","p":["In a disaster, rescuers go first to the most serious situations. During the first days, every household must be able to cope on its own: this has been the European Union's recommendation since 2025, confirmed in November 2026."]},{"titre":"The three steps","etapes":["Get informed: your country's alert system, radio, official sources.","Make a plan: contacts, meeting point, everyone's role.","Build up the home stock and the evacuation bag, little by little."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"In Bivaska","texte":"Today > Ready 72 h: your household, your calculated kit, your stock with dates and your family plan, all working offline."}}]},"preparer":{"titre":"Preparing your trip","resume":"Route, someone to tell, pocket survival kit, clothing: 90% of safety is decided before you leave.","sections":[{"titre":"Tell someone","p":["This is the most important rule in this whole guide. Before each trip, give a loved one: your planned route, where you will sleep, your return time, and the time after which they should call the emergency services if they haven't heard from you."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"One person who knows where you are is worth more than all the gear in the world."}},{"titre":"Preparing the route","liste":["Check the rules of the place: bivouac allowed, fires, protected areas (see the Law and ethics chapter).","Locate water points, escape routes (roads, villages) and areas without signal.","Check the weather the day before and on the day, and the time of sunset (Bivouac tab).","Allow plenty of time: you move more slowly than you think with a loaded pack, in a forest or at night.","Keep the map cached in Bivaska and take a paper map."]},{"titre":"The pocket survival kit","p":["A small waterproof case that never leaves your pocket or belt, even for a short walk. If it is in the pack and the pack is lost, it is useless."],"liste":["Lighter + fire steel (ferrocerium) + a few fire starters","Survival blanket","Whistle","Small torch or head torch","Knife or multi-tool","5 m of strong cord","Water purification tablets","A few plasters, dressings and a tick remover","An energy bar","A pencil and waterproof paper"]},{"titre":"Dressing in layers","p":["The body cools quickly when it is wet, from rain or from sweat. Rather than one big garment, you stack layers that you take off and put back on according to effort and weather."],"etapes":["Base layer next to the skin: merino wool or synthetic, never cotton (it holds moisture and chills you).","Warm layer: fleece or down jacket.","Shell: waterproof and windproof jacket and trousers."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"Take off a layer BEFORE you sweat going uphill, put it back on AS SOON as you stop. Keep a dry outfit in a waterproof bag, for the night only."}},{"titre":"Materials, in brief","liste":["Cotton: pleasant when dry, but it holds water and chills you as soon as it is wet. Avoid it in cool, damp weather.","Wool (merino): warm even when a little damp, doesn't hold odours. Ideal as a base layer and for socks.","Fleece: light, dries quickly, warm even when damp, but lets the wind through.","Down (feathers): the best warmth-to-weight ratio… as long as it stays dry. Wet, it hardly warms at all.","Synthetic (polyester wadding): a little heavier than down, but keeps a good part of its warmth when damp. The right choice in our rainy climates.","Waterproof breathable membranes: block rain and wind while letting some vapour out. Open the vents during effort."]},{"titre":"The bivouac pack","p":["The full checklist, editable and with your photos, is in the Bivouac tab. The essentials: shelter (tent or tarp), sleeping system suited to the night temperature, insulating mat, stove, water, head torch, first-aid kit, charged phone and power bank."]}]},"campement":{"titre":"The camp","resume":"Choosing the spot, organising your camp, sleeping warm and dry.","sections":[{"titre":"Arrive early","p":["Setting up camp at night, in the rain and tired, is the best way to sleep badly and put yourself in danger. Plan to arrive at least 1 h 30 before sunset (shown in the Bivouac tab). In winter, night sometimes falls before 5 pm."]},{"titre":"Choosing the spot","liste":["Legal: bivouac area, private land with permission, or an area where bivouacking is tolerated.","Above you: no dead branch and no dead or leaning tree. These branches fall without warning, especially in wind.","Under you: flat ground, without roots or stones, slightly domed so that water runs off. Not in a hollow.","Around: not at the bottom of a valley (cold air sinks there at night) nor right at the edge of a river (damp, rising water).","Sheltered from the prevailing wind by terrain, a copse or a rock.","Not on a game trail, near a burrow or a wasps' or processionary caterpillars' nest.","More than 50 m from water, to protect it and not disturb the animals that come to drink."]},{"titre":"Look up, around and down","p":["Before putting down your pack, two minutes of observation avoid most bivouac accidents: a dead branch above the shelter, a stream that can rise, a hollow where cold air collects, a wild boar path. The sheet “Spotting the dangers of the camp” details each point."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"A dead branch can fall without a breath of wind. Never sleep under dead wood, even for a single night."}},{"titre":"Organising your camp","p":["A well-organised camp means less lost gear and fewer accidents. Place the areas relative to each other, taking the wind into account:"],"liste":["Sleeping area: upwind, away from smoke.","Fire and cooking area (if allowed): downwind, at least 3 m from the tent.","Wood store: within reach of the fire but not too close, sheltered from rain.","Water area: bottles and boiled water kept apart from raw water, so you never mix them up.","Toilets: more than 50 m from the camp and the water, downwind.","Food: closed, hung or stored outside the tent at night (wild boar, foxes, rodents)."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"Always keep head torch, knife and water bottle in the same place in the tent: you'll find them in the dark."}},{"titre":"Sleeping warm","etapes":["Insulate from the ground: cold ground draws away your heat much faster than air. An insulating mat, or failing that 20 to 30 cm of dead leaves, ferns or dry needles.","Eat a fatty, sugary meal before sleeping: the body produces heat while digesting.","Have a hot drink, then go to the toilet before bed: a full bladder cools you down.","Sleep with a hat, dry socks and the dry outfit kept for the night.","Slip a well-closed bottle of hot water into the sleeping bag (hot-water bottle).","Never sleep in the damp clothes of the day."]},{"titre":"The COLD rule","p":["Military cold-weather manuals sum up the art of staying warm in four letters:"],"liste":["C, clean: dirt and grease crush the air pockets in clothing, which then insulates less well.","O, avoid overheating: open up and remove a layer as soon as you sweat, because sweat chills you as it dries.","L, loose and in layers: nothing tight, several thin layers rather than one thick one.","D, dry: protect your clothes from water, dry socks and gloves against your body or on your pack."]},{"titre":"Managing damp","p":["Damp is enemy number one when bivouacking in our climates. Condensation forms inside the tent when the warm, moist air of your breath touches the cold fabric."],"liste":["Keep a vent open, even in cold weather.","Don't cook or dry clothes in the tent.","Wipe off condensation in the morning with a small cloth.","Let the sleeping bag dry in the sun or air as soon as possible."]},{"titre":"Breaking camp","p":["In the morning, take things down in reverse order, check that the fire is completely out, take everything with you, and leave the spot as you found it: grass straightened, leaves scattered. The best bivouac is one whose existence nobody can guess."]},{"titre":"Animals around the camp","p":["In Belgium and neighbouring countries, night visitors are foxes, wild boar, martens and above all mice. They come for food and smells. Anything edible or scented (toothpaste included) goes in a closed bag, hung away from where you sleep. A wild boar won't attack a camper, but it will turn your pack over if it smells a meal."]},{"titre":"When it rains","p":["In the rain, put up the roof first, settle underneath, then change into dry clothes straight away. Wet clothes go back on the next day for walking: the night outfit must stay dry, whatever it takes."]}]},"abris":{"titre":"Shelters","resume":"Tarp, hammock, natural and emergency shelters: choose the shelter that suits the weather, put it up quickly and well, and sleep warm and dry in it.","sections":[{"titre":"What a shelter is for","p":["A shelter isn't only for the rain. It cuts the wind (which can halve the felt temperature), keeps in body heat and insulates you from the ground. So a good shelter is small, waterproof, out of the wind and well insulated underneath."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"The smaller a shelter, the warmer it is. You build for your body, not for comfort."}},{"titre":"Choosing a shelter for the weather","liste":["Dry, mild weather: a simple roof or an open lean-to is enough, and lets you see the sky.","Rain: a low A-frame, well tensioned, with drip lines. Always insulate the ground well.","Wind: plough-point or very low A-frame, narrow end to the wind. Never an open lean-to facing the wind.","Cold: a small, low shelter that your own warmth can heat, with lots of ground insulation. A natural shelter covered with leaves is often warmer than a tarp.","Snow: a covered trench or the hollow at the foot of a spruce, with a vent that stays open.","Wet or sloping ground: the hammock, with insulation underneath and a tarp above."]},{"titre":"The right shelter gear","liste":["A 3 × 3 m tarp: big enough for one person and their pack, light enough (400 to 900 g). With 3 × 4 m you can make a groundsheet.","At least 15 m of strong cord, in several pieces: 5 m for the ridgeline, the rest for the corners.","6 to 8 light pegs. You can also carve them on site from dead wood.","A groundsheet or a big bin bag, to protect the mat from damp.","A few bits of string for drip lines, and wide straps if you sleep in a hammock."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"Practise pitching your tarp in your garden or in a park, in daylight and then by head torch. On bivouac night, in the rain and the dark, the moves must be automatic."}},{"titre":"With a tarp","p":["A 3 × 3 m tarp and 15 m of cord allow many configurations. The most useful ones:"],"liste":["The A-frame: cord stretched between two trees, tarp draped over it, edges pegged. Protects on all sides except the ends.","The lean-to: the tarp stretched as a slope on one side only, open towards the fire (where allowed). Good view, but exposed to wind.","The closed lean-to: like the lean-to, with one side folded down to the ground facing the prevailing wind.","The simple roof: tarp stretched flat with a slight slope, for shelter during the day or over a hammock."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"Tension the ridgeline with a trucker's hitch and adjust the guylines with taut-line hitches: the tarp won't flap in the wind."}},{"titre":"Natural shelters","p":["Without gear, the forest provides everything. These shelters take time (1 to 3 hours) and energy: start them well before night. Keep them for real emergencies or for land where it is allowed, using dead wood only."],"liste":["The debris hut: a ridgepole resting on a stump, ribs on each side, and a thick layer of dead leaves. The warmest shelter without gear.","The branch lean-to: a pole between two trees, poles leaning against it, covered with branches like roof tiles.","Existing shelters: rock overhang, large fallen tree. Check they are stable and that no animal is living there."]},{"titre":"Emergency shelters in 5 minutes","liste":["The survival blanket: wrap yourself tightly in it, covering your head too. Which side (gold or silver) faces out matters little: what matters is being well wrapped, out of the wind and insulated from the ground.","The big bin bag: cut a hole for the head, and it becomes a poncho that blocks wind and rain.","The emptied backpack under your legs and feet, to insulate from the ground.","The hollow at the foot of a large conifer: the low branches form a natural roof."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"A survival blanket without ground insulation is not enough: you lose a lot of heat through contact with cold, wet ground."}},{"titre":"A night without a sleeping bag","p":["An unplanned night without a sleeping bag is handled with three priorities: insulate from the ground, protect from wind and trap air around the body. Dry dead leaves stuffed between two layers of clothing make a surprisingly effective insulator. The sheet “Spending the night without a sleeping bag” details what to do."]},{"titre":"Always insulate the ground","p":["Whatever the shelter, the layer under your body matters as much as the roof. Dry dead leaves, ferns, pine needles or fine branches: you need 20 to 30 cm, because they compress under your weight. A layer that looks huge in the evening is often 5 cm by morning."]},{"titre":"The most common mistakes","liste":["Setting up too late: a shelter goes up in daylight. Aim for at least an hour before sunset.","Making it too big: a big shelter is cold and catches the wind. You build for your body.","Forgetting the ground: you can have a perfect roof and shiver all night from underneath.","Not looking up properly: a dead branch above the shelter is danger number one.","Pitching the tarp flat: water pools and ends up coming through. Always a slope."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"A good shelter is a good spot, a sloping roof, insulated ground and a small size. In that order."}}]},"feu":{"titre":"Fire","resume":"Understand fire, light it even when wet, keep it going, cook and put it out. Always where it is allowed.","sections":[{"titre":"First: is it allowed?","p":["Fires in forests are forbidden or tightly controlled almost everywhere in Europe: in Wallonia, only at designated spots; in France, forbidden in woods and within 200 m of them for anyone who is not the owner, with total bans in summer in many départements. A stove remains the normal way to cook on a bivouac.","In a real emergency (hypothermia, unplanned night), a careful, controlled fire can save a life: it is then a survival tool, not a leisure activity."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"Never light a fire in a drought, in strong wind, under low branches, on peat, near the tent or inside a closed shelter."}},{"titre":"The fire triangle","p":["A fire needs three things: fuel, heat and oxygen. If just one is missing, it goes out. Most failures come from fuel that is too big or too damp (not enough heat to catch) or a fire packed too tightly (not enough air)."]},{"titre":"The three materials","liste":["Tinder: what catches from a spark or a flame. Birch bark (burns even when damp thanks to its oils), dry grass, thistle or bulrush down, very fine shavings, cotton soaked in petroleum jelly (prepare it at home).","Kindling: twigs from the thickness of a match to that of a pencil. Dead twigs still on the tree, at the bottom of spruces, are the driest.","Fuel wood: from thumb to wrist thickness, then bigger. Only dead wood; wood that snaps cleanly with a dry crack is dry, wood that bends is damp."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"Prepare twice as much of each material as you need BEFORE lighting. Running off for wood while the flame dies means starting again."}},{"titre":"Ways to light it","liste":["The lighter: the simplest. Keep it warm in an inside pocket (gas works badly in the cold).","Storm matches: burn even in the rain, keep them in a waterproof box.","The fire steel (ferrocerium): works wet and thousands of times. It needs very fine tinder.","A magnifying glass or the polished bottom of a can: only in bright sun.","Friction (bow drill): very difficult, needs perfectly dry wood and a lot of practice. Learn it at home, not on the day you need it."]},{"titre":"Building the fire","p":["The teepee is the ideal starting structure: the flame rises in the centre and catches the wood arranged in a cone. Once the fire has taken well, you can switch to a log cabin of crossed logs that gives lasting embers for cooking, or a star fire (big logs whose ends you push towards the centre) to last all night with little wood."],"etapes":["Clear the ground down to bare earth, or build a platform of sticks on wet ground.","Put the tinder in the centre, the finest kindling in a cone around it, open on the wind side.","Light it at ground level, shield the flame from the wind with your body.","Gradually add bigger wood without crushing the structure, blow gently at the base.","Never leave a fire unattended."]},{"titre":"Other fire structures","p":["Depending on the weather and the use, other layouts do better than the teepee. Each has its own detailed sheet with a diagram:"],"liste":["The lean-to fire: a green stick angled at 30° into the wind protects the tinder. Ideal in wind or drizzle.","The cross trench: a cross dug under the tinder creates a draught when there is no wind.","The pyramid: layers of crossed logs, lit from the top. The fire burns down slowly and heats all night without constant tending.","The Dakota hole: a buried fire with an air inlet. Discreet, not very sensitive to wind, economical with wood, perfect for a pot.","The reflector wall: logs stacked behind the fire send the heat back to you and cut the wind."]},{"titre":"Lighting without a lighter","p":["The “old-fashioned” methods work, provided you have practised them beforehand. They all rely on a good material that catches the ember."],"liste":["Char cloth: squares of cotton baked without air in a metal tin. It catches the smallest spark and is prepared at home for the survival kit.","Tinder fungus: this hoof-shaped fungus grows on dead beech and birch. Its felty layer, well dried and beaten, holds an ember for a long time.","The bow drill: a spindle spun with a bow on a board of soft wood until an ember forms. The most reliable friction method, but physical.","A magnifying glass or the polished bottom of a can: only in full sun."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"Keep a few squares of char cloth in a waterproof tin with your fire steel: it's the most reliable pair in all weathers."}},{"titre":"In wet weather","liste":["Look for dry material: dead twigs under conifers, birch bark, the inside of dead stumps.","Split dead wood: the heart is dry even when the outside is soaked.","Make feather sticks: carve long, thin curls without detaching them from the stick (see the Knife chapter).","Light the fire on a platform of sticks, not on wet earth.","Dry the next wood around the fire, without putting it in."]},{"titre":"Using the fire","liste":["Warming up: a wall of logs, a rock face or a tarp behind the fire sends the heat back towards you (reflector fire).","Boiling water: the only truly safe improvised method.","Cooking: on the embers, not the flames (see the Cooking chapter).","Drying clothes and socks, at a reasonable distance: synthetics melt quickly.","Signalling: white smoke by day (add green leaves), a bright flame at night."]},{"titre":"Putting it out completely","etapes":["Stop adding wood an hour before leaving or sleeping.","Drown the embers with plenty of water.","Stir with a stick and drown again.","Hold the back of your hand above: no heat should be felt.","Scatter the cold ashes and erase the traces."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"An “extinguished” fire covered with earth can smoulder for hours underground and flare up again. Always drown and stir."}}]},"eau_guide":{"titre":"Water","resume":"How much to drink, recognising dehydration, finding water and making it safe to drink.","sections":[{"titre":"How much to drink","p":["At rest and in mild weather, an adult needs about 2 to 3 litres of water a day. Effort, heat, dry cold and altitude greatly increase this need. It is better to drink small amounts often than a litre in one go."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"In survival, you don't ration the water you have: you ration sweating. Get into the shade, slow down, keep your head covered."}},{"titre":"Recognising dehydration","liste":["Thirst, dry mouth, dark and scanty urine: drink without waiting.","Headache, tiredness, cramps, irritability: dehydration has set in.","Confusion, dizziness, no urine: emergency, call for help."]},{"titre":"Finding water","liste":["Springs, streams and rivers: prefer running water, upstream of villages, pastures and campsites.","Valley bottoms, greener and denser vegetation, insects and birds flying in the morning and evening often point to water.","Rainwater, collected with a tarp: the most generous improvised source.","Morning dew, collected with a cloth: a very limited extra.","Listed drinking water points: shown on Bivaska's map (“Drinking water” layer)."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"Avoid stagnant water, water near cultivated fields (pesticides), roads or industrial sites, and seawater (which makes dehydration worse)."}},{"titre":"Making water safe","p":["Even the clearest wild water can contain bacteria, viruses or parasites (such as Giardia) that cause severe diarrhoea, which then makes dehydration worse."],"liste":["Boiling: 1 minute at a rolling boil (3 minutes above 2,000 m). The only completely safe improvised method against germs.","Hiking filter: effective against bacteria and parasites, not always against viruses depending on the model.","Tablets (chlorine, chlorine dioxide): respect the stated waiting time, longer if the water is cold.","Pre-filtering (cloth, sand-charcoal filter): clears cloudy water but does not disinfect it. Always boil afterwards.","Without a pot: dry stones heated in the fire, dropped one by one into a container, will boil water (see the dedicated sheet)."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"No improvised method removes chemical pollutants. If in doubt about where water comes from, look for another source."}},{"titre":"Collection methods","p":["When there is no running water, you can collect a little. According to a 2025 scientific review, here is what to expect:"],"liste":["Rainwater: about 5 to 10 litres per shower with a tarp. By far the most worthwhile.","Transpiration bag on a branch: from a few decilitres to 1 litre a day, in the sun.","Solar still (hole + plastic): 0.3 to 0.6 litres a day, spending a lot of energy digging. Rarely worthwhile in our climates.","Dew: about a glass. Just a supplement."]}]},"nourriture":{"titre":"Food","resume":"Why food comes after everything else, foraging without poisoning yourself, and what the law says.","sections":[{"titre":"A low priority… but not zero","p":["A healthy adult can last several weeks without eating. On a bivouac of a few days, hunger is mainly a matter of comfort and morale. On the other hand, eating helps fight the cold: in cold weather, the body needs much more energy to keep its temperature up.","So the real survival rule is to bring your own food: energy-dense foods (dried fruit, nuts, chocolate, hard cheese, pasta, oat flakes), and an emergency reserve that is never touched."]},{"titre":"The golden rule of foraging","etapes":["Only eat what you identify with absolute certainty, checking several features (leaves, stem, flower, smell, habitat), never just one.","Know the dangerous look-alikes of each species: that is what each Bivaska sheet is for.","Never rely on a single photo or an identification app.","Avoid the whole umbellifer family (umbrella-shaped flowers): several are deadly and they all look alike.","Start with a small amount of a species new to you, even an edible one (allergies, intolerances).","Wash or cook what grows at ground level (parasites spread by foxes), and avoid roadsides and treated fields."]},{"titre":"Why not the “universal edibility test”","p":["Some military manuals describe a test that involves rubbing a plant on the skin, then the lip, then chewing a little and waiting several hours. In Europe, this test is dangerous: hemlock, hemlock water-dropwort or yew can kill in small doses, even before the test's symptoms appear. And it doesn't apply to mushrooms at all."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"Never taste an unknown plant or mushroom “to see”. No certainty, no picking."}},{"titre":"The best wild resources","liste":["Easy fruits with no dangerous look-alike: blackberries, raspberries, bilberries, wild strawberries, hazelnuts, sweet chestnuts (checking against horse chestnut).","Abundant, safe leaves: nettle (cooked), dandelion, plantain, wild garlic (with extreme caution regarding lily of the valley and autumn crocus).","Autumn and winter fruits: rose hips, sloes after the frosts, roasted beechnuts.","Wetlands: bulrush (shoots and cooked rhizomes), only in clean water, being wary of yellow flag iris.","Mushrooms: only if a mycologist has checked the harvest. In a real survival situation, the energy they provide isn't worth the risk."]},{"titre":"Insects","p":["Rich in protein, they are the most accessible and safest animal resource in a real emergency. Grasshoppers, locusts, crickets and wood grubs are always eaten cooked (roasted), with legs and wings removed. Avoid brightly coloured, hairy or stinging insects or those that smell strongly, as well as caterpillars."]},{"titre":"Fishing, hunting and traps: the law","p":["In Belgium as in France, Switzerland or Germany, fishing requires a licence or a fishing permit, and hunting or setting traps without authorisation is poaching. The snares and deadfalls described in survival manuals should therefore only be considered in a real life-threatening emergency, and dismantled before you leave."]}]},"poissons":{"titre":"Fish and fishing","resume":"What you are allowed to fish, what you can eat, what you must release, and the real dangers: pollution, parasites, spines and venom.","sections":[{"titre":"First, the law","p":["In Wallonia as in France, fishing requires a licence, and each species has its seasons, minimum sizes and sometimes quotas. Some species must always be released: eel, salmon, and protected species such as bullhead or the noble crayfish. The sheet “Fishing: what the law says” summarises the rules."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"If in doubt about a species or a rule: release it. A fish put back costs you nothing, a protected species taken can cost a lot."}},{"titre":"Eat or release: the three groups","liste":["Good to eat, within the rules: trout, perch, zander, roach, chub, pike (watch the bones and teeth).","Eat with caution: large bottom-feeders and old predators (carp, catfish, barbel, big pike), which concentrate pollutants. Barbel roe is poisonous.","Always release: eel, salmon, grayling, bullhead, noble crayfish.","Invasive species (signal crayfish, black bullhead) can be eaten, but must never be transported alive."]},{"titre":"Pollution, an invisible danger","p":["PCBs, dioxins, mercury and PFAS can't be seen or smelled and don't disappear with cooking. They build up in fish fat over the years. Simple rule: small, young, lean and caught upstream, away from towns. Never eel. Remove skin and fat."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"Never eat a fish found dead or a fish caught in an area where a ban on taking fish is posted."}},{"titre":"Parasites: always cook","p":["Some freshwater fish (pike, perch, coarse fish) can carry parasitic worms transmissible to humans, such as the fish tapeworm. Thorough cooking destroys them: the flesh must be opaque and come away from the bone. No tartare, no ceviche, no barely seared river fish."]},{"titre":"Spines, teeth and venom","liste":["Perch and zander: dorsal spines. Fold the fin down before grabbing the fish.","Pike: very sharp teeth. Never put your fingers in its mouth.","Catfish (bullhead): hard spines and a very painful sting.","In the North Sea and the Atlantic, the weever hides in the sand; in the Mediterranean, the scorpionfish: venomous spines. The venom is treated with very hot water."]},{"titre":"In a real survival situation","p":["An improvised line (pin, thorn, gorge hook) and bait found under stones can feed you. But fishing is slow and uncertain: a healthy adult lasts weeks without eating, only a few days without water, and only a few hours if very cold. The priorities remain shelter, water, fire and signalling."]}]},"cuisine":{"titre":"Bivouac cooking","resume":"Cooking on a stove or on embers, without utensils, and a few recipes with foraged food.","sections":[{"titre":"Stove or fire?","p":["A stove (gas, alcohol, wood) is quick, clean and allowed where fires are not. It remains the main bivouac tool. A wood fire is for designated places or emergencies.","With a stove: always cook outside or under a well-ventilated porch, never in the tent (fire and carbon monoxide), on flat, stable ground, protected from the wind by a windshield."]},{"titre":"Cooking on the fire","liste":["Cook on the embers, not the flames: they heat evenly and don't blacken everything.","For the pot: rest it on two big parallel logs or two dry stones, or hang it from a tripod.","Never use river stones near the fire: the water they contain can make them burst.","Never skewer food with yew, oleander or cherry laurel wood (poisonous)."]},{"titre":"Cooking without a pot","liste":["In a parcel: wrap the food in aluminium foil (or large edible leaves, such as lime), and put it in the embers.","On a flat stone: a dry, flat stone heated at the edge of the fire works as a griddle.","On a spit: on a fresh hazel branch, above the embers.","In the ashes: scored chestnuts, potatoes and apples cook straight in the embers."]},{"titre":"Recipes with foraged food","liste":["Nettle soup: young leaves picked with gloves, boiled for 10 minutes with a stock cube and a few instant mash flakes.","Embers-roasted chestnuts: score each chestnut with a cross, 15 to 20 minutes in the embers, turning them.","Pine tea: a handful of chopped pine needles steeped for 10 minutes (beware of yew).","Rose hip tea: fruits opened and cleared of their hairs, boiled and then carefully strained through a cloth.","Wild garlic pesto: leaves identified one by one, crushed with oil, salt and hazelnuts."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"Every wild ingredient must have been identified with certainty. If in doubt about a single leaf, throw everything away."}},{"titre":"Kitchen hygiene","liste":["Wash your hands before cooking (hand gel or soapy water away from the river).","Boil the water for cooking and washing up.","Wash up more than 50 m from water, and strain the washing-up water before scattering it.","Take all leftovers away: they attract animals."]},{"titre":"Campfire recipes inspired by video games","p":["Skewers, stews, honey apples: many adventure games have their heroes cook on a campfire. These recipes are real-life versions, doable on a bivouac with a mess tin, a green branch and good embers. Each sheet lists the “loot” to bring, the cooking and a small tongue-in-cheek effect."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Unofficial","texte":"Original recipes written for Bivaska. The games mentioned are inspirations: Bivaska is neither affiliated with nor endorsed by their publishers."}}]},"orientation_guide":{"titre":"Finding your way","resume":"Map and compass, natural methods, and what to do when you're lost.","sections":[{"titre":"Map basics","liste":["North is at the top of the map.","Scale: on a 1:25,000 map, 1 cm represents 250 m. It's the ideal scale for hiking.","Contour lines join points of the same altitude: close together, the slope is steep; far apart, the ground is flat.","Orient the map: turn it so that what you see in front of you is in front of you on the map."]},{"titre":"Using a compass","etapes":["Put the compass on the map, its edge joining your position to your destination.","Turn the dial until the dial's north-south lines are parallel to the map's north-south lines, arrow pointing north.","Hold the compass flat in front of you and turn on the spot until the red needle sits inside the dial's arrow.","The direction-of-travel arrow now shows the way. Aim at a distant landmark in that direction (tree, rock), walk to it, and repeat."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"In Western Europe, the difference between magnetic north and true north is now very small: you can ignore it when hiking."}},{"titre":"Natural methods","liste":["The sun rises in the east, is due south around midday and sets in the west.","The stick-and-shadow method gives the east-west line in 15 minutes.","An analogue watch gives south in good weather.","The North Star gives north at night.","Moss growing “on the north side” of trees is NOT reliable: it grows mainly on the damp side, which varies with the terrain."]},{"titre":"Navigating precisely","liste":["Count your steps: most adults take 60 to 70 double steps per 100 m. Calibrate yourself before you go.","Cross-bearing: two compass bearings on two known landmarks give your exact position on the map.","Handrail: follow a linear feature (path, river, forest edge) rather than cutting through the woods.","Aiming off: deliberately aim to one side of the target so you know which way to turn when you arrive.","Catching feature: a feature beyond your goal that tells you you've gone too far."]},{"titre":"GPS: strengths and limits","liste":["The phone's GPS position works without a network: only the map must have been loaded beforehand (in Bivaska, by browsing the area).","Accuracy drops under dense forest cover, at the bottom of gorges and near cliffs: you can be off by several dozen metres.","Cold and GPS drain the battery quickly: keep the phone warm, in aeroplane mode, and carry a power bank.","GPS tells you where you are, not which way to go: a map, a compass and the habit of reading the terrain remain essential."]},{"titre":"When you're lost","etapes":["Stop (STOP method): walking on at random almost always makes things worse.","Look at your GPS position in Bivaska: it works without a network, and the map stays visible if the area was displayed before.","Go back to the last place you knew for certain, if it's possible and close.","Otherwise, look for a handrail: a path, a river to follow downstream, a power line, a ridge.","If night is coming, stop, build a shelter and signal: you'll set off again in daylight."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"If someone knows where you are, staying put is almost always safer than wandering."}}]},"deplacement":{"titre":"Moving safely","resume":"Walking pace, difficult terrain, walking at night and crossing streams.","sections":[{"titre":"The right pace","liste":["Walk at a pace where you can still talk: if you're out of breath, you sweat and wear yourself out.","A short break every hour to drink, eat a little and check your feet.","In a group, the slowest walks in front and sets the pace.","Count about 4 km an hour on a flat path, much less in forest, uphill or with a heavy pack. Add an hour for every 300 to 400 m of climb."]},{"titre":"Difficult terrain","liste":["Downhill: small steps, knees slightly bent, poles in front. Most sprains happen going down, when tired.","Slippery ground (roots, wet rocks, leaves): put your foot down flat, never on a slanting root.","Dense vegetation: go round rather than force through, protect your eyes from branches, keep one hand free.","Scree and steep slopes: one at a time, never one person above another."]},{"titre":"Walking at night","p":["At night, speed halves and the risk of a fall or a navigation error soars. Only walk at night on a known path, head torch on, with spare batteries. Otherwise, the safest thing is to stop, build a shelter and set off again at daybreak."]},{"titre":"Crossing a stream","p":["This is one of the most dangerous situations when hiking. A current reaching the knees is enough to knock an adult over."],"etapes":["First ask yourself whether you can avoid it: a bridge further on, a detour, waiting for the level to drop (in the morning, after a storm).","Choose the spot: wide, shallow, split into several channels, without slippery rocks, upstream of a sandbank.","Keep your shoes on, undo the pack's straps.","Cross facing the current, leaning on a stick on the upstream side, angling downstream.","In a group, stand shoulder to shoulder, the strongest upstream."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"Never above a waterfall or rapids, never in very cold water or a flood. If you fall: on your back, feet first at the surface, swim diagonally towards the bank."}}]},"meteo_dangers":{"titre":"Weather and natural hazards","resume":"Thunderstorms, cold, heat, fog, floods and wind: anticipate and react.","sections":[{"titre":"Thunderstorms","p":["Lightning is one of the great dangers of mountains and wide open spaces. Count the seconds between the flash and the thunder: under 30 seconds, the storm is less than 10 km away and you should already be under shelter. Then wait 30 minutes after the last thunderclap before setting off again."],"liste":["Leave summits, ridges, isolated trees, cable lines and water (lake, river).","In a forest: stand among trees of the same height, in a hollow, away from the tallest trees.","Move away from metal objects (poles, pylons, fences).","If caught in the open: crouch, feet together, on your pack or mat, head tucked in, without lying down.","In a group: spread out several metres apart so a single strike doesn't hit everyone."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Warning","texte":"A tent or a shallow cave doesn't protect you from lightning. A closed car does."}},{"titre":"Cold and hypothermia","p":["You can get hypothermia at +10 °C, in rain and wind. The signs progress: shivering, clumsy movements, then confusion and drowsiness. Someone who stops shivering while still cold is in great danger."],"liste":["Shelter the casualty from wind and rain, insulate them from the ground.","Replace wet clothes with dry ones, cover the head.","Give hot, sweet drinks if they are conscious.","Warm gently: skin-to-skin contact in the sleeping bag, wrapped hot-water bottles on the trunk (not the limbs).","Call 112 as soon as there is confusion or drowsiness."]},{"titre":"Heat","liste":["Set off early, take your break during the hot hours, in the shade.","Drink regularly before you feel thirsty, and eat salty food.","Signs of heatstroke: hot skin, headache, nausea, confusion. Move to the shade, cool down with water on the skin and by fanning, call 112 if there is confusion."]},{"titre":"Fog and night","liste":["Fog makes you lose your bearings in minutes: stop, follow a known path or the compass, stay together.","At night, move slowly, head torch on, and don't leave the paths. Better to bivouac than to walk in the dark on unknown ground."]},{"titre":"Floods and wind","liste":["Never camp in a riverbed, on a gravel bar or right by the water: the level can rise very quickly after a storm upstream.","Never cross a river in flood or whose water is above the knees.","In strong wind (gusts over 60 km/h, alerts in the Bivouac tab): avoid the forest and never sleep under trees."]}]},"couteau":{"titre":"Knife, tools and knots","resume":"Using a knife safely, carving a feather stick, sawing, and the essential knots.","sections":[{"titre":"Choosing your knife","p":["A good bivouac knife has a fixed or securely locking blade of 8 to 10 cm, strong and easy to sharpen. A multi-tool with a small saw complements it very well. In Belgium as elsewhere, a knife is carried stowed away (in the pack or its sheath) and only for a justified use such as bivouacking."]},{"titre":"Safety rules","liste":["Always cut with the blade moving away from you.","The “blood circle”: before cutting, stretch out your arm holding the closed knife and turn around. Nobody should be inside that circle.","Work sitting down, elbows on knees, blade outside your thighs (the femoral artery runs on the inside).","Never cut towards the hand holding the wood.","Close or put away the knife as soon as you stop using it.","A sharp knife is safer than a blunt one, which slips and makes you force it."]},{"titre":"The feather stick","p":["The king of techniques for lighting a fire in wet weather: you turn a dead stick into a tuft of dry curls that catch easily."],"etapes":["Choose a dead stick as thick as a thumb, and split it to reach the dry heartwood.","Rest the end of the stick on a stump or log, angled at about 45°.","Lay the blade flat against the wood and push it down in long, very thin curls.","Stop each curl just before the base so it stays attached.","Make 10 to 20 curls, turning the stick: you get a “feather” ready to take a spark."]},{"titre":"Sawing and splitting","liste":["A folding saw is safer and more effective than an axe for bivouac wood.","To split a log with a knife (“batoning”): place the blade on the end of the upright log and strike the back of the blade with another stick. Only with a strong fixed-blade knife.","Never cut living wood: it doesn't burn and it is forbidden."]},{"titre":"Making your own cord","p":["Without rope, nature provides: long nettle stems and the inner bark of the lime tree give strong fibres. They are combined by “reverse twisting”: two strands each twisted towards you then crossed away from you. The result holds surprisingly well for tying, lashing or hanging."]},{"titre":"The essential knots","p":["Five knots cover almost every bivouac need. Their detailed sheets, with photos, are in the Knots category."],"liste":["The bowline: a fixed loop that doesn't slip.","Round turn and two half hitches: tying a rope to a tree.","The trucker's hitch: tensioning a ridgeline very tightly.","The taut-line hitch: adjusting the tension of the tarp's guylines.","The Prusik: an attachment point that slides on a taut rope."]}]},"hygiene":{"titre":"Hygiene and health","resume":"Looking after your feet, hands and health during several days outdoors.","sections":[{"titre":"Feet first","p":["Damaged feet turn a trip into an ordeal, and in a survival situation they can stop you reaching help."],"liste":["Keep your feet dry: change socks as soon as they're wet, dry the damp pair on you or in the sun.","Treat hot spots BEFORE they become blisters.","In the evening, wash, dry and air your feet, and check for blisters and ticks.","Cut your toenails short before you go.","Feet that are wet and cold for hours, several days in a row, can develop “immersion foot” (white, swollen, numb and then painful feet). Dry and warm them every evening, and see a doctor if feeling doesn't come back."]},{"titre":"Hands, teeth and washing","liste":["Wash your hands after the toilet and before eating: most bivouac diarrhoea comes from hands, not water.","Brush your teeth with boiled or filtered water, spit far from water sources.","Wash with a flannel or biodegradable wipe, soap away from the river.","Keep a clean, dry outfit for the night."]},{"titre":"Toilets in the wild","etapes":["Go more than 50 m from water, camp and paths.","Dig a hole 15 to 20 cm deep with a small trowel or a stick.","Fill it in carefully and put leaves or moss back on top.","Take the toilet paper away in a sealed bag: it takes months to disappear."]},{"titre":"Small enemies","liste":["Ticks: full check every evening (armpits, groin, behind the knees, scalp), removal with a tick remover.","Mosquitoes and horseflies: long clothing at dusk, repellent.","Processionary caterpillars: don't camp under infested trees.","Nettle, giant hogweed: know the plants that sting or burn."]},{"titre":"The first-aid kit","liste":["Plasters of various sizes, blister plasters, dressings, bandage, tape","Disinfectant, hand gel","Tick remover, tweezers, small scissors","Painkillers, personal medicines","Survival blanket, disposable gloves","Emergency numbers (in Bivaska: SOS button and Poisoning sheet)"]}]},"secours_guide":{"titre":"Signalling and getting rescued","resume":"Calling for help, giving your position, making yourself visible and guiding a helicopter.","sections":[{"titre":"Calling","etapes":["Dial 112: it works throughout Europe, and goes through any available mobile network.","Say who you are, what is happening, how many people are involved and their condition.","Give your GPS position (Bivaska's SOS button: coordinates to read out or copy).","Don't hang up first, and keep the phone switched on afterwards."],"encadre":{"type":"astuce","titre":"Tip","texte":"If the call doesn't go through, an SMS sometimes gets through where voice fails. Climbing higher also improves signal."}},{"titre":"Saving the battery","liste":["Aeroplane mode between attempts, brightness at minimum.","Power bank kept warm in an inside pocket (cold drains batteries).","Agree a switch-on time with the rescuers if you're in contact."]},{"titre":"Making yourself visible","liste":["Anything in threes means distress: 3 whistle blasts, 3 flashes of light, 3 fires in a triangle.","On the ground, in a clearing: a large V (need help) or X (need medical assistance), at least 4 × 6 m, with strokes 1 m wide, in branches, stones or high-contrast clothing.","A brightly coloured garment or survival blanket, spread out in an open place.","A mirror or the phone screen to flash the sun at a plane or helicopter.","By day, white smoke (green leaves on the fire); at night, a bright flame or the head torch."]},{"titre":"When a helicopter arrives","liste":["Signal with both arms raised in a Y towards the sky: “yes, need help”. One arm raised, the other lowered: “no, no need”.","Tie down or put away anything that could blow away (tarp, bags, survival blanket).","Never approach the aircraft without a sign from the crew, and always from the front, in the pilot's field of view."]}]},"loi":{"titre":"Law and ethics, country by country","resume":"Where are you allowed to sleep, light a fire, forage? The rules in Belgium, France, Switzerland, Germany, the Netherlands and Luxembourg.","sections":[{"titre":"The main principles","p":["In all these countries, the basic rule is the same: you can't camp just anywhere. Bivouacking (a single night, evening to morning, without setting up camp) is sometimes tolerated where camping is forbidden. Nature reserves, national parks and private land always have their own rules. This information is a summary: always check the local rules before you go."]},{"titre":"Belgium","liste":["Wallonia: camping in the forest is forbidden, except in designated bivouac areas (shown on Bivaska's map when they are listed) or on private land with the owner's permission.","Flanders: only in the designated bivouac zones.","Fire: forbidden in forests, except at designated spots.","Foraging: generally tolerated in small quantities for personal use, unless locally banned; forbidden in nature reserves; owner's permission on private land."]},{"titre":"France","liste":["Bivouacking is allowed where it isn't forbidden. Frequent bans: listed and protected sites, the seashore, around drinking-water catchments, public roads, private land without permission.","National parks: each has its own rules. Some allow bivouacking at set times (often 7 pm to 9 am) or near mountain huts, others ban it completely (Port-Cros, Calanques).","Fire: forbidden in woods and within 200 m of them for anyone who isn't the owner, with stricter bans in summer."]},{"titre":"Switzerland","liste":["Wild camping isn't banned by federal law, but cantons and municipalities set the rules, which are often restrictive.","Above the tree line, one night in a small tent, not in a group, is generally tolerated.","Forbidden in nature reserves, the Swiss National Park, federal game reserves and wildlife quiet zones."]},{"titre":"Germany","liste":["No single rule: each Land decides. Camping in the forest almost everywhere requires the permission of the owner or the forestry authority.","Sleeping under the stars for one night, without a tent (“Biwak”), is often tolerated outside protected areas.","Some regions offer official bivouac pitches (Trekkingplätze), to be booked."]},{"titre":"Netherlands and Luxembourg","liste":["Netherlands: wild camping is forbidden. You sleep at “paalkampeerplaatsen”, free and basic bivouac pitches in the forest.","Luxembourg: considered generally forbidden outside campsites; on private land, the owner's permission is required."]},{"titre":"Leave no trace","liste":["Take all your rubbish away, including food waste.","Stay discreet: arrive late, leave early, no music, small groups.","Respect wildlife: don't feed, don't approach, dogs on a lead.","Forage in moderation, never protected species.","Leave the place cleaner than you found it."],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"Every badly kept bivouac gets one more place closed. Bivouacking stays tolerated as long as it stays invisible."}}]},"secourisme":{"titre":"First aid, alone and far from everything","resume":"When help won't come quickly, or at all: stabilise, protect from the cold, monitor, then decide whether to stay or go.","sections":[{"titre":"A different logic","p":["In town, first aid is about holding on for a few minutes. Far from everything, without signal, you may have to look after a casualty, or yourself, for hours or days. The goal is no longer to wait for the ambulance: it is to stabilise, prevent things getting worse, and choose the right moment to get out.","Even without signal, still try to raise the alarm: climb to a high point, send an SMS to a loved one with your position, prepare distress signals (Signals sheet)."]},{"titre":"Priorities, in order","etapes":["Danger: remove whatever still threatens (falling rocks, water, fire), without putting yourself in danger.","Heavy bleeding: stop it immediately (pressure, tourniquet if needed).","Breathing: open the airway, recovery position if the person is unconscious and breathing.","Cold: insulate from the ground, cover, shelter. A casualty who isn't moving cools down very quickly, even in summer.","Everything else next: fractures, wounds, pain, drinking."]},{"titre":"Stay or go?","liste":["Stay and signal if: the casualty can't walk or can't be left alone, night or bad weather is coming, the way is unknown, or someone knows where you are and is expecting you.","Go if: nobody will look for you, the casualty can walk safely or can be left stable and sheltered, and you know the way to somewhere you can call from.","A casualty who can still walk should move slowly, with a lightened pack, by the safest route, not the shortest."]},{"titre":"Signs that mean getting out fast","liste":["Consciousness drops or the person becomes confused.","They have trouble breathing.","Bleeding won't stop, or a tourniquet has been applied.","Pallor, cold sweats, fast pulse: shock.","Increasing belly pain, repeated vomiting.","A wound becomes infected (spreading redness, fever).","Hypothermia: shivering that stops, clumsy movements, incoherent speech."],"encadre":{"type":"danger","titre":"Just one of these signs is enough","texte":"As soon as one of these signs appears, waiting becomes more dangerous than the effort: do everything to evacuate or be found."}},{"titre":"Take notes to decide","p":["Every hour, note the casualty's condition: consciousness, breathing, pulse, skin, pain. A change shows in the notes long before you can see it. The sheet Monitoring a casualty explains how."]},{"titre":"The kit that changes everything","liste":["A tourniquet and dressings, a compression bandage","Strips (adhesive sutures) and plasters","Fine tweezers and a tick remover","Something to rinse a wound (syringe or bottle with a pierced cap)","A survival blanket and gloves","Painkillers and antihistamines, your adrenaline pen if you are allergic","A notebook and a pencil"],"encadre":{"type":"retenir","titre":"Remember","texte":"First-aid training, and if possible a “remote environment” module, is worth more than the best kit."}}]}},"techs":{"priorites":{"titre":"The rule of 3","intro":"A simple guide to what to do first when things go wrong.","etapes":["3 minutes without air: clearing the airway and stopping a haemorrhage are the absolute priority.","3 hours without shelter in cold, wet weather or great heat: protect yourself before looking for food.","3 days without water: finding water and making it safe comes next.","3 weeks without food: on a bivouac of a few days, food is never the emergency.","Keep a cool head: stop, think, observe, plan (the “STOP” of survival manuals)."],"attention":["These durations are orders of magnitude, not limits to test."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"eau":{"titre":"Making water safe to drink","intro":"Even when clear, river water can contain bacteria and parasites (farms and villages upstream). Boiling is the only truly safe improvised method.","etapes":["Pre-filter through a clean cloth to remove particles.","Bring to a rolling boil for 1 minute (3 minutes above 2,000 m).","Let it cool covered, in a clean container.","Without a fire: a certified hiking filter or purification tablets, respecting the stated waiting time.","Draw water upstream of the camp, away from pastures and stagnant water."],"attention":["Boiling doesn't remove chemical pollutants: avoid water near treated fields, roads or industrial sites."],"source":"WHO; review “Improvised Water Procurement in Wilderness Medicine” (medRxiv, 2025)"},"filtre":{"titre":"Improvised filter (sand and charcoal)","intro":"Clears cloudy water before boiling it. It does NOT make it drinkable on its own.","etapes":["Cut off the bottom of a plastic bottle and turn it upside down, cap slightly unscrewed or pierced.","Stack from bottom to top: crushed charcoal (from last night's fire, without ash), fine sand, gravel, then cloth or grass.","Pour the water slowly in from the top and throw away the first runs, which are often still cloudy.","Boil the filtered water for 1 minute before drinking it."],"attention":["No study shows that a home-made filter removes germs: always boil afterwards."],"source":"WHO; review “Improvised Water Procurement in Wilderness Medicine” (medRxiv, 2025)"},"pluie":{"titre":"Collecting rainwater","intro":"The best improvised source: a few litres per shower for very little effort.","etapes":["Stretch a tarp, poncho or survival blanket sloping towards a low point.","Put a clean container under that low point (bottle, pot, dry bag).","Let the first rain rinse the tarp before collecting.","Store it under cover and boil it if the water has stood or touched leaves."],"source":"WHO; review “Improvised Water Procurement in Wilderness Medicine” (medRxiv, 2025)"},"transpiration":{"titre":"Transpiration bag","intro":"A clear plastic bag over a leafy branch collects the water the plant gives off. Useful in sun, weak in November.","etapes":["Choose a leafy branch of a NON-poisonous tree or shrub, well exposed to the sun.","Slip a clear plastic bag over it and close it tightly around the branch.","Let one corner of the bag hang down, with a small pebble inside to create a low point.","Collect at the end of the day by piercing the corner or opening the bag, then change branch."],"attention":["Yield from a few decilitres to 1 litre a day, and only in fine weather.","Never on yew, foxglove, bittersweet or an unidentified plant."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain); WHO; review “Improvised Water Procurement in Wilderness Medicine” (medRxiv, 2025)"},"rosee":{"titre":"Collecting dew","intro":"An extra top-up, early in the morning, when the grass is soaked.","etapes":["Just before sunrise, tie an absorbent cloth (T-shirt, rag) around your ankles or hold it in your hand.","Walk through or drag it in tall wet grass.","Wring it out regularly into a container.","Boil before drinking."],"attention":["Very low yield (about a glass): never count on it as a main source."],"source":"WHO; review “Improvised Water Procurement in Wilderness Medicine” (medRxiv, 2025)"},"feu":{"titre":"Preparing your fire","intro":"90% of failed fires fail for lack of preparation. Fires in forests are tightly controlled: in Wallonia, forbidden except at designated spots; in France, forbidden in woods and within 200 m of them for anyone who isn't the owner. Check the local rules.","etapes":["Choose an allowed spot, sheltered from the wind, cleared of leaves and away from roots and low branches.","Gather a big handful of tinder: birch bark (burns even when damp), dry grass, dead needles, fine shavings.","Prepare the kindling in graded sizes, from match to thumb thickness.","Plan the fuel wood (wrist thickness), only dead wood picked up from the ground.","Gather twice as much of each category as seems necessary, BEFORE lighting."],"attention":["Never cut living branches: they don't burn and it is forbidden."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"tipi":{"titre":"Teepee fire","intro":"The simplest structure to start with: the flame rises and catches the wood above.","etapes":["Put the tinder in a ball in the centre.","Stand the finest kindling in a cone around it, leaving an opening on the wind side.","Light the tinder through the opening, at ground level.","Blow gently at the base when the flames catch.","Add bigger and bigger wood without crushing the structure."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"cabane":{"titre":"Log cabin fire (crossed logs)","intro":"Burns for a long time and gives a fine bed of embers, ideal for cooking.","etapes":["Start a small teepee in the centre.","Lay two parallel logs on each side, then two more across on top, like a log cabin.","Build 3 to 5 layers, slightly reducing the size towards the top.","Let the fire collapse into embers before putting the pot on."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"ferro":{"titre":"Lighting with ferrocerium","intro":"A fire steel works wet, in severe cold and thousands of times. It belongs in every bivouac pack.","etapes":["Prepare a nest of very fine, dry tinder (grated birch bark, cotton, shavings).","Put the tip of the rod IN the tinder, striker pressed at about 45°.","Pull the rod towards you rather than pushing the striker: you won't scatter the nest.","Repeat firmly until the sparks catch, then blow gently."],"attention":["Sparks exceed 3,000 °C: keep fingers and synthetic clothing away."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"feupluie":{"titre":"Making fire in the rain","intro":"In wet weather, in autumn and winter, everything is wet. You have to look for dry material where it hides.","etapes":["Gather dead twigs still on the tree, at the bottom of spruces: they stay dry under the branches.","Take birch bark from dead trees or from the ground.","Split pieces of dead wood: the heart stays dry even when the outside is soaked.","Build a platform of sticks on the wet ground and light the fire on it.","Shelter the starting fire with your body or a high tarp, never right over the flames."],"attention":["Never light a fire under a tent or a closed tarp: risk of fire and carbon monoxide."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"froid":{"titre":"Preventing hypothermia","intro":"In autumn and winter, nights near 0 °C and damp. Cold and wet together are the real danger of bivouacking.","etapes":["Stay dry: change before you get cold, keep a dry outfit for the night only.","Layering system: breathable base (no cotton), warm layer, waterproof shell.","Insulate from the ground: at rest, you lose a large part of your heat into it. A good mat matters as much as the sleeping bag.","Eat and drink something hot before sleeping.","Warning signs: uncontrollable shivering, clumsy movements, confusion. Warm the person and call 112 if it gets worse."],"attention":["Someone who stops shivering while still cold is in danger: call the emergency services."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"abri":{"titre":"Choosing your spot","intro":"A good spot is half of a good night.","etapes":["Only on an authorised bivouac area or private land with permission (see the Map tab).","Look up: no dead branch or dead tree above you.","Avoid the valley bottom and the very edge of the river: cold air, damp, possible rising water.","Look for a dry, slightly raised ledge, close to dead wood and water.","Turn the shelter's entrance away from the prevailing wind."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"bache":{"titre":"A-frame shelter with a tarp","intro":"The quickest with gear: a 3 × 3 m tarp and 10 m of cord.","etapes":["Stretch a cord between two trees, from hip to shoulder height depending on the weather (lower = warmer).","Drape the tarp over the cord.","Peg the four corners to the ground, sides well tensioned so the rain runs off.","Line the ground with dry leaves or needles under the mat."],"attention":["Tie a little piece of string onto the cord on each side: water drips off it instead of running into the shelter."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"appentis":{"titre":"Branch lean-to","intro":"A sloping roof open on one side, effective with a reflected fire in front (where fires are allowed).","etapes":["Wedge a strong ridgepole between two trees, at chest height.","Lean poles against it, on the wind side, at about 45°, 30 cm apart.","Weave flexible branches across them to form a grid.","Cover with leafy branches, ferns or leaves, from the bottom up like roof tiles.","Insulate the ground with a thick layer of dry leaves."],"attention":["Only with dead wood and on land where it is allowed."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"debris":{"titre":"Debris hut","intro":"The warmest survival shelter without gear: a giant sleeping bag of dead leaves.","etapes":["Find a ridgepole about 1.5 times your height.","Rest one end on a stump or tripod at knee height, the other on the ground.","Lay branches along both sides, close together, to form ribs.","Cover with small branches then a layer of dead leaves 60 cm to 1 m thick (an arm's length).","Fill the inside with dry leaves, go in feet first and block the entrance with a pile of leaves."],"attention":["The inside must be just big enough for you: the smaller, the warmer."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"orientation":{"titre":"Finding your way without signal","intro":"In valleys or forests, mobile signal is often patchy.","etapes":["Before leaving: study the route and display it in the Map tab to keep it cached.","Following a river downstream leads sooner or later to a village or a road.","The sun is due south around the middle of the day: between 12 and 1 pm in winter time, between 1 and 2 pm in summer time (Western Europe).","With an analogue watch: point the hour hand at the sun. South is halfway between it and 12 (1 in summer time)."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"baton":{"titre":"Stick-and-shadow method","intro":"Find east and west with a simple stick, as soon as there is a little sun.","etapes":["Plant a straight stick in flat ground.","Mark the tip of its shadow with a pebble: that is WEST.","Wait 15 minutes and mark the new tip of the shadow: that is EAST.","The line between the two marks runs west to east. A perpendicular gives north and south."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"polaire":{"titre":"Finding north at night","intro":"The North Star shows north to within about 1°, all year round in the northern hemisphere.","etapes":["Find the Plough (Big Dipper), the “saucepan” of 7 bright stars.","Take the two stars on the edge of the pan, opposite the handle.","Extend their line by about 5 times the gap between them.","The fairly bright, isolated star you reach is the North Star: it shows north."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"urgence":{"titre":"Emergency and rescue","intro":"112 goes through any available mobile network, even if it isn't your operator's.","etapes":["Call 112 and give your GPS coordinates (crisis button in the middle of the app).","If the call doesn't go through, send an SMS to a loved one with your position, then climb higher to look for signal.","Stay put if someone knows where you are: a person who stays still is found faster.","Always tell someone your route and return time before you leave."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"signaux":{"titre":"Distress signals","intro":"Anything repeated three times means “distress”, all over the world.","etapes":["Whistle: 3 long blasts, a pause, and repeat. It carries much further than the voice and is less tiring.","Light: 3 repeated flashes, or SOS (3 short, 3 long, 3 short).","Fires: 3 fires in a triangle about thirty metres apart, in a clearing visible from the sky (if the situation requires).","On the ground, symbols at least 4 × 6 m in branches or stones: V = need help, X = need medical assistance (see the Ground-to-air codes sheet).","Wear a bright colour and spread it out in an open place."],"attention":["In the mountains, the Alpine distress signal is 6 signals a minute, then a minute's pause."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"piege":{"titre":"Survival traps: what you need to know","intro":"Military manuals describe snares and deadfalls (like the “figure 4” trap). In Belgium as in France, setting them is poaching, except in a real life-threatening emergency.","etapes":["On a bivouac of a few days, food is never the priority: bring your own.","In a real emergency, favour what takes little energy: foraging for plants identified with certainty (Species tab).","Traps only work if you set many of them on active runs (paths, tracks, droppings) and check them often.","A forgotten trap keeps killing: in a real situation, dismantle them all before leaving."],"attention":["Forbidden outside a life-threatening emergency: fines and prosecution for poaching.","Fishing requires a licence (fishing licence in Wallonia, fishing card in France…)."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"traces":{"titre":"Leave no trace","intro":"This is what allows bivouac areas to stay open.","etapes":["Take all your rubbish away, including food waste and toilet paper.","Toilet: more than 50 m from water, in a 15 cm hole filled back in.","No soap in the river, even biodegradable.","Arrive late, leave early, stay discreet, leave the place cleaner than you found it."],"source":"“Leave No Trace” principles"},"chaise":{"titre":"Bowline","intro":"THE fixed loop: it doesn't slip and is easy to untie after loading. To tie a rope to a tree or make a rescue loop around the waist.","etapes":["Make a small loop in the standing part (the “rabbit hole”), the working end passing over the top.","Pass the working end up through the small loop from underneath: “the rabbit comes out of the hole”.","Pass it behind the standing part: “it goes round the tree”.","Bring it back down through the small loop: “it goes back down the hole”.","Tighten by holding the working end and the big loop, then pull the standing part. Leave a tail of at least 10 cm."],"attention":["It can loosen when not loaded: secure the tail with a half hitch."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"tendeur":{"titre":"Taut-line hitch","intro":"The guyline knot: it slides to adjust tension and locks under load. Ideal for tensioning a tarp or tent.","etapes":["Pass the cord around the peg or tree.","With the working end, make two turns around the standing part, working towards the peg (inside the loop).","Make a third turn around the standing part, this time outside the first two.","Tighten: slide the knot to tension or slacken."],"attention":["On very slippery cord (Dyneema), add one more turn on the inside."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"cabestan":{"titre":"Clove hitch","intro":"Quick for fixing a rope to a pole or peg, and the starting point of all lashings.","etapes":["Make a full turn around the pole.","Cross over the standing part and make a second turn beside the first.","Tuck the working end under this second turn, between the pole and the rope.","Tighten by pulling the two ends in opposite directions."],"attention":["It can slip if the support turns: add a half hitch for safety."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"tourmort":{"titre":"Round turn and two half hitches","intro":"A strong mooring of a rope to a tree, easy to untie even after a heavy load. Perfect for a ridgeline.","etapes":["Make two full turns around the tree (the “round turn” takes the strain).","Tie a half hitch around the standing part.","Tie a second half hitch in the same direction as the first.","Push the hitches up against the round turn and tighten."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"camionneur":{"titre":"Trucker's hitch","intro":"An improvised pulley that doubles the force: to tension a ridgeline like a guitar string.","etapes":["Tie one end to the first tree (round turn and two half hitches).","Towards the second tree, form a fixed loop in the rope (figure-eight loop or a locked slip loop): that is the “pulley”.","Pass the working end around the second tree, come back and thread it through the loop.","Pull the working end towards the tree: the tension increases sharply.","Lock it with two half hitches just below the loop."],"attention":["Don't over-tighten on a weak branch or a dead tree."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"huit":{"titre":"Figure-eight knot","intro":"The standard stopper knot at the end of a rope, and, tied in a doubled rope, a very safe fixed loop (figure-eight loop).","etapes":["Make a loop with the working end.","Pass the working end around the standing part.","Tuck it through the loop from above: the rope forms an “8”.","Tighten. For a loop: double the rope and tie exactly the same knot with the doubled part."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"plat":{"titre":"Reef knot","intro":"For tying the two ends of a bandage, a bag or a bundle. Not for joining two ropes that bear a load.","etapes":["Cross the left end over the right and tuck it under (first overhand).","Cross the right end over the left and tuck it under (second overhand).","Check that each end comes out on the same side as its own rope: the knot is flat and symmetrical.","Tighten."],"attention":["If the ends come out at an angle, it's a “granny knot” that slips: tie it again.","Never for climbing or rescue."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"ecoute":{"titre":"Sheet bend","intro":"Joins two ropes, even of different diameters. For lengthening a rope that is too short.","etapes":["Form a U (a bight) with the end of the thicker rope.","Pass the end of the thinner rope up through the U from underneath.","Take it round behind both strands of the U.","Tuck it under itself, without going through the U.","Tighten: both free ends must come out on the same side."],"attention":["For extra security, make two turns instead of one: double sheet bend."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"pecheur":{"titre":"Double fisherman's knot","intro":"Joins two ropes securely, or closes a cord into a loop (for a Prusik). Doesn't come undone by itself.","etapes":["Lay the two ends side by side, pointing in opposite directions.","With the first end, make two turns around both ropes working back towards you, then pass it through these two turns.","Do the same with the second end, in the other direction.","Pull on the two ropes: the two knots slide together and lock against each other."],"attention":["Very hard to untie after a heavy load."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"prusik":{"titre":"Prusik knot","intro":"A loop of cord that slides by hand along a rope and locks under load. For tensioning a tarp on a ridgeline or climbing up a rope.","etapes":["Take a cord thinner than the main rope, closed into a loop (double fisherman's knot).","Pass the loop behind the main rope.","Pass the loop through itself around the rope, three times.","Dress the turns neatly parallel and tighten: push the knot to move it, pull the loop to lock it."],"attention":["The thinner the cord compared with the rope, the harder it grips."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"bois":{"titre":"Timber hitch","intro":"For dragging a log or carrying a bundle of wood. Holds by tension and comes undone in one move.","etapes":["Pass the rope around the log or bundle.","Make a half hitch around the standing part.","Wrap the working end three times around itself, along the loop.","Pull to tighten. To drag a long log, add a half hitch further towards the end."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"constricteur":{"titre":"Constrictor knot","intro":"The tightest-gripping knot: closing a bag, binding a bundle, making an emergency whipping on a pole.","etapes":["Make one turn around the object.","Cross over and make a second turn, like a clove hitch.","Tuck the working end under the X crossing, so it comes out between the two strands.","Pull both ends hard to tighten."],"attention":["Almost impossible to untie once tight: plan to cut it."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"brelagecarre":{"titre":"Square lashing","intro":"Joins two poles crosswise for a shelter frame, a table or a drying rack.","etapes":["Cross the two poles at right angles.","Tie a clove hitch on the upright pole, just below the crosspiece.","Make 3 to 4 turns: over the crosspiece, behind the upright, over the crosspiece on the other side, behind the upright.","Make 2 to 3 frapping turns between the two poles, around the previous turns.","Finish with a clove hitch on the crosspiece."],"attention":["Only with dead wood, and dismantle it before leaving."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"brelagetrepied":{"titre":"Shear or tripod lashing","intro":"Joins two or three poles to make a tripod (hanging a pot, raising a ridgepole).","etapes":["Lay the poles side by side, ends aligned.","Tie a clove hitch on an outer pole.","Make 8 to 10 loose turns around all the poles.","Make 2 frapping turns between each pole.","Finish with a clove hitch, then spread the legs: the turns tighten by themselves."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska; US Army survival manual FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain)"},"ps_saignement":{"titre":"Bleeding wound","intro":"Bleeding can almost always be controlled by firm direct pressure.","etapes":["Protect your hands if possible (gloves, plastic bag).","Press hard and without letting go directly on the wound with a clean cloth, for at least 10 minutes.","If the cloth fills with blood, don't remove it: add another on top and keep pressing.","Lay the casualty down, cover them, call 112 if the bleeding doesn't stop or is heavy.","Small wound: rinse with drinking water, disinfect, cover with a plaster."],"attention":["A tourniquet is only a last resort, on a limb, when direct pressure isn't enough, noting the time it was applied."],"isole":["Press hard and without letting go for at least 10 minutes, timed: continuous pressure is what stops the bleeding.","If it starts again: press again for 20 minutes, fingers right on the bleeding spot, through a clean cloth.","Once it has stopped: make a pressure dressing (folded cloth on the wound, bandage or scarf tied firmly on top) and check that the fingers or toes stay pink and warm.","A limb that spurts blood or won't stop despite pressure: apply a tourniquet (Improvised tourniquet sheet).","Once the bleeding is under control: clean the wound with plenty of drinking water (Treating a wound sheet) and check it every day.","Give fluids, keep warm and rested: blood loss weakens and chills you."],"alerte":["Blood spurts or soaks everything within minutes","Wound to the neck, chest or belly","Pallor, cold sweats, fast pulse: shock"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_ampoule":{"titre":"Blisters on the feet","intro":"The hiker's number one enemy. Better to treat the hot spot before it blisters.","etapes":["As soon as an area gets hot, stop and protect it (second-skin plaster, tape).","Closed blister: don't pop it, protect it with a special plaster or a foam ring around it.","Open blister: clean it, don't tear off the skin, cover with a clean dressing.","Change wet socks: damp encourages blisters."],"attention":["Spreading redness, pus or fever: sign of infection, see a doctor."],"isole":["Stop as soon as an area gets hot and protect it before it opens.","A very tight blister that stops you walking: pierce it at the edge with a disinfected needle (flame then alcohol), press gently, keep the skin in place.","Cover with a dressing and pad around it (foam, folded cloth) so the shoe no longer presses on it.","At every break, dry and air your feet. At night, sleep in dry socks.","Redo the dressing every day, rinsing with drinking water."],"alerte":["Spreading redness or a red streak up the leg","Pus, heat, fever"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_entorse":{"titre":"Sprained ankle","intro":"A misstep on a wet path happens quickly. What matters: knowing whether you can still walk.","etapes":["Stop and rest the ankle.","Cool it for 20 minutes with cold water or a cold pack wrapped in cloth (never directly on the skin).","Raise the leg.","If you can't take 4 steps, or if the pain is sharp on the bone: don't force it, call for help."],"attention":["Don't remove the shoe if the foot swells a lot and you still have to walk to an access point."],"isole":["Cool it for 20 minutes in the cold water of a stream or with a wet cloth, several times a day, and raise the leg when resting.","To set off again: keep the shoe on, lace it tight, or make a figure-eight bandage around the ankle with a bandage or scarf.","Make a walking stick from a strong branch at hip height, held on the side opposite the injured ankle.","Lighten the pack, shorten the stages and take the flattest path, even if it is longer.","Can't put the foot down: immobilise it like a fracture (Splint sheet), settle somewhere sheltered and signal rather than forcing it."],"alerte":["Unable to take 4 steps","Deformity or bone very painful to touch","Foot cold, pale or numb"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_brulure":{"titre":"Burns","intro":"Vital reflex: cool for a long time with lukewarm water.","etapes":["Cool immediately under lukewarm water for 15 to 20 minutes (30 minutes for a chemical).","Remove rings and clothing that are not stuck to the skin.","Don't burst blisters, don't apply anything (no butter, toothpaste or ice).","Cover with a clean cloth or cling film.","Call 112 if the burn is bigger than the casualty's palm, or affects the face, hands, joints or genitals."],"attention":["Clothes on fire: stop the person from running, make them roll on the ground or smother the flames with a blanket."],"isole":["Cool for 20 minutes with water from the stream or the bottle. It is still useful if you couldn't do it straight away.","Cool the burn, not the person: keep the rest of the body covered and warm.","Don't burst blisters. Cover with a clean, dry dressing or cling film.","Redo the dressing once a day, rinsing gently with drinking water.","Give plenty to drink: an extensive burn makes the body lose water."],"alerte":["Burn bigger than 10 of the casualty's palms: risk of shock","Face, neck, hoarse voice, cough, soot in the nose: the airway may swell","White, leathery or painless burn: deep burn"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_intox":{"titre":"Poisoning (plant, mushroom)","intro":"Poison centres answer 24/7: Belgium 070 245 245, Luxembourg 8002 5500, Switzerland 145, France: your regional poison centre or 15. Elsewhere: 112.","etapes":["Call the poison centre (numbers above), or 112 if the person has trouble breathing, loses consciousness or has seizures.","Keep the leftovers: the plant, raw or cooked mushrooms, even peelings, for identification.","Note the time of the meal, the amount and the time of the first symptoms.","Don't make them vomit and don't give anything to drink without medical advice."],"attention":["Mushrooms: symptoms that appear 6 hours or more after the meal often signal serious poisoning (liver, kidneys), even if the person feels better afterwards."],"isole":["Stop eating, keep all the leftovers (even peelings) and note the time of the meal.","Don't make them vomit.","If the person is conscious and not vomiting: small regular sips of water to make up for losses.","Note the time of each symptom (nausea, diarrhoea, vision problems, sweating, confusion).","Unconscious and breathing: recovery position, and watch them constantly.","Mushrooms and first symptoms 6 hours or more after the meal: getting out or getting found becomes the absolute priority, even if the person seems better."],"alerte":["Drowsiness, confusion, seizures","Symptoms appearing 6 h or more after a mushroom meal","Skin or eyes turning yellow"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_morsure":{"titre":"Snake bite","intro":"Adder bites are very rare in Belgium, but the right actions are simple.","etapes":["Stay calm and lie down: agitation speeds up the spread of venom.","Immobilise the bitten limb, remove rings, watch and bracelets.","Disinfect the bite.","Call 112."],"attention":["Never suck, aspirate or cut the bite, and never apply a tourniquet."],"isole":["Stay calm, sit or lie down: most adder bites in Europe are not serious, but you must limit effort.","Immediately remove rings, watch, bracelet, and the shoe if it's the foot, before it swells.","Immobilise the limb with a splint (Splint sheet), without tightening.","Every hour, mark the edge of the swelling with a pen and the time: you'll see whether it's spreading.","Drink water, don't eat, don't drink alcohol.","Don't walk if you can avoid it: signal for help. If you must leave alone, walk slowly and take breaks."],"alerte":["Swelling goes past the knee or elbow","Vomiting, feeling faint, sweating, swelling of the face","Bleeding from the gums or nose"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_piqure":{"titre":"Wasp, bee or hornet sting","intro":"Painful but harmless… unless there's an allergy or a sting in the mouth.","etapes":["Bee: remove the sting by scraping it with a fingernail or a card, without squeezing the venom sac.","Cool with cold water to limit swelling.","Watch the person for 30 minutes.","Call 112 if the face or throat swells, if there is difficulty breathing, feeling faint or multiple stings. If the person has an adrenaline pen, help them use it."],"attention":["Sting in the mouth: suck ice or cold water and call 112 if it swells."],"isole":["Bee: scrape out the sting with a fingernail or card. Cool with cold water.","Watch the person for at least an hour.","Itchy local swelling: antihistamine from the kit if you have one.","Known allergy with an adrenaline pen: inject into the side of the thigh, even through trousers. If there's no improvement after 5 to 15 minutes and you have a second pen, use it.","Difficulty breathing: keep them sitting, leaning forward. Feeling faint: lying down, legs raised."],"alerte":["Swelling of the face, lips or throat","Wheezing, change in voice","Red blotches all over the body, feeling faint","Sting in the mouth"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_tique":{"titre":"Tick bite","intro":"Check every bivouac evening: armpits, groin, behind the knees, scalp.","etapes":["Remove the tick as early as possible with a tick remover, twisting without pulling.","Don't put anything on it first (no ether, alcohol or oil).","Disinfect and note the date and place of the bite.","Watch the area for a month: a redness spreading in a ring or fever means seeing a doctor."],"isole":["Inspect the whole body every evening and morning: armpits, groin, behind the knees, navel, scalp.","Remove each tick with the tick remover, twisting without pulling. Without one: fine tweezers gripped close to the skin, pulling slowly and straight.","Disinfect and note the date and place in the app's notebook.","Back home, watch the area for a month."],"alerte":["Spreading ring-shaped redness (show a doctor when you get back)","Fever, great tiredness, joint pain in the following weeks"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ULB); Belgian Poison Centre; manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"feu_appentis":{"titre":"Lean-to fire","intro":"Ideal in wind or light rain: an angled stick protects the tinder and guides the flame.","etapes":["Push a green stick into the ground, angled at about 30°, the tip pointing into the wind.","Tuck a good handful of tinder right at the back, under the stick.","Lean kindling against the stick, on both sides, leaving air gaps.","Light the tinder on the wind side, then add kindling as it catches."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"feu_croix":{"titre":"Cross-trench fire","intro":"A small cross-shaped trench under the tinder creates a draught: useful when there is no wind.","etapes":["Mark a cross about 30 cm across in the ground and dig it 7 to 8 cm deep.","Put a big ball of tinder in the centre of the cross.","Build a small pyramid of kindling on top.","Light it: air flows through the trenches and feeds the flame from below."],"attention":["Fill in the trenches when you leave, once the ashes are cold."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"feu_pyramide":{"titre":"Pyramid fire (all night)","intro":"A fire that burns slowly from the top down, without needing attention during the night.","etapes":["Lay two parallel logs on the ground.","Cover them with a tight layer of logs laid across.","Add 3 or 4 layers, each thinner than the last and crossed at right angles.","Light a small starter fire (teepee) at the very top of the pyramid.","The fire burns down layer by layer and gives heat for hours."],"attention":["Even a “self-tending” fire must never be left unattended near a shelter or in a dry forest."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"feu_dakota":{"titre":"Dakota fire hole","intro":"A buried fire, discreet and not very sensitive to wind, that uses little wood and heats a pot well.","etapes":["Dig a hole about 30 cm deep and 20 cm wide, keeping the turf aside.","On the wind side, dig a second hole 20 to 30 cm away, joined to the first by a slanting tunnel: this is the air intake.","Light the fire at the bottom of the main hole with tinder and kindling.","Put the pot directly over the opening, on two green sticks if needed.","When leaving: drown it, let it cool, fill in the holes and put the turf back."],"attention":["Don't do it among roots (they can smoulder) or on peaty ground.","Even buried, the fire is still subject to the local rules."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"reflecteur":{"titre":"Reflector wall","intro":"A wall behind the fire sends the heat back towards you or the shelter, and cuts the wind.","etapes":["Drive in two pairs of stakes about 1 m behind the fire.","Stack logs between the stakes to form a wall 50 to 80 cm high.","Sit between the fire and the shelter: heat reaches you from both sides.","Without logs: a big dry rock or a tarp stretched at a safe distance."],"attention":["Never use wet or porous stones around the fire: the water they contain can make them burst."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"arc_feu":{"titre":"Bow drill","intro":"The most reliable friction method, but it takes practice and perfectly dry wood. Learn it at home, not on the day you need it.","etapes":["Prepare the four parts: a board of soft, dry wood (lime, willow, poplar) about 2.5 cm thick; a straight, dry spindle about 2 × 25 cm, rounded at the top and flat at the bottom; a handhold (hollowed stone or hardwood) to hold the top of the spindle; a bow of flexible wood the length of your arm, with a taut cord.","Carve a small hollow in the board 2 cm from the edge, then cut a V-notch from the edge to the centre of the hollow.","Put the board on a piece of dry bark (which will catch the ember), your foot on the board.","Wrap the bow cord once around the spindle, put the spindle in the hollow, the handhold on top, wrist braced against your leg.","Start with long, slow strokes, then speed up, pressing harder and harder: black dust fills the notch and the smoke thickens.","Keep going a few seconds after the smoke appears, then remove the spindle: the dust should smoke on its own, that's the ember.","Slide it into a nest of very fine tinder, close the nest and blow gently until it flames."],"attention":["Dry dead wood gathered standing, never from the ground (too damp).","Blisters on the hands come quickly: protect your palm with a glove or cloth."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"tissu_charbonne":{"titre":"Char cloth and tinder fungus","intro":"Two materials that catch a single spark and hold the ember for a long time. The cloth is prepared at home; tinder fungus grows in our forests.","etapes":["Char cloth: cut squares of 100% cotton fabric (old T-shirt, jeans) a few centimetres across.","Lay them flat in a small closed metal tin with a small hole pierced in the lid.","Put the tin on a fire or stove: smoke comes out of the hole. Wait until it stops (5 to 10 minutes).","Remove it and let it cool without opening. The squares should be black and supple, not burnt. Keep them dry.","Tinder fungus: this hard, hoof-shaped fungus grows on dead beech and birch. Under the crust is a brown, felty layer.","Remove this felty layer, dry it well then beat it to soften it: it catches a fire steel's spark and holds an ember for many minutes."],"attention":["Only take tinder fungus from dead wood, in small quantities."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"alambic":{"titre":"Solar still","intro":"The sun evaporates moisture from the soil and plants, which condenses under plastic and runs into a container. Low yield: keep it for dry, sunny regions.","etapes":["Dig a hole about 90 cm wide and 60 cm deep, in a sunny, damp spot (valley bottom, wet ground).","Put a container in the centre of the hole, and line the edge with cut green non-poisonous plants.","Cover the hole with a clear plastic sheet, edges buried so it's airtight.","Put a small pebble in the middle of the sheet, just above the container, to form a cone.","Wait: drops run to the tip of the cone and fall into the container. Collect in the evening without lifting the sheet if possible (a tube helps)."],"attention":["Yield of 0.3 to 0.6 litres a day, for a lot of effort: in temperate areas, rain is almost always a better option.","Never poisonous plants in the hole."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"pierres_chaudes":{"titre":"Boiling without a pot","intro":"Stones heated in the fire and dropped into water can boil it in any container that can't go on the fire.","etapes":["Gather dry stones the size of an egg or a fist, away from water (never river pebbles).","Heat them in the heart of the fire for at least 30 minutes.","Prepare the container: plastic bottle or mess tin, wooden bowl, or a hole in the ground lined with a tarp.","Pick up a stone with two sticks used as tongs, shake off the ash, drop it into the water.","Add the stones one by one until there is a rolling boil, and keep it for 1 minute."],"attention":["Damp or porous stones can burst: keep your distance from the fire and protect your eyes.","Then strain the water through a cloth to remove the ash."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"cold":{"titre":"The COLD rule for staying warm","intro":"Four principles from military cold-weather manuals, valid whenever it's cool and damp.","etapes":["C for Clean: dirty, greasy clothes insulate poorly, because dirt crushes the air pockets that hold warmth.","O for avoid Overheating: open your jacket, take off a layer, remove hat or gloves as soon as effort makes you sweat. Sweat wets clothing and chills you as it dries.","L for Loose and Layered: clothes that are too tight cut off circulation. Several thin layers beat one thick one, thanks to the air trapped between them.","D for Dry: protect your clothes from rain and snow, dry them as soon as possible. Damp socks dry against your body or on your pack while you walk."],"attention":["Never dry leather boots too close to the fire: they harden and crack."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"riviere":{"titre":"Crossing a stream","intro":"River crossings are among the most serious hiking accidents. The best decision is often not to cross.","etapes":["Don't cross if the water is fast and above the knees, very cold, murky (you can't see the bottom) or in flood.","Look for the right spot from a high point: where the river is wide and splits into several channels, shallow, without slippery rocks, upstream of a sand or gravel bar.","Avoid rapids, rock steps, the area near a waterfall and the eddies behind large obstacles.","Keep your shoes on, undo the pack's hip belt and chest strap so you can get rid of it.","Cross facing the current, leaning on a strong stick placed upstream (three points of support), moving diagonally downstream.","In a group: stand shoulder to shoulder in a line in the direction of the current, the strongest upstream.","Dry and warm your feet and legs immediately on the other side."],"attention":["If you're swept away: on your back, feet first at the surface to cushion against rocks, and swim diagonally towards the bank. Never try to stand up in a strong current (risk of trapping a foot)."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"pas":{"titre":"Measuring distance by counting steps","intro":"Without GPS, knowing how many metres you've covered lets you locate yourself on the map.","etapes":["Calibrate before you go: walk a measured 100 m counting one step each time your left foot touches the ground (a “double step”).","Most adults take 60 to 70 double steps per 100 m on flat ground. Note your number.","While walking, tie a knot in a cord or move a pebble from one pocket to the other every 100 m.","Adjust for the terrain: you take more steps uphill, on steep descents, in vegetation, snow or at night."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"recoupement":{"titre":"Finding your position by cross-bearing","intro":"With a map, a compass and two visible landmarks, you can find your exact position.","etapes":["Pick two clearly visible features that are easy to find on the map (summit, pylon, church tower, end of a lake), ideally 60 to 90° apart.","Aim at the first with the compass and read its angle (bearing).","Work out the back bearing: add 180° if it's less than 180°, otherwise subtract 180°.","On the map, draw a line from that landmark in the direction of the back bearing.","Do the same with the second landmark: you are where the two lines cross.","Simpler: if you're on a known path or river, one bearing is enough, as you are where the line meets the path."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"navigation":{"titre":"Navigation tricks","intro":"Four simple techniques so you don't miss your destination.","etapes":["The handrail: follow a linear feature (path, river, forest edge, power line) that leads near the goal, rather than cutting through the woods.","Aiming off: to reach a bridge over a river, deliberately aim a little to the left. When you reach the river, you know you need to turn right.","The attack point: start from an obvious, nearby landmark (junction, end of a lake) to find a discreet goal (a spring, a bivouac area).","The catching feature: find on the map a feature just beyond the goal (stream, road). If you reach it, you've gone too far."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"miroir":{"titre":"Signal mirror","intro":"In sunshine, a mirror is the best way to be seen from afar: a flash can be spotted dozens of kilometres away.","etapes":["Without a mirror: the phone screen, a CD, a survival blanket, the polished bottom of a mess tin.","Stretch your free arm out in front of you, fingers in a V, and place the plane or helicopter between the two fingers.","With the other hand, angle the mirror so the patch of sunlight falls on your outstretched hand.","Gently and rhythmically tilt the mirror to sweep the reflection from your hand to the target, then back.","Climb to a high point if possible, and aim towards the sound if you can't see the aircraft."],"attention":["Don't flash very fast, and don't keep the reflection on the cockpit for more than a few seconds: you'd dazzle the pilot."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"solair":{"titre":"International ground-to-air codes","intro":"Five symbols understood by all air rescue services, to mark on the ground in an open area.","etapes":["V: need help.","X: need medical assistance.","N: no.","Y: yes.","Arrow: I'm leaving in this direction.","Size: at least 4 m wide by 6 m long (2:3 ratio), with strokes about 1 m wide.","Materials: branches, stones, clothing, survival blanket, trampled grass or packed snow. The symbol must contrast with the ground: straight lines and sharp angles don't exist in nature and stand out."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"cordelette":{"titre":"Making nettle cord","intro":"Long nettle stems and the inner bark of the lime tree give strong fibres. The “reverse twist” technique turns them into cord.","etapes":["Cut tall nettle stems in late summer or autumn (with gloves), and strip the leaves.","Crush each stem flat along its whole length, open it and remove the woody core: you're left with a strip of fibres.","Let the fibres dry a little, then soften them between your fingers.","Fold a bundle of fibres in two, not quite in the middle. Hold the fold in your left hand.","Twist the upper strand towards you, then cross it over the other, away from you. Repeat with the new upper strand.","Add new fibres before the end of each strand, staggered, to lengthen the cord.","Test the strength by pulling hard and tying a knot: if it doesn't break, the cord is usable."],"attention":["Only cut what you need, and never take bark from a living tree."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"ps_pls":{"titre":"Recovery position","intro":"For an unconscious person who is breathing: on their side, their tongue no longer blocks the throat and they won't choke if they vomit.","etapes":["Check that they don't respond but are breathing normally (watch the belly rise, listen, feel the breath for 10 seconds).","If they're not breathing or breathing abnormally: call 112 and start chest compressions if you're trained.","Place the arm nearest you at a right angle, palm up.","Put the back of their other hand against their cheek, on your side, and hold it there.","Raise their far knee, foot on the ground, and pull on that knee to roll them towards you onto their side.","Adjust the upper leg to a right angle and tilt the head back slightly, mouth towards the ground.","Call 112, cover the person and check their breathing regularly until help arrives."],"attention":["After a serious fall with suspected neck injury, only move them if they risk choking or the place is dangerous."],"isole":["Put the person in the recovery position (steps below in Help reachable mode) and stay close to them.","Check their breathing every 2 to 3 minutes at first, then regularly.","Insulate them from the ground (pack, mat, branches) and cover them: an unconscious person cools down very quickly.","Every 30 minutes, turn them onto the other side to avoid pressure injuries.","Note the time, their breathing and their responses: these notes will help the rescuers.","If they stop breathing: lay them on their back and start chest compressions (30 compressions in the centre of the chest, 5 to 6 cm deep, 100 to 120 per minute)."],"alerte":["Irregular, noisy breathing or breathing that stops","No waking after several minutes","Seizures"],"attentionIsole":["Alone and with no help, prolonged CPR has very little chance of success, except after drowning, lightning or hypothermia. Keep going for at least 30 minutes if you can."],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ERC) cross-checked with manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_choc":{"titre":"Shock","intro":"After a haemorrhage, an extensive burn, a fracture or a severe allergic reaction, the body may no longer supply the organs properly with blood. It is a life-threatening emergency.","etapes":["Recognise the signs: pale, cold and clammy skin, fast and weak pulse, fast breathing, thirst, anxiety, weakness, then confusion.","Treat the cause if possible (stop bleeding with pressure).","Lay the casualty on their back, insulate them from the ground, cover them.","If there's no injury to the legs, pelvis or back: raise their legs about 30 cm.","Give them nothing to drink or eat, talk to them, reassure them.","Call 112 and monitor their breathing continuously."],"attention":["A casualty in shock can deteriorate very quickly: never leave them alone if you can help it."],"isole":["Stop the cause if possible: pressure on the bleeding, cooling a burn.","Lay them on their back, insulate from the ground and cover. Raise the legs about 30 cm if they're not injured.","Build a shelter against wind and rain over the casualty.","Every 15 minutes: count the pulse and breathing and write them down (Monitoring a casualty sheet).","If they're fully awake, with no belly injury and no vomiting: small sips of water. Otherwise, nothing by mouth.","Don't leave them alone if there are several of you."],"alerte":["Shock on its own is a warning sign: do everything to get out or be found"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ERC) cross-checked with manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_attelle":{"titre":"Immobilising a limb (improvised splint)","intro":"With a fracture or a bad sprain, immobilising the limb limits pain and damage while waiting or during transport.","etapes":["Don't try to put a bone or joint back in place: immobilise it in the position found.","Prepare two rigid splints (sticks, walking poles, rolled foam mat, cardboard) long enough to block the joint above AND below the injury.","Pad with clothing, especially at bony points (ankles, knees, wrists).","Tie with bandages, scarves or cord, above and below the injury, never directly on it.","Arm: after the splint, support the bent forearm in a sling (jacket, scarf) against the body.","Check every 15 minutes that fingers or toes stay warm, pink and have feeling: if not, loosen."],"attention":["Open fracture (bone visible): cover the wound with a clean cloth without pressing on the bone, and call 112."],"isole":["Immobilise in the position found, blocking the joint above and below (steps in Help reachable mode).","Leg with no sticks to hand: tie it to the other leg, with padding between them.","Arm or wrist: splint, then sling and a bandage strapping the arm to the body. You can often still walk.","Check fingers or toes every hour: warm, pink, with feeling. Loosen the ties if it swells.","Bone visible: rinse the wound thoroughly with drinking water, cover with a clean cloth, don't touch the bone. The risk of infection makes getting out a priority.","Limb badly bent with a cold, pale, numb hand or foot, and no help for hours: pull gently and slowly in line to straighten it, then immobilise. Stop if the pain increases sharply or it won't move."],"alerte":["Hand or foot cold, pale or numb","Fracture of the thigh or pelvis: risk of major blood loss","Bone visible"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ERC) cross-checked with manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_brancard":{"titre":"Improvised stretcher","intro":"Carrying a casualty is exhausting and risky: only do it if help can't come or the place is dangerous.","etapes":["Find two strong poles about 2 m long (sound dead wood, joined walking poles).","Jacket method: zip up 2 or 3 jackets, turn the sleeves inside out and slide the poles through the sleeves.","Tarp method: lay one pole a third of the way across the tarp, fold over, lay the second pole, fold the rest over: the casualty's weight locks the fabric.","Test the stretcher with an uninjured person before putting the casualty on it.","Carry with 4 people if possible (2 are enough on easy ground), walking out of step, and take frequent breaks.","On a slope, keep the casualty's head uphill. Cover them and check their breathing at each break."],"attention":["Don't move a casualty who may have injured their spine, unless there is immediate danger."],"isole":["Alone with a casualty: only move them a short distance, to shelter or out of danger.","Drag the casualty lying on a tarp, sleeping bag or jacket, pulling by the shoulders, head towards you.","Over a longer distance: build a travois (two poles in a V joined by crosspieces and a tarp) that you pull by the point.","Take frequent breaks, check breathing and keep the casualty covered."],"alerte":["Suspected back or neck injury: only move if there is immediate danger"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ERC) cross-checked with manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"ps_laisser":{"titre":"Leaving a casualty to fetch help","intro":"If nobody can call and you're alone with a casualty, you may have to go and fetch help. Preparing this departure well can save their life.","etapes":["First, try everything to call: 112, SMS, climbing to a high point, waiting for someone to pass on the path.","Settle the casualty: sheltered, insulated from the ground, covered (survival blanket, dry clothes), in the recovery position if unconscious.","Leave within reach: water, food, torch, whistle, an extra warm garment.","Note their exact position (GPS coordinates from the crisis button, or precise landmarks) and write it down.","Leave a visible note: time of departure, the casualty's condition, direction taken.","Mark the route (branches, tape) to guide the rescuers back, and head for the nearest place you can call from."],"attention":["If the group has at least three people: one stays with the casualty, the other two leave together."],"isole":["Stay with the casualty and signal if they can't be left alone (unconscious, in shock), if night or bad weather is coming, or if you don't know the way.","Go for help if nobody will come looking for you, the casualty is stable and sheltered, and you know the way to somewhere you can call from.","Before leaving: casualty warm, insulated from the ground, with water, food, torch and whistle within reach.","Note their position (the app's crisis button), leave a note with the time of departure and the direction taken, and mark the route."],"alerte":["Casualty unconscious or in shock: only leave them alone as an absolute last resort"],"source":"Public first-aid guidance (Red Cross, ERC) cross-checked with manual FM 4-25.11 (public domain)"},"nuit_sans_duvet":{"titre":"Spending the night without a sleeping bag","intro":"An unplanned night outdoors without a sleeping bag: the aim is not to lose your heat, neither to the ground, nor to the wind, nor to damp.","etapes":["Stop before nightfall and before you're exhausted, so you have time to prepare.","Insulate from the ground first: a thick layer (20 to 30 cm compressed) of dead leaves, ferns or fine branches, or your emptied pack.","Get out of the wind: a dip in the ground, a big trunk, a rock, a tarp or branch shelter.","Put on all your dry clothes. Stuff dry leaves into the space between two layers of clothing: they trap air like down.","Wrap yourself in the survival blanket or a big bin bag, head covered.","Eat and drink before sleeping, and move (tense your muscles) whenever the cold wakes you.","Huddle together if there are several of you."],"attention":["If a fire is possible and allowed: a fire along your body with a reflector wall behind you, at a safe distance from the shelter."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten for Bivaska"},"chaleur":{"titre":"Protecting yourself from heat","intro":"In summer, heat and sun wear you out faster than cold. Shade and calm are your best allies.","etapes":["Walk early in the morning and late in the day, stop in the shade during the hot hours.","Cover your head and neck, wear loose, light-coloured clothing, keep long sleeves against the sun.","Make effective shade: stretch a tarp at least 50 cm off the ground so air can circulate. With two sheets stacked 20 to 30 cm apart, the shade is much cooler.","Don't lie directly on scorching ground: insulate yourself with your pack or branches.","Drink regularly before you're thirsty, and eat salty food.","Wet a scarf or T-shirt: evaporation cools you."],"attention":["Hot, dry skin, violent headache, confusion: heatstroke, a life-threatening emergency. Shade, water on the skin, fanning, 112."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten for Bivaska"},"essences_bois":{"titre":"Choosing your firewood","intro":"Not all woods burn the same. Knowing which to choose saves wood and energy.","etapes":["To start: birch (its bark burns even when damp), spruce and pine twigs (resinous, they catch quickly), thin hazel.","To last and make embers: beech, oak, hornbeam and ash, hardwoods that burn long and give steady heat.","Avoid as logs: conifers (pine, spruce), which crackle, throw sparks and blacken pots; poplar, willow and alder, which burn too fast.","Recognising dry wood: it's light, rings clearly when you knock two pieces together, snaps cleanly, and the bark comes off easily.","Never burn yew, oleander, cherry laurel or treated or painted wood: their fumes are toxic."],"attention":["Always dead wood picked up from the ground or dry standing wood, never a living tree."],"source":"US Army survival manual FM 3-05.70 (public domain), rewritten for Bivaska"},"contourner":{"titre":"Getting round an obstacle without losing your bearing","intro":"A marsh or a ravine in your way? With the compass and your steps, you can go round it and get back exactly on your bearing.","etapes":["At the obstacle, turn 90° (to the right, for example) and walk, counting your steps, until you're past the obstacle.","Take up your original bearing again and walk until you're past the obstacle on the other side.","Turn 90° the other way (to the left) and walk the SAME number of steps as before.","Take up your original bearing again: you're back on your line of march.","Simpler when you can see far: pick a tree or rock on the other side of the obstacle, on your line, walk round to it and take a new bearing from there."],"source":"US Army map reading and land navigation manuals (public domain), rewritten for Bivaska"},"ps_garrot":{"titre":"Improvised tourniquet","intro":"The life-saving action when a limb is bleeding heavily and pressure isn't enough, or when you need your hands free.","attention":["A tourniquet hurts a lot: that's normal. Not tight enough, it makes the bleeding worse: tighten until it stops completely.","Never a tourniquet on the neck, chest or belly: there, pressure only."],"isole":["Take a band 4 to 5 cm wide: folded scarf, fabric belt, strap. Never a string or thin cord.","Place it 5 to 7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the heart, not on a joint.","Tie a knot, put a strong stick on it, tie another knot over the stick.","Twist the stick until the bleeding stops completely.","Lock the stick (slide it under a tie) so it doesn't unwind.","Write the time it was applied where it can be seen, on the tourniquet or on the skin.","Don't loosen it to “let the limb breathe”: it stays on until medical care takes over."],"alerte":["A tourniquet in place means urgent evacuation: do everything to get out or be found"],"source":"Written for Bivaska: manuals FM 4-25.11 and FM 21-76 (public domain), cross-checked with remote-area first-aid recommendations (WMS, ERC)"},"ps_plaie_soin":{"titre":"Caring for a wound over several days","intro":"Far from help, the danger of a wound is infection. Thorough cleaning is the most effective action.","attention":["Before you go, check that your tetanus vaccination is up to date (booster every 10 years)."],"isole":["Once the bleeding has stopped, rinse the wound with at least 1 litre of drinking water (boiled then cooled, or filtered), as a jet: bottle with a pierced cap or a pierced plastic bag that you squeeze.","Remove remaining dirt with disinfected tweezers.","Clean, tidy wound less than 6 hours old: bring the edges together with strips (adhesive sutures), without tightening.","Dirty wound, animal bite or older wound: don't close it. Leave it open under a clean dressing so it can drain.","Change the dressing every day, or as soon as it's wet or dirty, rinsing again.","Keep the area rested and raised if possible."],"alerte":["Spreading redness, heat and swelling","Pus, bad smell","Red streak running towards the heart","Fever, chills"],"source":"Written for Bivaska: manuals FM 4-25.11 and FM 21-76 (public domain), cross-checked with remote-area first-aid recommendations (WMS, ERC)"},"ps_surveiller":{"titre":"Monitoring a casualty over time","intro":"Without help, how the casualty's condition develops decides everything: stay, leave or do everything to signal. Taking notes lets you see it coming.","isole":["At first every 15 minutes, then every hour, note in the notebook: the time, consciousness, breathing, pulse, skin, pain.","Consciousness: fully awake and coherent? Responds only to voice? Only when pinched? Doesn't respond?","Breathing: count the belly movements for 30 seconds and multiply by 2. Normal at rest: 12 to 20 per minute.","Pulse at the wrist (thumb side) or neck: count for 30 seconds and multiply by 2. Normal at rest: 60 to 100.","Skin: colour, temperature, sweating. Pale, cold and clammy: warning.","If they're conscious and have no belly injury, give them drinks regularly. Clear urine shows they're drinking enough.","Keep them dry, insulated from the ground, out of the wind, and change their position if they can't move by themselves."],"alerte":["Consciousness drops from one note to the next","Breathing over 30 or under 10 per minute","Pulse over 120 at rest","No urine for 12 hours"],"source":"Written for Bivaska: manuals FM 4-25.11 and FM 21-76 (public domain), cross-checked with remote-area first-aid recommendations (WMS, ERC)"},"simple":{"titre":"Overhand knot","intro":"The basic knot, at the root of almost all the others. A quick stopper at the end of a rope or line.","etapes":["Make a loop by crossing the working end over the standing part.","Pass the working end through the loop, going in from underneath.","Tighten by pulling both ends."],"attention":["It jams under a heavy load and weakens the rope. For a solid stopper, prefer the figure-eight."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"arret_double":{"titre":"Double overhand stopper","intro":"A bigger stopper than the overhand: it won't pull through an eyelet and is the basis of the double fisherman's knot.","etapes":["Start like an overhand knot, without tightening.","Pass the working end through the loop a second time, in the same direction.","Tighten gently, guiding the two turns so they wrap side by side."],"attention":["Very hard to untie after a heavy load."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"plat_ganse":{"titre":"Slipped reef knot","intro":"The reef knot that comes undone in one pull, like a shoelace. Handy for closing a bag, rolling a tarp or tying up a pack.","etapes":["Tie the first half of a reef knot: cross the left end over the right and tuck it under.","For the second half, fold one of the ends into a bight (a U-shaped loop).","This time cross the right end over the left and tie with the bight.","Tighten. To untie, just pull the free end of the bight."],"attention":["Like the reef knot, it is only for binding something: never for joining two ropes that will bear a load."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"chirurgien":{"titre":"Surgeon's knot","intro":"An improved reef knot: the first crossing is doubled and doesn't slip while you tie the second. Ideal for tightening a bandage, a bundle or fishing line.","etapes":["Cross the two ends and wrap them twice around each other.","Pull to tighten this double crossing: it holds by itself.","Cross again, the other way, just once.","Tighten all four strands evenly."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"huit_boucle":{"titre":"Figure-eight loop","intro":"A very strong fixed loop that is easy to check at a glance. For hanging a load, clipping onto a rope or tensioning a line.","etapes":["Fold the rope to form a bight about 30 cm long.","With this doubled section, make a loop by crossing it over both strands.","Pass the bight behind both strands.","Bring it back through the loop from the front.","Tighten by pulling the loop one way and both strands the other. The strands must stay parallel, without crossing."],"attention":["Hard to untie after a big load. If the strands cross inside the knot, start again."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"huit_rengaine":{"titre":"Figure-eight follow-through","intro":"The same figure-eight loop, but tied around a tree, a ring or a harness that you can't thread into a loop already tied.","etapes":["Tie a single figure-eight, without tightening it, about 1 m from the end.","Pass the working end around the tree or through the ring.","Feed it back into the eight where the working end of the first eight comes out.","Follow exactly the path of the first strand, in reverse, always staying on the same side of it.","Come out parallel to the standing part, tighten, and leave a tail of at least 10 cm."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"papillon":{"titre":"Alpine butterfly","intro":"A fixed loop in the middle of a rope that takes a pull in three directions. For hanging gear on a taut line, or isolating a damaged section of rope.","etapes":["In the middle of the rope, make a bight.","Twist this bight twice on itself, in the same direction: it forms an eight, with a small opening at the bottom.","Fold the top of the bight down, in front of the twists.","Pass it through the bottom opening, then pull it upwards.","Tighten by pulling the loop one way and the two strands in opposite directions."],"attention":["Check the shape: a loop coming out to the side, and the two strands coming out of the knot on opposite sides. If not, start again."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"chaise_double":{"titre":"Bowline on a bight","intro":"Two fixed loops that don't slide, tied in the middle of a rope. They can serve as an improvised seat for hoisting or lowering someone.","etapes":["Fold the rope in two to form a long bight.","With this doubled section, make a small loop as for a bowline.","Pass the bight up through the small loop from underneath.","Open the bight and pass it over the two big loops, until it encircles the knot from behind.","Tighten by pulling the two big loops one way and the standing part the other."],"attention":["To carry a person, first test the knot with your own weight, close to the ground."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"vache":{"titre":"Lark's head (cow hitch)","intro":"The quickest way to fix a loop of rope or a sling around a branch, a post or a handle.","etapes":["Fold the rope in two to form a bight.","Pass the bight around the branch or through the ring.","Pass both strands through the bight.","Pull both strands to tighten."],"attention":["It only holds well if both strands pull together. With only one strand loaded, it can slip."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"demi_cabestan":{"titre":"Munter hitch","intro":"A rope brake using a single carabiner: for lowering a load gently or belaying someone climbing.","etapes":["Make two identical loops, side by side, in the rope.","Fold them against each other like closing a book, not one behind the other as for a clove hitch.","Clip the carabiner through both loops and lock the gate.","Always keep hold of the brake strand: your hand does the braking. The closer the brake strand is brought to the loaded strand, the less it brakes."],"attention":["Only with a locking carabiner, preferably pear-shaped.","It twists the rope. Abseiling without training is very dangerous: this hitch is mainly for lowering gear or belaying someone."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"machard":{"titre":"Klemheist knot","intro":"A friction hitch: it slides by hand and locks as soon as you pull down. For climbing a rope, tensioning a line or making a handle.","etapes":["Take a loop of cord thinner than the main rope.","Wrap it 4 or 5 times around the rope going upwards, in tight turns that don't overlap.","Pass the bottom end through the top loop.","Pull this end down: the knot locks. To move it, push the turns by hand."],"attention":["It only locks in one direction, downwards. To lock both ways, use the Prusik knot."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"largage":{"titre":"Quick-release knot","intro":"It holds a load and comes undone at once when you pull the free end, even from a distance. Handy for a hammock, a hanging bag or an animal.","etapes":["Pass a bight of the working end behind the branch.","Form a bight in the part that will bear the load and pass it, from the front, through the first bight.","Pull the working end to tighten the first bight onto the second.","Form a bight with the working end and pass it through the second bight.","Pull the load-bearing part to lock everything. To release, pull the free end."],"attention":["Never for carrying a person: it can come undone with jerks."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"jambe_chien":{"titre":"Sheepshank","intro":"Shortens a rope without cutting it, or sets aside a damaged section until something better turns up.","etapes":["Fold the rope into an S to form three parallel strands, the length to be removed. The damaged part should be in the middle strand.","At one end, make a loop in the single strand and slip it over the bight on that side.","Do the same at the other end.","Put the rope under tension: the knot holds as long as it's loaded."],"attention":["It comes undone if the rope slackens. For more security, lock each bight with a toggle or a whipping."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"ruban":{"titre":"Water knot (tape knot)","intro":"The only reliable knot for joining two flat slings, or closing a sling into a loop.","etapes":["Tie a loose overhand knot at the end of one sling, nice and flat, without twists.","Feed the end of the other sling in where the first end comes out.","Follow exactly the path of the knot, in reverse, staying flat against the first sling.","Tighten very hard by pulling each strand, and leave tails of at least 7 cm."],"attention":["It can creep little by little with use: check the length of the tails before each use."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"ancre":{"titre":"Anchor bend","intro":"Fixes a rope securely to a ring, an improvised anchor or a stone with a hole. It stands up well to jerks and water.","etapes":["Make two round turns around the ring or branch.","Pass the working end around the standing part.","Tuck it under the two round turns.","Finish with a half hitch on the standing part and tighten."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"brelage_diagonal":{"titre":"Diagonal lashing","intro":"Joins two poles that cross at an angle, or that tend to spring apart: for bracing a shelter frame or a tripod.","etapes":["Tie a timber hitch around both poles, diagonally, at the crossing point.","Make 3 or 4 tight turns along the same diagonal.","Make 3 or 4 turns along the other diagonal.","Make 2 or 3 frapping turns between the two poles, around the previous turns.","Finish with a clove hitch on one of the poles."],"attention":["Only with dead wood, and dismantle everything before leaving."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"palomar":{"titre":"Palomar knot","intro":"The simplest hook knot and one of the strongest: useful for a survival fishing line.","etapes":["Double about 15 cm of line and pass the loop through the eye of the hook.","Tie a loose overhand knot with the doubled line, the hook hanging below.","Pass the whole hook through the loop.","Wet the knot, tighten by pulling both strands, then trim the tail."],"attention":["Fishing is regulated: a licence is generally required, and the rules vary by country and waterway."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"clinch":{"titre":"Improved clinch knot","intro":"The other great knot for a hook or lure, when the eye is too small for a doubled line.","etapes":["Pass the end of the line through the eye.","Wrap the end 5 to 7 times around the main line.","Pass the end through the small loop that has formed near the eye.","Then pass it back through the big loop that has just formed.","Wet it and tighten gently, then trim the tail."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"surliure":{"titre":"Whipping","intro":"Stops the end of a rope from fraying, using thin thread: string, dental floss or thick sewing thread.","etapes":["Fold the thread into a bight and lay it along the rope, loop towards the end of the rope.","Wind the thread tightly around the rope and the bight, working towards the end, over a length of about 1.5 times the rope's diameter. Leave the loop sticking out.","Pass the end of the thread through the loop.","Pull the other end of the thread, the starting one, to draw the loop under the turns, as far as the middle.","Trim both ends flush."],"attention":["A synthetic rope can also be melted with a lighter, but a whipping holds better and doesn't cut your hands."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"simple_ganse":{"titre":"Slipped overhand knot","intro":"A temporary stopper or tie that comes undone at once when you pull the end.","etapes":["Make a loop as for an overhand knot.","Instead of passing the end, pass a bight of the working end through the loop.","Tighten. To untie, pull the end of the bight."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"huit_ganse":{"titre":"Slipped figure-eight","intro":"A figure-eight stopper that comes undone instantly: to lock a rope temporarily in an eyelet or a pulley.","etapes":["Make a loop, then pass the working end behind the standing part, as for a figure-eight.","For the last pass through the loop, pass a bight of the working end instead of the end.","Tighten. To untie, pull the end of the bight."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"stevedore":{"titre":"Stevedore knot","intro":"A figure-eight with one more turn: a bigger stopper that won't pull through a wide hole and is still easy to untie.","etapes":["Make a loop by crossing the working end over the standing part.","Wrap the working end twice around the standing part, instead of once for the figure-eight.","Pass the working end through the starting loop.","Tighten by pulling both ends."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"pomme":{"titre":"Monkey's fist","intro":"A ball of rope at the end of a cord, which adds weight for throwing it far: over a branch to hang a food bag, or to someone.","etapes":["Wrap the rope 3 times around two fingers, leaving the standing part hanging.","Make 3 turns around the first ones, at right angles, tightening them in the middle.","Pass the end inside the first set of turns, then make 3 turns around the second set, passing inside the first each time.","Remove your fingers. Tighten little by little, following the rope in order, until you get a firm ball.","Tie the end to the standing part with two half hitches."],"attention":["Don't put a stone in the centre if it might be thrown towards a person: the ball becomes dangerous."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"ecoute_double":{"titre":"Double sheet bend","intro":"Joins two ropes of very different thickness, or wet and slippery ones. Safer than the single sheet bend.","etapes":["Make a bight with the thicker rope.","Pass the end of the thinner rope up through the bight from underneath.","Take it behind both strands of the bight, then tuck it under itself.","Make a second turn in the same place, next to the first, and tuck that under itself too.","Tighten by pulling both ropes."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"carrick":{"titre":"Carrick bend","intro":"Joins two thick ropes that will bear a heavy load. It stays easy to untie, even after getting wet.","etapes":["With the first rope, make a loop whose end passes over the standing part.","Lay the end of the second rope under this loop.","Work the second rope forward, alternating at each crossing: over the first rope's standing part, under its end, over the loop.","Keep alternating: under its own standing part, then over the loop. The two ends come out diagonally, on opposite sides.","Pull both ropes: the knot capsizes and settles. That's normal."],"attention":["Check that at each crossing the rope goes over once, under once. A single mistake and the knot fails."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"pecheur_simple":{"titre":"Fisherman's knot","intro":"Joins two cords or two lines of the same thickness, simply and securely.","etapes":["Lay the two ends side by side, pointing in opposite directions.","With the first end, tie an overhand knot around the other cord.","With the second end, tie an overhand knot around the first cord.","Pull both cords: the two knots slide towards each other and lock."],"attention":["To bear a person's weight, prefer the double fisherman's knot."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"sang":{"titre":"Blood knot","intro":"The fishing knot for joining two fine nylon lines without slipping.","etapes":["Cross the two lines over about ten centimetres.","Wrap the first end 5 times around the other line, then bring it back between the two lines, at the crossing point.","Wrap the second end 5 times around the first line, in the other direction.","Pass this second end through the same central gap, but in the opposite direction to the first.","Wet it, pull the two lines slowly, then trim the ends."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"roulant":{"titre":"Rolling hitch","intro":"It grips a pole or another rope and doesn't slip when pulled lengthwise. For pulling a log, taking the strain off a loaded rope or tensioning a tarp.","etapes":["Pass the end around the pole, crossing the standing part, on the side you'll pull towards.","Make a second turn next to the first, crossing the standing part again, still on the pulling side.","Pass the end around the pole on the other side of the standing part and tuck it under itself.","Tighten, then pull lengthwise, on the side of the two turns."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"hune":{"titre":"Topsail halyard hitch (buntline hitch)","intro":"Fixes a rope to an eyelet or ring that keeps moving, like a tarp flapping in the wind: it tightens instead of loosening.","etapes":["Pass the end through the eyelet or ring.","With the end, make a turn around the standing part.","Make a second turn around the standing part, between the first turn and the ring.","Tuck the end under this second turn: it's a clove hitch tied around the standing part.","Tighten by pulling the standing part."],"attention":["Very hard to untie after a heavy load."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"levier":{"titre":"Marlinspike hitch","intro":"Slip a stick into a rope to make a handle: for pulling hard, tightening a lashing or making the rungs of a rope ladder.","etapes":["Make a loop in the rope.","Fold it back over the standing part.","Slide the stick over the sides of the loop and under the standing part.","Pull: the rope locks on the stick. To release, remove the stick."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"bachmann":{"titre":"Bachmann hitch","intro":"A friction hitch with a carabiner: the carabiner acts as a handle to slide it. Practical with gloves or cold hands.","etapes":["Clip a loop of cord into a carabiner.","Hold the carabiner flat against the main rope, gate facing out.","Wrap the cord 3 or 4 times around the rope and the back of the carabiner, working upwards, passing through the carabiner each time.","Load the cord loop downwards: the knot locks. Push the carabiner to move it."],"attention":["It only locks when pulled downwards. Never grab the turns during a fall: the knot would no longer lock."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"chaise_tours":{"titre":"Double bowline","intro":"A safer bowline: the doubled central loop grips the rope better, especially if it's slippery or stiff.","etapes":["Make two small loops on top of each other instead of one, the working end passing over the top.","Pass the working end up through both loops from underneath.","Take it behind the standing part.","Bring it back down through both loops.","Tighten and leave a tail of at least 10 cm."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"boucle_simple":{"titre":"Overhand loop","intro":"The quickest loop to tie, for a light load: hanging a bag, a bottle or a drying line.","etapes":["Fold the end of the rope into a bight.","Tie an overhand knot with this doubled section.","Tighten by pulling the loop and both strands."],"attention":["It jams and weakens the rope: for a real load, prefer the figure-eight loop."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"chainette":{"titre":"Daisy chain (chain sinnet)","intro":"Shortens and stores a long rope without tangles, and unravels it in one go.","etapes":["Make a loop near the end of the rope.","Pull a bight of the standing part through this loop.","Pull a new bight through the previous one, and carry on like this along the whole length.","To lock it, pass the end through the last bight. To undo it, pull the end out and pull: everything unravels."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"tresse":{"titre":"Three-strand braid","intro":"Makes a stronger cord from thin strands: nettle fibres, lime bark, grasses, string.","etapes":["Tie the three strands together at one end, or wedge them under a stone.","Pass the right strand over the middle one: it becomes the middle strand.","Pass the left strand over the new middle strand.","Repeat, alternating right and left, tightening evenly.","Add a new strand by overlapping it for a few centimetres when one gets short. Finish with an overhand knot."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"filet":{"titre":"Improvised net","intro":"With cord, a net can serve as a hammock, a carrying bag or a drying rack.","etapes":["Stretch a head rope between two trees.","Attach equal lengths of cord to it at regular intervals, with lark's heads.","Tie each strand to its neighbour with an overhand knot, all at the same height: that's the first row of meshes.","Next row: shift by half a mesh and tie each strand to its other neighbour.","Carry on row after row, keeping the meshes the same size, then tie the bottom onto a second rope."],"attention":["Fishing or hunting with a net is forbidden in most European countries."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"epissure_oeil":{"titre":"Eye splice","intro":"A permanent loop at the end of a three-strand rope, stronger than any knot.","etapes":["Unlay the three strands for about 15 cm and whip the end of each one.","Fold the rope to form the eye of the size you want.","Tuck the middle strand under one strand of the standing part, against the lay.","Tuck the left strand under the next strand of the standing part, to the left of the first.","Turn the splice over and tuck the last strand under the remaining free strand of the standing part.","Carry on: each strand goes over one strand and under the next. Make at least 5 tucks, then trim the ends."],"attention":["Only for laid rope (visible strands), not for braided climbing rope."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"epissure_arriere":{"titre":"Back splice","intro":"Finishes the end of a three-strand rope neatly so it never frays.","etapes":["Unlay the strands for about ten centimetres.","Make a crown: fold each strand over its neighbour, in the same direction, and pass the last one through the loop of the first. Tighten.","Tuck each strand back towards the rope, over one strand and under the next, against the lay.","Make 3 tucks with each strand.","Roll the splice under your foot to settle it, then trim the ends."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"epissure_courte":{"titre":"Short splice","intro":"Repairs a cut three-strand rope, or joins two, without a knot: the join stays strong.","etapes":["Unlay the strands of both ends for about 15 cm and whip the end of each strand.","Interlock the two ends: each strand of one rope sits between two strands of the other.","Temporarily tie the centre with string so it holds.","On one side, tuck each strand over one strand and under the next, against the lay. Make 3 tucks.","Remove the temporary tie and do the same on the other side.","Roll the splice under your foot to settle it, then trim the ends."],"attention":["The splice thickens the rope: it will no longer run through a small pulley."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"surliure_francaise":{"titre":"French whipping (half-hitch whipping)","intro":"Another way of finishing the end of a rope, or making a non-slip grip on a handle, with a series of half hitches.","etapes":["Tie the thin thread onto the rope with an overhand knot.","Make a half hitch around the rope and pull it tight against the knot.","Make the next one right beside it, always in the same direction: the knots form a small spiral ridge.","Carry on for a length equal to 1.5 times the diameter, or along the whole handle.","Finish with two tight half hitches and cut the thread."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"noeuds_eviter":{"titre":"Two knots not to confuse","intro":"The reef knot has two false twins that fail under load: the granny knot and the thief knot. Knowing how to recognise them avoids a nasty surprise.","etapes":["Correct reef knot: left over right, then right over left. Both ends come out on the same side and the knot is nicely symmetrical.","Granny knot: left over right twice. The ends come out at an angle, the knot is twisted and it slips.","Thief knot: it looks like the reef knot, but the two ends come out diagonally, on opposite sides. It fails very easily.","If in doubt, untie it and tie it again saying out loud: “left over right, right over left”."],"source":"Standard knotting methods, written for Bivaska"},"partir_leger":{"titre":"Travelling light","intro":"The old principle of the backwoodsmen: every item must earn its weight. A light pack tires you less, and you stay sharper.","etapes":["Spread all your gear on the floor before leaving and weigh the pack.","For each item, ask yourself: “Did I use it last time? Does another item already do the same job?”","Keep first what's hard to make outdoors: a cutting tool, shelter, a way to make fire, a water container, cord.","Choose items that do several jobs (the pot is also a bowl, the tarp is also a rain collector).","Share the group gear among the members, so nobody carries everything.","Weigh the pack when you get back and note what wasn't used: that's the list of what you'll leave behind next time."],"attention":["Light, but never without the safety minimum: something to warm you, signal and treat yourself."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"habits_couches":{"titre":"Dressing for the bivouac","intro":"Two thin garments keep warmth better than one thick one: the air trapped between them insulates.","etapes":["Next to the skin: wool or synthetic. Never cotton, which stays wet and cold when you sweat.","On top: one or two thin warm layers (fleece, wool), easy to take off.","Outside: a wind and rain jacket, loose enough to go over everything else.","A collar that turns up, a hat and gloves: you lose a lot of comfort through the neck and extremities.","Keep a dry outfit just for the night, packed in a waterproof bag.","Take off a layer before you sweat, going uphill, and put it back on as soon as you stop."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"secher":{"titre":"Drying shoes and clothes","intro":"Damp things mean a cold night and blisters the next day. But too close to the fire, leather cracks and synthetics melt.","etapes":["Stuff wet shoes with dry grass, leaves or paper, and change it every hour: it soaks up the water from the inside.","Dry things in moving air, on a branch at a safe distance from the fire: you should be able to hold your hand in the same spot comfortably.","Never put shoes or synthetic clothing against hot stones or above the flames.","Damp socks and gloves: slip them under your jacket or into the sleeping bag, against you. Your warmth dries them overnight.","In the morning, shake the sleeping bag and air it: it holds your body's moisture."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"affuter":{"titre":"Looking after knife and hatchet","intro":"A tool that cuts well is a safer tool: it slips less and needs less force.","etapes":["Bring a small sharpening stone or plate, one coarse side and one fine side.","Wet the stone, lay the blade flat then raise the spine about 20° (the thickness of two coins under the spine).","Slide the blade as if cutting a thin slice off the stone, from heel to tip, the same number of times on each side.","Finish on the fine side, then on a leather belt or your trousers, spine of the blade first.","Wrap a little waxed string or tape around the hatchet's handle: the grip stays secure with wet hands.","Wipe and dry the blade every evening; a drop of cooking oil is enough against rust."],"attention":["Always cut outwards, never towards your leg or hand. Kneel for the hatchet: if it bounces, it hits the ground and not your shin."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"ustensiles":{"titre":"Improvised utensils","intro":"With a knife and dead wood, you can make in minutes whatever is missing around the fire.","etapes":["Poker: a dry stick about 1 m long, as thick as two fingers, flattened at the end, with a notch for lifting a pot's handle.","Ember tongs: a flexible stick a finger thick, thinned in the middle, warmed gently near the embers then bent in two. Trim both ends so they meet.","Fork: a small forked branch, peeled and sharpened.","Spatula and spoon: a board split from sound dead wood, hollowed out with the tip of the knife.","Camp broom: fallen twigs tied around a stick, for sweeping the fire area and the shelter floor."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"silex":{"titre":"Flint and steel fire","intro":"The old-timers' method: a hard stone and a piece of steel throw sparks that catch on prepared tinder.","etapes":["Find a hard stone with a sharp edge: flint, quartz, chert. Soft stones (limestone, slate) don't work.","Take a piece of carbon steel: a fire striker, an old file, or the spine of a non-stainless steel knife.","Lay a piece of char cloth or tinder fungus on the stone, held with your thumb, 1 mm below the edge.","Strike the edge of the stone with the steel, a sharp scraping blow downwards, at about 45°.","When a spark catches on the tinder (a small red dot), blow on it gently.","Slip it into a nest of dry fibres and blow until it flames."],"attention":["Steel sparks are cool: they only catch on very dry, charred tinder (Char cloth sheet)."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"amadou_naturel":{"titre":"Finding natural tinder","intro":"Tinder is what turns a spark into a flame. The forest offers it everywhere, as long as you keep it dry.","etapes":["Birch bark: the thin white outer layers burn even when damp thanks to their oil. Take them from a dead tree or the ground.","Dry grass and fibres: dead grass, old man's beard (clematis), bulrush down, fibres of the inner bark of dead wood, rubbed between your hands.","Dry puffball: the brown powder of an old, dry puffball takes sparks.","Resinous wood shavings: scrape the inside of a dead pine stump with your knife, very rich in resin.","Keep everything in a waterproof box or bag, and gather tinder as you walk whenever you come across some."],"attention":["Don't take bark from a living tree: you injure it. And don't take bird or rodent nests."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"feu_neige":{"titre":"Making fire on snow","intro":"A fire laid on snow sinks and goes out in its own water. It needs a floor.","etapes":["Tread down the snow over a circle a metre across.","Lay a floor of dry dead wood: a layer of sticks packed side by side, then a second layer crosswise.","Build the fire on this floor, with plenty of kindling prepared in advance.","Shield it from the wind with a tarp, a rock or a row of logs.","Regularly add logs to the floor as it burns away."],"attention":["Never light a fire under a snow-laden tree: the heat brings it down in one block onto the fire."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"feu_trois_buches":{"titre":"Three-log fire (star fire)","intro":"An economical fire that lasts: three big logs meet in the centre, and you push them in as they burn.","etapes":["Light a small, lively fire.","Arrange three logs of dead wood around it, like the spokes of a star, their ends in the embers.","For more heat, push the logs towards the centre. For less, pull them apart.","At night, push them together just before sleeping: the fire smoulders until morning."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"feu_pierre":{"titre":"Fire on rock: beware of stones","intro":"A damp or porous stone can burst when it heats up and throw out fragments.","etapes":["Avoid river pebbles and stones that have been soaking in water.","On a rock slab, first spread a layer of earth or sand a few centimetres thick.","For a stone ring, choose dry stones taken well away from water.","Don't sit with your back to a rock face heated by the fire."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"feu_cuisine":{"titre":"Preparing a cooking fire","intro":"You cook on embers, not flames: flames blacken pots, burn the outside and leave the inside raw.","etapes":["Light your fire 30 to 45 minutes before cooking, with hardwood if possible (oak, beech, ash).","Lay two parallel logs, the width of your pot apart: they support it and keep the embers in the middle.","Let the wood turn into a bed of red embers.","Keep a small fire going alongside to produce new embers, and rake them under the pot with a stick.","For tea or coffee water, put the pot on the embers, not in the smoke: otherwise it will taste of smoke."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"cremaillere":{"titre":"Pot hanger and pot support","intro":"For hanging a pot over the fire and adjusting the height, without putting anything in the embers.","etapes":["Drive two forked sticks into the ground on either side of the fire, about 70 cm apart.","Lay a crossbar in the two forks, above the fire.","Simple hook: a small forked branch turned upside down, hung from the crossbar, with a notch at the bottom for the handle.","Adjustable hook: a stick with several notches cut at different heights. Change notch to raise or lower the pot.","One-stick version: push a long stick into the ground at an angle, its end above the fire, and wedge its base with a big stone."],"attention":["Use dead wood thick enough not to catch fire: check it regularly."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"cuire_sans":{"titre":"Cooking without utensils","intro":"A frying pan isn't essential: embers, sticks and ashes are enough to cook fish, meat or bread.","etapes":["Skewer: spear the food on a stick of sound dead wood, and hold it above the embers, not in the flame. Turn it often.","Flat fish: split it open, wedge it between two split sticks, and plant the whole thing at an angle facing the embers.","Ash bread: shape a thick flatbread (flour, water, salt), lay it on a very hot flat stone or wrap it around a stick above the embers.","Cooking under the ash: wrap potatoes, fish or vegetables in foil or large wet leaves, and bury them under the embers.","Hanging roast: hang a piece of meat in front of the fire on a wet string. As it dries, the string twists by itself and turns the meat."],"attention":["Never use yew, cherry laurel or holly wood for skewers: they're poisonous."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"trou_braises":{"titre":"Ember-pit oven","intro":"The old-timers' slow cooking: a pot buried in the embers cooks by itself while you sleep or walk.","etapes":["Dig a hole a little bigger than your lidded pot (ideally cast iron).","Light a good fire in it and let it burn for an hour to heat the sides.","Remove some of the embers, put the closed pot at the bottom, put the embers back around and on top.","Cover with earth to keep the heat in.","Let it cook for 4 to 8 hours (beans, stew), then carefully clear away the earth."],"attention":["Only where fires are allowed, away from roots, and fill in the hole before leaving."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"bannock":{"titre":"Trapper's bread (bannock)","intro":"The bread of the Far North voyageurs: a few dry ingredients, water, and a pan or a stick.","etapes":["At home, mix in a bag: 2 glasses of flour, 1 teaspoon of baking powder, 1 pinch of salt, and if you like, powdered milk or sugar.","At camp, add water little by little until you get a soft dough that doesn't stick to your fingers.","In a pan: flatten the dough to 2 cm, cook over gentle embers, 8 to 10 minutes on each side.","On a stick: roll the dough into a long sausage, wind it in a spiral around a dry stick and hold it above the embers, turning it.","The bread is done when it sounds hollow when tapped."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"lit_camp":{"titre":"Making an insulating camp bed","intro":"The cold that ruins a night almost always comes from the ground, not the air. A good bed insulates from below.","etapes":["Never sleep directly on bare earth, even with a sleeping bag: the ground draws away your heat.","Choose flat, slightly raised ground, cleared of stones and branches.","With a mat: add a layer of dry dead leaves underneath to double the insulation.","Without a mat: pile up at least 20 to 30 cm of dry dead leaves, dry grass or dead bracken. Once compressed, a hand's thickness should remain.","Mark out the bed with two dead logs, one at the head and one at the feet, so the layer doesn't spread.","Lie down for a while, then add more padding where it has compressed."],"attention":["In Europe, you don't cut living trees or branches without the owner's permission: use dead wood fallen on the ground. The old-timers cut living fir boughs: today that is forbidden and unnecessary, as dead leaves insulate very well."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"azimut_retour":{"titre":"Retracing your steps with a compass","intro":"If you noted your direction when setting off, you can always come back: just follow the opposite direction.","etapes":["When leaving the camp or path, take a bearing with the compass and note the angle (for example 60°).","To come back, add 180° if the angle is less than 180°, or subtract 180° otherwise (60° + 180° = 240°).","Set the compass to this new angle and walk aiming at successive landmarks: a tree, a rock, then the next one.","Check the compass every few minutes: you almost always drift to the same side without noticing.","If the sun or a shadow is suddenly on the “wrong” side, you've turned: stop and get back on track."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"perdu_calme":{"titre":"Thinking you're lost: the right reflexes","intro":"Most accidents involving lost people come from panic, not the terrain. Your first tool is staying calm.","etapes":["Stop, sit down, drink a little water. Don't set off again at random.","Remember the last safe place you recognise, and how long you've been walking since.","Look at the map, the compass, the app (GPS position without signal), the sun.","If you really don't know where you are: mark the spot (branch, coloured cloth) and explore in a star pattern, 100 steps maximum in each direction, returning to the starting point each time.","A river or a path almost always leads to houses: follow them downstream.","If night is falling or the weather is turning: settle in for the night and prepare signals rather than carrying on."],"source":"Inspired by Nessmuk (1884), Kephart (1906) and Beard (1920), public-domain works, rewritten and adapted for Bivaska"},"ligne_faitiere":{"titre":"Rigging a ridgeline","intro":"The rope stretched between two trees is the backbone of almost every tarp shelter. Well tensioned, the tarp doesn't flap and water runs off.","etapes":["Choose two healthy trees, a little further apart than the length of the tarp (4 to 5 m for a 3 m tarp).","Tie one end to the first tree with a round turn and two half hitches, at the height you want: about 1 m for a low A-frame, 1.5 m for a lean-to.","Go to the second tree, pass the rope around it and tension it with a trucker's hitch. It should ring like a guitar string.","Lock it with two slipped half hitches: you'll undo them in one pull in the morning.","Lay the tarp over the rope, or clip its eyelets on with two Prusik knots so you can slide it and tension it lengthwise.","Check that the rope is level, or slightly lower at the foot end so water runs off that way."],"attention":["Protect the bark with a rag or wide strap if the rope is thin, and never tighten around a young tree."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"goutte_eau":{"titre":"Stopping water running into the shelter","intro":"In rain, water follows the ridgeline and ends up dripping… onto your sleeping bag. A simple piece of string solves the problem.","etapes":["Cut two pieces of string about 20 cm long.","Tie one onto the ridgeline on each side, just before the point where the rope goes under the tarp.","Let the ends hang towards the ground: water sliding along the rope runs down the string and drips outside.","Do the same on the corner guylines if water runs along them.","If needed, dig a small channel under the edge of the tarp on the downhill side to carry away water falling from the roof."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"appentis_bache":{"titre":"Tarp lean-to","intro":"A sloping roof, open on one side only: the quickest shelter, with a nice view and room to get organised. Back to the wind.","etapes":["Work out where the wind is coming from: the low side of the shelter must face it.","Rig a ridgeline about 1.2 to 1.5 m high.","Lay one edge of the tarp over the rope and tie its corners.","Pull the other edge down at a slope towards the ground, on the wind side, and peg it at ground level with pegs or sticks.","To block a side wind, fold down and peg one of the front corners, or close the side with dead branches.","Put your bedding right at the back, where the roof is lowest: that's the most sheltered area."],"attention":["Poorly protected if the wind changes: watch the weather and be ready to lower the front."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"soc_charrue":{"titre":"Plough-point tarp","intro":"A single high tie-out, three corners on the ground: a one-person shelter, very wind-resistant, pitched in a few minutes.","etapes":["Spread the square tarp out as a diamond, one point towards the tree.","Tie that point to the tree (or to the top of a walking pole) at about 1.2 m high.","Peg the opposite point to the ground, well tensioned, on the side the wind comes from.","Peg the two side points to the ground, pulling so the fabric is taut without creases.","The opening is on the tree side, back to the wind. Slide in head towards the tree, where it's highest."],"attention":["Little room: ideal for a night alone, not for storing a whole pack."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"bache_tube":{"titre":"A-frame with a groundsheet","intro":"With a big tarp, you fold the edges under you: you get a roof and a floor in one piece. Very useful when the ground is wet.","etapes":["You need a tarp of at least 3 × 4 m.","Rig a low ridgeline, 80 cm to 1 m off the ground.","Drape the tarp over the rope, lengthwise.","Instead of pegging the edges, fold them inwards under the shelter: they overlap and form a floor.","Your weight on the floor tensions both sides of the roof. Weigh down the folded edges with your pack."],"attention":["The shelter is open at both ends: point it in the direction of the wind, never across it."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"abri_poncho":{"titre":"Poncho shelter","intro":"A rain poncho is a small tarp you wear. In an unplanned night out, it becomes a roof.","etapes":["Close the hood tightly: pull the drawcord and knot it on itself so it doesn't act as a funnel.","Stretch a cord between two trees about 80 cm off the ground.","Put the poncho over it, as a lean-to or an A-frame depending on its size.","Tie the corners to pegs or roots with taut-line hitches.","Insulate the ground with your emptied pack and a thick layer of dead leaves."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"bache_tempete":{"titre":"Pitching your tarp in rain and wind","intro":"In bad weather, the order of actions matters: put the roof up first, then settle underneath in the dry.","etapes":["Keep the tarp and cord at the top of your pack, reachable without unpacking everything.","Rig the ridgeline first, then the tarp, before taking anything else out.","Pitch the shelter low: the lower it is, the less the wind can grab it. Edges at ground level on the wind side.","Drive the pegs in at 45°, tip towards the shelter, and double the anchors on the wind side.","Re-tension all the guylines after an hour: wet cord stretches.","Once the roof is up, set up your bedding underneath and put what must stay dry into waterproof bags."],"attention":["In violent wind, move away from trees: branches break and fall (see “Spotting the dangers of the camp”)."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"ancrages":{"titre":"Pegs and improvised anchors","intro":"Lost peg, ground too hard, sand or snow: there's always a way to tie down a corner of the tarp.","etapes":["Wooden peg: a sound dead stick, thumb-thick, sharpened to a point, with a notch at the top for the cord. Drive it in at 45° outwards.","Stony ground: tie the cord around a big stone, or drive a peg between two stones and wedge it with a third.","Roots and stumps: simply pass the cord around them.","Sand or snow (deadman anchor): tie the cord to the middle of a stick, dig a 25 cm hole, lay the stick at the bottom across the direction of pull, fill in and pack down.","Ground too hard: put a big stone on the corner of the tarp or on a stick holding it."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"hamac":{"titre":"Sleeping in a hammock","intro":"A hammock solves the problem of wet ground, slopes and stones. But it's cold underneath and needs to be set up methodically.","etapes":["Choose two healthy trees, at least as thick as a thigh, 4 to 5 m apart, with no dead branch above.","Use wide straps (at least 2.5 cm): they protect the bark. Never thin cord around a trunk.","Attach the straps at roughly eye level, so they form a 30° angle with the ground once the hammock is loaded.","Adjust the hammock so it's at chair height (45 cm) when you sit in it.","Lie diagonally: your back is almost flat and you don't feel folded in two.","Insulate underneath: under your weight, the sleeping bag compresses and no longer keeps you warm. Slip a mat into the hammock, or use an underquilt.","Stretch a tarp above, on a ridgeline higher than the straps, with drip lines on the straps."],"attention":["Below 10 °C, a hammock without insulation underneath is freezing, even with a good sleeping bag.","Check that trees and straps are allowed on the site."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"abri_conique":{"titre":"Conical pole shelter","intro":"Poles stood in a cone and covered: a shelter you can sit up in, which keeps out rain well if it's steep enough.","etapes":["Gather three strong dead poles about 2.5 m long.","Lash them together near one end with a tripod lashing, then spread the legs into a triangle.","Lean other poles all around, leaving a narrow opening on the side away from the wind.","Cover from the bottom up, like roof tiles, with large pieces of dead bark, dead branches, then a thick layer of leaves.","The steeper the slope, the better the water runs off: aim for really steep sides.","Insulate the ground inside with 20 to 30 cm of dry leaves."],"attention":["Only dead wood fallen on the ground: in Europe, cutting living branches is forbidden without the owner's permission.","Takes 2 to 4 hours: start well before nightfall, and dismantle everything when you leave."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"abri_chablis":{"titre":"Sheltering against an uprooted tree","intro":"The root plate of a fallen tree forms a natural wall against the wind. You can lean a lean-to against it in minutes.","etapes":["First check its stability: the tree must be lying firmly on the ground, not held up by other trees.","Settle on the side sheltered from the wind, against the root plate or along the trunk.","Lean dead poles from the trunk to the ground to form a lean-to roof.","Cover with dead branches and leaves, or stretch your tarp over it.","Insulate the ground: the hollow left by the roots is often damp, fill it with dry leaves."],"attention":["Never cut a root of a half-uprooted tree: the root plate can spring back up suddenly and crush whatever is underneath.","The root hole fills with water in the rain: don't sleep at the bottom."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"abri_rocher":{"titre":"Sleeping under a rock overhang","intro":"An overhanging rock offers a ready-made roof. But you must check it's not dangerous.","etapes":["Look at the ground: fresh rock chips or recently fallen stones point to rockfall. Move on.","Look at the ceiling: no open cracks or blocks that look loose.","Check that no water runs or drips from the rock, and that the floor isn't a temporary stream bed.","Look for animal signs (droppings, hair, bones): the place may already be occupied.","Close off the front with your tarp or dead branches to cut the wind, and insulate the ground well, which is often cold."],"attention":["Never light a fire right under the rock: the heat can make the stone burst and loosen slabs.","The limestone cliffs of the Ourthe are often protected sites: check that bivouacking is allowed there."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"abri_neige":{"titre":"Snow shelter","intro":"Snow insulates surprisingly well: a well-made shelter stays around 0 °C even when it's freezing hard outside.","etapes":["At the foot of a big spruce, the low branches often form a snow-free hollow: clear it further by hand and settle underneath.","On flat ground, dig a trench as long as your body, shoulder-wide, about 1 m deep.","Cover it with dead poles or walking poles, then your tarp, then a layer of snow.","Keep a small opening, preferably lower than where you sleep: cold air stays at the bottom.","Insulate yourself from the snow underneath: mat, pack, dead branches. That's the most important thing.","Poke a ventilation hole in the roof with a stick, and check it stays open."],"attention":["Never a stove or candle in a poorly ventilated snow shelter: risk of carbon monoxide poisoning.","At the foot of a tree, the snow on the branches can fall in one block: don't settle there in a thaw or in wind."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"dangers_camp":{"titre":"Spotting the dangers of the camp","intro":"Before putting down your pack, take two minutes to look up, around and down. Most bivouac accidents come from there.","etapes":["Up: look for dead branches and dead or leaning trees. Foresters call them “widowmakers”. Never sleep under them, even in calm weather.","Around: avoid ant hills, wasp nests, burrows and animal paths (narrow trails, flattened grass, wild boar rooting).","Down: no dry stream bed, hollow where water collects or low bank. A river can rise a metre overnight after a storm upstream.","Cold: cold air flows down and collects at the bottom of valleys and hollows. Settle a little above the bottom, on a ledge.","Wind: avoid summits and ridges. A forest edge or a copse cuts the wind.","Thunderstorms: no isolated tree, summit, pylon or metal fence. In a forest, a stand of trees of the same height is better."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"plan_camp":{"titre":"Organising your camp","intro":"A tidy camp means fewer things lost in the dark, fewer animal visits and a safer night.","etapes":["Pitch the shelter first, in the best spot, back to the prevailing wind.","Kitchen area: downwind of the shelter, at least a few metres away, so smoke, sparks and smells drift away from your bed.","Water: fill your bottles before night. Wash up away from the stream (at least 60 m) and scatter the dirty water.","Toilet: at least 60 m from water, camp and paths, in a 15 cm hole filled back in.","Food: never in the shelter. Store it away from camp (see “Protecting your food from animals”).","Keep within reach at night: head torch, water, knife, whistle, phone, glasses. Always in the same place.","Before dark, walk round the camp: everything that must stay dry is put away, the wood is under the tarp, the guylines are visible (a bit of light-coloured cloth on them stops you tripping)."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"nourriture_animaux":{"titre":"Protecting your food from animals","intro":"In Europe, no bears, but foxes, wild boar, martens and above all rodents that can chew through a bag in one night.","etapes":["Store all food and rubbish in a closed bag, odour-proof if possible (double freezer bag).","Never food in the shelter or tent: a wild boar or fox will come looking for it there.","Hang the bag from a branch at least 2 m off the ground and 1 m from the trunk, about twenty metres from where you sleep.","To hang it: tie a stone to a cord, throw it over the branch, attach the bag and hoist it up, then tie the other end to a nearby tree.","Also put toothpaste, creams and anything scented in the bag.","Never feed animals and pick up all leftovers: an animal that gets used to it becomes a problem for the next campers."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"nuit_chaude":{"titre":"Sleeping warm","intro":"A sleeping bag doesn't produce heat: it keeps in the heat you make. A few habits gain you several degrees.","etapes":["Eat a fatty, hot meal in the evening, and keep a snack by you for the night: digesting warms you.","Go to the toilet before bed: your body won't spend energy keeping that liquid warm.","Get into bed warm, not chilled: walk or do a few squats before slipping into the sleeping bag.","Hot-water bottle: fill a metal or well-sealed bottle with hot water, wrap it in a sock and put it between your thighs or against your belly.","Keep a hat and dry socks just for the night. Never the damp socks of the day.","Don't breathe into the sleeping bag: the moisture in your breath wets it. Keep your nose and mouth outside and close the hood around them.","Add your dry clothes on top of you or inside the bag where you feel cold, and your pack under your feet to insulate from the ground."],"attention":["A hot-water bottle that's too hot against the skin can burn you while you sleep: always wrap it.","If you shiver uncontrollably or become clumsy, it's the start of hypothermia: see the dedicated sheet."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"condensation":{"titre":"Managing damp and condensation","intro":"In the morning, the inside of the tarp is streaming and the sleeping bag is damp? That's condensation: your breath and moist air turning into water on the cold fabric.","etapes":["Avoid valley bottoms, wet meadows and the immediate edge of the water: that's where the air is dampest and the dew heaviest.","Keep the shelter open and well ventilated: a tarp raised a few centimetres on one side keeps the air moving.","Don't dry wet clothes under the shelter overnight.","If the fabric is streaming, wipe it with a cloth before it drips.","In the morning, turn the sleeping bag inside out and let it dry in the sun or air during breakfast.","Pack the wet tarp separately, in an outside bag, and dry it at the first sunny break."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"camp_pluie":{"titre":"Setting up camp in the rain","intro":"Arriving at the bivouac in a downpour is when everything can get wet. A method to keep the essentials dry.","etapes":["Pack your bag the day before in reverse order of setting up: tarp and cord at the top, sleeping bag right at the bottom in a dry bag.","Put the roof up first (see “Pitching your tarp in rain and wind”).","Under the roof, lay the groundsheet or a layer of leaves, then and only then the mat and sleeping bag.","Change immediately: take off wet clothes and put on the dry outfit kept for the night.","Keep the wet clothes for the next day, rolled in a bag: you'll put them back on to walk, and the dry outfit will stay dry.","Have a hot drink and eat: a cold, hungry body warms up badly."],"source":"Standard bivouac know-how and manuals FM 21-76 / FM 3-05.70 (public domain), written for Bivaska"},"peche_regles":{"titre":"Fishing: what the law says","intro":"Fishing without a licence in a public river is an offence. Before casting a line, a few checks are needed.","etapes":["Wallonia: a fishing licence is compulsory in public rivers, canals and lakes. It can only be bought online (permisdepeche.be) and is valid until 31 December. A 14-day “tourist” licence exists for visitors.","Some stretches also require a club card or the riverbank owner's permission.","Each species has its open seasons, minimum size and sometimes a daily quota: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche (maisondelapeche.be).","Species to release in Wallonia: eel, salmon, and all protected species (bullhead, noble crayfish…).","Forbidden: fishing in marked spawning areas, using whole animals (frogs, chicks, mice) as bait, or live bait not on the authorised list.","France: you need a fishing card from an approved association (AAPPMA), bought online or from a dealer. Other countries have similar rules.","Fish traps, nets, ledger lines left unattended or hand fishing are forbidden for amateur anglers in most cases."],"attention":["In real distress (lost for several days, without food), survival comes before the rules. But a healthy person lasts weeks without eating: water, warmth and signalling remain the priorities."],"source":"Wallonia's public fishing rules (SPW, Maison wallonne de la Pêche), INBO opinion on pollutants in freshwater fish, standard naturalist knowledge; written for Bivaska"},"poisson_pollution":{"titre":"Can you eat river fish?","intro":"Many rivers in Western Europe still contain pollutants that build up in fish: PCBs, dioxins, mercury, PFAS. Cooking doesn't destroy them.","etapes":["The most contaminated: eel (never eat it), large bottom-feeders (carp, catfish, barbel) and old large predators (pike, zander).","The least contaminated: young, small, lean fish, and trout from upstream brooks, far from towns and industry.","Avoid fishing to eat downstream of large towns, industrial areas, sewage works or in water with an abnormal smell or colour.","Pollutants lodge mainly in fat: remove the skin, the belly fat and the brown line along the sides, and don't eat the liver or roe.","Cook on a grill or in the oven so the fat drains away, rather than in a sauce or soup.","Limit yourself to a meal of wild fish now and then. Pregnant women and children should avoid river fish.","Never eat a fish found dead, nor a fish caught in an area where a ban on taking fish is posted."],"attention":["A Flemish study (INBO) concludes that a large share of freshwater fish in Flanders still exceeds consumption limits, and that anglers who eat their catch have high levels of pollutants in their blood."],"source":"Wallonia's public fishing rules (SPW, Maison wallonne de la Pêche), INBO opinion on pollutants in freshwater fish, standard naturalist knowledge; written for Bivaska"},"poisson_frais":{"titre":"Recognising healthy, fresh fish","intro":"Without a fridge, fish spoils within hours, even faster in hot weather. A few signs don't lie.","etapes":["Bulging, bright, clear eyes. Sunken, dull or milky eyes: fish too old.","Bright red, moist gills. Brown, grey or slimy: throw it away.","Firm flesh: a finger leaves no mark. Soft flesh that keeps the dent is no longer good.","A light smell of water or seaweed. A strong, sour or ammonia smell: throw it away.","Be wary of a sick fish: sores, swellings, frayed fins, abnormal swimming, strange colour.","Once caught, kill it straight away, gut it, and eat it within hours, or dry or smoke it (sheet “Preparing and cooking a fish”)."],"source":"Wallonia's public fishing rules (SPW, Maison wallonne de la Pêche), INBO opinion on pollutants in freshwater fish, standard naturalist knowledge; written for Bivaska"},"poisson_preparer":{"titre":"Preparing and cooking a fish","intro":"Killed properly, gutted straight away and cooked through: that's what makes a river fish safe and good.","etapes":["Kill quickly: a sharp blow on top of the head with a stick, just behind the eyes. It's more humane, and the flesh keeps better.","Bleed: cut the gills on one side and let it drain for a few minutes.","Scale: scrape with the back of the blade, from tail to head. Not needed for trout.","Gut: cut open the belly from the vent to the head, remove all the guts and scrape out the dark line along the backbone (the kidney). Never keep barbel roe.","Do this away from the water and the camp, and bury the remains: they attract animals.","Cook through: the flesh must become opaque and come away easily from the bone. On a spit, wrapped in leaves or on a hot stone (sheet “Cooking without utensils”).","To keep it longer: cut into thin strips and dry and smoke them for several hours over a gentle, smoky fire."],"attention":["Never eat freshwater fish raw, marinated or barely cooked: it can transmit parasitic worms (fish tapeworm, liver fluke). Only thorough cooking, or long freezing at -20 °C, destroys them."],"source":"Wallonia's public fishing rules (SPW, Maison wallonne de la Pêche), INBO opinion on pollutants in freshwater fish, standard naturalist knowledge; written for Bivaska"},"peche_survie":{"titre":"Fishing in a survival situation","intro":"In real distress, an improvised line can provide food. But fishing takes time and patience: it's never priority number one.","etapes":["Hook: a bent safety pin, a hawthorn or blackthorn spine tied onto a small stick, or a “gorge hook”: a small stick pointed at both ends, tied in the middle, which the fish swallows lengthwise.","Can-tab hook: cut the ring-pull of a can on one side of the ring, bend the cut part into a hook and sharpen the point on a stone. The other ring is for tying on the line.","Line: thin cord, dental floss, braided nettle fibres or the inner threads of paracord.","Bait: earthworms (under stones, logs, in damp soil), insect larvae under dead bark or river stones, grasshoppers. On site, the stomach of a first fish gives the ideal bait.","Where: in the eddies behind rocks, under undercut banks, at the edge of weed beds, near submerged wood, at the bottom of rapids.","When: at dawn and dusk, when fish feed. In overcast weather, they also bite during the day.","Stay discreet: approach slowly, without casting a shadow on the water, without vibrations on the bank.","Check the line often and never leave an abandoned line in the water: it kills fish and birds."],"attention":["Outside a real emergency, these methods are forbidden: see “Fishing: what the law says”.","Fishing often costs more energy than it brings in. Water, shelter, fire and signalling come first."],"source":"Wallonia's public fishing rules (SPW, Maison wallonne de la Pêche), INBO opinion on pollutants in freshwater fish, standard naturalist knowledge; written for Bivaska"},"piqure_vive":{"titre":"Weever or scorpionfish sting","intro":"An immediate, violent pain in the foot after walking in the sand at low tide: it's almost always a weever. The venom is destroyed by heat.","etapes":["Get out of the water and sit down calmly.","Immerse the stung area in very hot water, around 45 °C, for at least 15 to 30 minutes, or until the pain eases. Top up with hot water as you go.","Test the temperature with your unaffected hand or someone else's: the stung area is numb and could get burnt without you feeling it.","Without hot water: a hairdryer or a hot compress, at a safe distance from the skin.","Remove any visible bits of spine with tweezers, then clean and disinfect.","The pain usually eases within a few hours and disappears in one to two days."],"attention":["Don't cut, suck or urinate on it, and don't apply a tourniquet.","Feeling faint, severe dizziness, nausea, difficulty breathing or spreading swelling: see a doctor or call 112."],"source":"Wallonia's public fishing rules (SPW, Maison wallonne de la Pêche), INBO opinion on pollutants in freshwater fish, standard naturalist knowledge; written for Bivaska"},"blessure_poisson":{"titre":"Injuries from fish spines and teeth","intro":"Perch, zander or catfish spines, pike teeth: the small wounds made by a fish get infected easily, because they're deep and dirty.","etapes":["Let the wound bleed a little to help it clean itself.","Rinse for a long time with drinking water, then soap if possible.","Remove any visible piece of spine or tooth with tweezers.","Disinfect and cover with a clean dressing.","Very painful catfish sting: very hot water also helps (as for a weever).","Watch the wound for several days (sheet “Caring for a wound over several days”)."],"attention":["Spreading redness, heat, swelling, a red streak up the arm or fever: infection, see a doctor quickly.","Check that your tetanus vaccination is up to date."],"source":"Wallonia's public fishing rules (SPW, Maison wallonne de la Pêche), INBO opinion on pollutants in freshwater fish, standard naturalist knowledge; written for Bivaska"},"bache_montages":{"titre":"Ten ways to pitch a tarp","intro":"With a 3 × 3 m square tarp, a ridgeline and a few pegs, you can pitch about ten different shelters. The right one depends on the wind, the rain and the number of people.","etapes":["A-frame: the tarp draped over a low cord, both edges pegged to the ground. The classic against rain (dedicated sheet).","Closed A-frame with floor: the same, with the edges folded under you to make a floor. When the ground is wet (dedicated sheet).","Lean-to: one edge on the cord, the other pegged to the ground on the wind side. Open view, ideal in calm weather (dedicated sheet).","Envelope: a lean-to whose bottom is folded inwards to form a floor. Open on one side only.","Windbreak corner: a steeply angled lean-to with one side folded down to the ground facing the wind. Closed to the wind on two sides.","Plough point: a single corner tied high to a tree, the other three on the ground. Very good in wind for one person (dedicated sheet).","Arrowhead: one corner tied high, the tarp stretched in an A along a cord that runs down to the ground behind. High at the entrance, low at the feet, very stable.","Forest tent: one corner raised by a stick at the front, the two side corners pegged to the ground and the last one pulled out behind. A small, closed pyramid tent.","Flat roof: all four corners tied high to trees or sticks, one corner lower so water runs off. For cooking or resting out of the rain.","Walking poles: two poles set to 1.1 m hold up the front of the cord, the back is pegged to the ground. For camping where there are no trees."],"attention":["Always a slope on the roof: a tarp pitched flat makes a pocket of water that ends up coming through.","Practise at home: you don't discover a pitch in the rain and the dark."],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"sac_charger":{"titre":"Packing your backpack","intro":"A well-packed bag weighs the same, but feels much lighter: the centre of gravity stays close to your back and the bag no longer pulls you backwards.","etapes":["Right at the bottom: the sleeping bag, in a dry bag. You only need it at camp, and it cushions everything else.","In the centre, against your back, between the shoulder blades: the heaviest things (water, food, stove, cookset).","Around the heavy items: medium-weight things (spare clothes, tent or tarp), to wedge everything in.","At the top and towards the outside: light things and what you use during the day (rain jacket, fleece, first-aid kit).","In the lid and hip-belt pockets: map, compass, head torch, knife, snacks, phone, whistle. Everything that must come out without opening the bag.","Balance left and right, then tighten the compression straps so nothing moves.","Adjustment: the weight rests on your hips. Tighten the hip belt on your hip bones, then the shoulder straps, then the load lifters."],"attention":["Waterproof liner essential: a big bin bag inside the backpack keeps everything dry, even in a thunderstorm."],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"filtre_meche":{"titre":"Wick filter (water climbing a cloth)","intro":"A strip of cloth dipped in cloudy water, the other end hanging into a lower container: the water climbs through the fibres and drips out, much clearer. Spectacular, but be careful about what it really does.","etapes":["Put the container of dirty water higher than the clean container (on a stone).","Take a strip of clean cotton cloth, or a sock, thoroughly wet.","Dip one end to the bottom of the dirty water, run the cloth over the rim and let the other end hang into the clean container, lower than the dirty water.","Wait: the water slowly migrates through the fibres and drips. Allow several hours for half a litre.","Then boil the water you get for at least 1 minute (sheet “Making water safe to drink”)."],"attention":["This filter removes mud and particles, but NOT bacteria, viruses and parasites: clear water can make you very ill. Always boil it afterwards.","Very slow: useful for clearing muddy water before boiling it, not for drinking quickly."],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"nasse_bouteille":{"titre":"Bottle fish trap","intro":"A plastic bottle cut and turned inside out becomes a trap for small fish and crayfish: they get in through the neck and can't find the way out.","etapes":["Cut a large bottle a third of the way down, just below the shoulder.","Remove the cap, turn the top part upside down and push it into the body, neck facing inwards.","Fix the two parts together with string threaded through small holes, or with thorns.","Pierce a few small holes so water flows through and the bottle sinks.","Put bait inside (worms, crumbs, fish scraps) and a small stone to weigh it down.","Set it on the bottom, neck facing the current, tied to the bank with string. Check it every hour or two."],"attention":["A method forbidden to amateur anglers in Wallonia, France and most European countries. Keep it for a real emergency.","Release protected species immediately. American crayfish must never be transported alive."],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"ligne_ressort":{"titre":"Spring line (self-hooking)","intro":"A bent flexible branch acts as a spring: when a fish pulls on the bait, it releases a small trigger and the branch springs up, setting the hook.","etapes":["Choose a flexible dead branch or a young shoot already bent over the water. Don't cut anything living if you can avoid it.","Carve two pegs with a notch each: one is driven into the bank, the other (the trigger) hooks underneath it, notch against notch.","Tie the line to the end of the branch, bend the branch down and hook it onto the trigger.","Attach the line with the hook and bait to the trigger: when the fish pulls, the notches release and the branch springs up.","Set the depth of the bait, then move away and watch from a distance."],"attention":["A method forbidden to amateur anglers in Wallonia, France and most European countries. Keep it for a real emergency.","A branch that springs back can whip your face: never lean over it while setting it.","Never leave a set line unattended, and dismantle it when you leave."],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"ps_echarpe":{"titre":"Making an arm sling","intro":"For an injury to the hand, wrist, arm or shoulder: the sling supports the arm, eases the pain and often lets you keep walking.","etapes":["Take a square of cloth at least 1 m across (scarf, cut-up T-shirt, thin tarp) and fold it into a triangle.","Slide the triangle between the chest and the injured arm, the point towards the elbow on the injured side and one end going over the uninjured shoulder.","Gently bend the injured arm at a right angle across the chest, the wrist a little higher than the elbow.","Bring the other end of the triangle up over the forearm to the shoulder on the injured side.","Tie the two ends at the side of the neck, not on the back of the neck, with a little padding under the knot.","Close the point at the elbow with a pin or a knot, so the arm doesn't slip out.","For more immobilisation, add a wide band around the chest, over the arm."],"isole":["Without cloth: turn up the bottom of your jacket over the forearm and pin it, or slip your hand inside the zipped jacket.","A pack strap or a belt passed around the neck and the wrist makes a simple sling.","Check every hour that the fingers stay warm, pink and have feeling, and loosen if it swells.","With a good sling, you can often keep walking slowly: lighten the pack and carry it on the other side."],"alerte":["Fingers cold, pale, bluish or numb","Major deformity or bone visible","Pain increasing sharply despite immobilisation"],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"empreintes":{"titre":"Recognising European tracks","intro":"In the mud of a path or by a stream, tracks tell you who passes your camp at night. Here are the ones from our forests, not American ones.","etapes":["Wild boar: two large hoof toes, and two small marks (dewclaws) clearly visible behind. Often with the ground rooted up all around.","Roe deer: two slender, pointed toes in a heart shape, 4 to 5 cm. The dewclaws almost never show.","Red deer: like the roe deer but bigger and rounder, 7 to 9 cm.","Fox: four toes and a pad, in a narrow oval, with claws. The front toes stand apart: you can draw a cross between the pads. The trail runs in a straight line.","Dog: like the fox but rounder, toes more spread. The trail zigzags.","Wolf: like a big dog (over 10 cm), longer, in a straight line over long distances.","Badger: five toes in a row in front of a broad pad, with long claws. Like a small bear's paw.","Pine marten and beech marten: five small toes around a pad, prints often grouped in pairs (moving in bounds).","Hare: the two big hind feet land in front of the two small forefeet: the trail forms a Y.","Beaver and otter, by the water: five webbed toes. Large 15 cm hind foot for the beaver, round 6 cm print for the otter."],"attention":["Wolf: it avoids humans. Store your food properly, and report a sighting to the wolf network in your country (Réseau Loup in Wallonia).","Wild boar: never get between a sow and her piglets."],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"doigts_soleil":{"titre":"Estimating the time before sunset","intro":"Without a watch, your fingers are enough to tell how much time is left before dark, and so whether to set up camp now.","etapes":["Stretch your arm out in front of you, hand flat and horizontal, fingers together, little finger resting on the horizon.","Count how many fingers fit between the horizon and the bottom of the sun. Stack your other hand above if needed.","Count about 15 minutes per finger, roughly an hour per hand.","In Belgium and further north, the sun goes down at a slant: count rather 10 to 15 minutes per finger, especially in autumn and winter.","Keep a margin: it gets dark in the forest well before official sunset. Put up your shelter with at least an hour of daylight left."],"attention":["Never look straight at the sun: aim just below it, or through the trees."],"source":"Standard bivouac and survival know-how, written for Bivaska"},"rj_brochette_champi":{"titre":"Mushroom skewer","intro":"The very first dish you cook when setting out on an adventure: mushrooms threaded on a branch and browned over the embers.","etapes":["Cut a green hazel branch, bark removed at the end, and carve a point. Green wood doesn't burn.","Thread the whole mushrooms on by the stem, packed tightly so they don't spin.","Brush with oil or butter, add salt and the thyme.","Hold the skewer 10 cm above the embers, not in the flames, turning often: 6 to 8 minutes.","It's ready when they're golden and release a little juice."],"attention":["Shop-bought mushrooms only. Never cook a wild mushroom unless you're absolutely sure: some are deadly."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild","ingredients":["250 g of button mushrooms or oyster mushrooms","A knob of butter or a drizzle of oil","Salt","Pepper","Thyme"],"effet":"+morale","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_brochette_viande":{"titre":"Herb meat skewer","intro":"Pieces of meat and vegetables seared on a branch. The meal that gets you back on your feet after a long walk.","etapes":["At home: marinate the meat with the oil and herbs in a sealed box, kept cool.","At camp: thread meat, pepper and onion alternately onto a green branch carved to a point.","Cook over glowing red embers, 10 cm above, turning a quarter turn every 2 minutes.","Beef: 8 to 10 minutes. Chicken: 12 to 15 minutes, until cooked through.","Salt at the end, not before, so the meat stays tender."],"attention":["Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear.","Raw meat doesn't keep more than half a day out of the fridge: cook it on the first evening."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild","ingredients":["300 g of beef or chicken in 3 cm cubes","1 pepper","1 onion","Oil","Salt","Herbes de Provence"],"effet":"+vigour","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_pomme_miel":{"titre":"Honey baked apple","intro":"A melting apple that caramelises gently in the embers. The simplest bivouac dessert.","etapes":["Remove the apple's core with the tip of the knife, without piercing the bottom.","Fill the hole with honey, cinnamon and butter.","Wrap the apple in two layers of foil, the top well closed.","Put it in a hollow of hot ashes at the edge of the fire, not on glowing embers, and cover with ash: 20 to 25 minutes. It candies in its own juice.","It's ready when the tip of the knife goes in without resistance. Open carefully: the honey is scalding."],"attention":["Take the foil home with you: it doesn't burn and doesn't break down."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild and RPG healing potions","ingredients":["1 apple per person","1 spoonful of honey","A little cinnamon","A knob of butter","Aluminium foil"],"effet":"+warmth","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild and RPG healing potions. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_ragout_champi":{"titre":"Mushroom stew","intro":"A thick soup served in the mess tin, which warms you up in one go.","etapes":["Slice the onion and mushrooms on a board or the mess-tin lid.","Melt the butter in the mess tin over the flame, then soften the onion for 3 minutes.","Add the mushrooms and garlic, and cook for 5 minutes until they release their water.","Pour in 40 cl of water and the stock cube. Let it simmer for 10 minutes.","Off the heat, add the instant mash flakes to thicken and stir."],"attention":["Shop-bought mushrooms only. Never cook a wild mushroom unless you're absolutely sure: some are deadly."],"recette":{"jeu":"Minecraft","ingredients":["250 g of shop-bought mushrooms","1 onion","1 stock cube","2 spoonfuls of instant mash flakes","1 garlic clove","Butter"],"effet":"+satiety","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Minecraft. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_patate_braise":{"titre":"Ember-roasted potato","intro":"A potato put in the fire, opened steaming with a little butter.","etapes":["Wash the potato and prick it several times with the knife.","Wrap it in two layers of foil.","Make a space in the embers, put it in and cover it with embers.","Leave it 40 to 50 minutes, turning it once.","Test with the tip of the knife: it should go into the centre without forcing. Cut a cross on top, add butter, salt and cheese."],"attention":["Take it out with a stick or tongs: the foil stays scalding for a long time."],"recette":{"jeu":"Minecraft","ingredients":["1 large potato per person","Butter","Salt","Aluminium foil","Cheese if you have some"],"effet":"+satiety","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Minecraft. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_ragout_lapin":{"titre":"Rabbit stew","intro":"The big dish that simmers while you set up camp.","etapes":["Brown the legs in the oil at the bottom of the pot, 5 minutes on each side.","Add the sliced onion, then the carrots and potatoes in chunks.","Cover with water, add the stock cube, thyme and bay leaf.","Hang the pot from the pot hanger over small flames, and let it simmer for 45 minutes to 1 hour, lid on.","The meat should come off the bone by itself."],"attention":["Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear.","Don't hunt: buy the rabbit. Hunting requires a licence and is tightly regulated."],"recette":{"jeu":"Minecraft","ingredients":["2 shop-bought rabbit legs (or chicken)","2 carrots","2 potatoes","1 onion","1 stock cube","Thyme","Bay leaf","Oil"],"effet":"+long-lasting satiety","niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Minecraft. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_poisson_baton":{"titre":"Fish grilled on a stick","intro":"A whole trout planted in front of the fire, the skin crispy.","etapes":["Salt the inside and slip the thyme and a slice of lemon into the belly.","Thread the fish onto a green branch, from mouth to tail, running along the backbone.","Plant the branch in the ground, leaning towards the embers, 20 to 30 cm from the heat: the fish cooks by radiant heat, never in the flames.","Turn it after 8 to 10 minutes. Allow 15 to 20 minutes in all.","It's cooked when the flesh is white and opaque and comes away from the bone."],"attention":["See the sheet “Preparing and cooking a fish” to gut it properly."],"recette":{"jeu":"Minecraft, Far Cry and Red Dead Redemption 2","ingredients":["1 gutted trout per person (shop-bought, or caught with a licence)","Salt","Lemon","A sprig of thyme"],"effet":"+health","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Minecraft, Far Cry and Red Dead Redemption 2. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_steak_broche":{"titre":"Big spit-roasted steak","intro":"A big piece of meat turned on a spit over the fire. The whole art is stopping at the right moment.","etapes":["Plant two Y-shaped sticks on either side of the fire, 30 cm from the embers.","Push a straight green branch lengthwise through the meat, and rest it on the two Ys.","Brush with oil. Turn a quarter turn every 2 minutes.","Rare: 12 to 15 minutes. Medium: 18 to 20 minutes. Press the centre: springy means rare; firm means well done.","Let it rest 5 minutes on a clean stone before cutting, then salt it."],"attention":["Embers, not flames: a lively flame burns the outside and leaves the centre raw."],"recette":{"jeu":"Monster Hunter","ingredients":["A thick piece of beef (flank or sirloin, 3 to 4 cm)","Oil","Coarse salt","Pepper"],"effet":"+stamina","niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Monster Hunter. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_ragout_camp":{"titre":"Camp stew","intro":"The camp's big pot, to which everyone adds what they have. Meat, vegetables, and more water as long as there are people.","etapes":["Sear the meat in a little fat at the bottom of the pot, over a lively flame.","Add the onion, then the vegetables in big chunks and the paprika.","Cover with water, add the stock cube and thyme, and let it simmer on the pot hanger for 1 h 30, over a low fire.","Add the drained beans 10 minutes before the end.","Taste, adjust the salt, and serve with trapper's bread."],"attention":["Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear."],"recette":{"jeu":"Red Dead Redemption 2","ingredients":["400 g of stewing meat in cubes (beef, or shop-bought venison)","3 potatoes","2 carrots","1 onion","1 tin of beans","1 stock cube","Paprika","Thyme"],"effet":"+group morale","niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Red Dead Redemption 2. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_viande_herbes":{"titre":"Meat grilled with thyme and oregano","intro":"A slice of meat grilled on a hot stone, lifted with herbs.","etapes":["Heat a flat, dry stone at the edge of the fire for 20 minutes. Take it from outside the riverbed, see the warning.","Rub the meat with the herbs, salt and pepper.","Lay it on the scorching stone: it should sizzle straight away.","Turn it after 4 to 5 minutes, and allow the same on the other side. Longer for pork, which must be cooked through.","Let it rest 2 minutes before eating."],"attention":["Never use a stone taken from water or very damp: the water trapped inside can make it burst in the fire.","Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear."],"recette":{"jeu":"Red Dead Redemption 2","ingredients":["Pork chops or slices of beef","Salt","Pepper","Dried thyme and oregano (or fresh wild thyme, if you recognise it with certainty)"],"effet":"+vigour","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Red Dead Redemption 2. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_pommes_chou":{"titre":"Apple and cabbage stew","intro":"A sweet-and-sour winter stew, very Nordic, that fills you up and warms you.","etapes":["Melt the butter in the pot and brown the sliced onion.","Add the cabbage in thin strips and stir for 5 minutes until it shrinks.","Add the apples in quarters and the sausages in slices.","Pour in 30 cl of water with the stock cube, cover, and simmer for 25 minutes over a low fire.","The cabbage should be meltingly soft and the apples half fallen apart."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim","ingredients":["¼ of a white cabbage","2 apples","1 onion","2 smoked sausages","1 stock cube","Butter","Salt","Pepper"],"effet":"+warmth","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_roule_cannelle":{"titre":"Cinnamon roll on a stick","intro":"Dough rolled in a spiral around a stick, browned over the fire then rolled in cinnamon sugar. The evening treat.","etapes":["At camp, gradually add about 12 cl of water, or milk, and knead until you get a soft dough that no longer sticks.","Roll finger-thick sausages, 30 cm long.","Wind each sausage in a spiral around a peeled green branch as thick as your thumb.","Cook over the embers, turning constantly, 10 to 12 minutes, until golden and it sounds hollow.","Remove from the stick, roll in the cinnamon sugar, then drizzle with icing sugar mixed with a few drops of water to make a glaze."],"attention":["See also the sheet “Trapper's bread (bannock)”: same dough, savoury version."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim","ingredients":["200 g of flour","1 sachet of baking powder","1 pinch of salt","2 spoonfuls of sugar","Separately: sugar and cinnamon mixed","A little icing sugar"],"effet":"+morale","niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_soupe_carotte":{"titre":"Carrot soup","intro":"A sweet orange soup, easy and filling.","etapes":["Cut everything into small dice so it cooks quickly.","Soften the onion in the butter, then add carrots, potato and cumin.","Just cover with water with the stock cube, and boil gently for 20 to 25 minutes.","Mash the vegetables with a fork or the bottom of a mug, directly in the mess tin.","Add water if it's too thick."],"recette":{"jeu":"Valheim","ingredients":["4 carrots","1 potato","1 onion","1 stock cube","1 knob of butter","1 pinch of cumin"],"effet":"+health","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Valheim. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_saucisses":{"titre":"Campfire sausages","intro":"Sausages grilled on the end of a stick: the absolute campfire classic.","etapes":["Carve a green branch to a point for each person.","Spear the sausage lengthwise.","Hold it above the embers, not in the flames, and turn often so it cooks evenly.","Smoked sausage: 5 minutes. Fresh sausage: 12 to 15 minutes, it must be cooked through.","Slip it into the bread with mustard."],"attention":["Don't prick the skin too much: dripping fat sets the embers alight and blackens the sausage.","Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear."],"recette":{"jeu":"Valheim","ingredients":["Fresh or smoked sausages","Bread","Mustard"],"effet":"+morale","niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Valheim. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_iguane_baton":{"titre":"“Iguana” chicken skewer","intro":"The desert snack, Ardennes version: long strips of spiced chicken grilled on a stick. No iguana, promise.","etapes":["Cut the chicken into long, thin strips, lengthwise.","Rub with the oil, paprika, cumin and salt.","Thread each strip concertina-style, zigzagging, onto a green branch.","Grill over the embers 8 to 10 minutes, turning, until golden and cooked through.","Squeeze lime over it before eating."],"attention":["Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear."],"recette":{"jeu":"Fallout: New Vegas","ingredients":["2 chicken breasts","1 spoonful of smoked paprika","½ spoonful of cumin","Salt","Oil","1 lime"],"effet":"+stamina","niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Fallout: New Vegas. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_potion_soin":{"titre":"Healing potion: nettle and honey tea","intro":"The games' red potion, forest version: a hot tea of nettle, honey and lemon. It doesn't heal wounds, but it warms you up and lifts your spirits.","etapes":["Pick with a glove or a folded burdock leaf, away from paths, treated fields and places where dogs go.","Rinse the leaves in drinking water.","Boil 50 cl of water, take it off the heat and add the nettles.","Let it steep for 8 minutes, lid on, then strain.","Add the honey and lemon juice. The tea loses its sting while steeping."],"attention":["Only drink plants identified with certainty: several poisonous plants grow in the same places.","It's not a medicine: a real injury is treated with the “First aid” sheets."],"recette":{"jeu":"role-playing game healing potions","ingredients":["A handful of young nettle leaves (the tops of the stems)","Honey","Lemon","See the nettle species sheet"],"effet":"+warmth, +morale","niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by role-playing game healing potions. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_papillote_vigor":{"titre":"The Vigour parcel","intro":"Protein, salt for the effort, fat from the butter.","etapes":["The shield: spread out a large sheet of aluminium foil, doubled if it's thin.","The assembly: put the meat and mushrooms in the centre. Add the garlic, salt, pepper, and the butter on top.","The seal: fold the foil into an airtight parcel, the juice mustn't leak.","The fire: clear a bed of hot embers without flames, and put the parcel straight on it.","The timer: 15 to 20 minutes, turning once halfway with two sticks. Open carefully: the steam burns."],"attention":["Shop-bought mushrooms only. Never cook a wild mushroom unless you're absolutely sure: some are deadly.","With bacon, it must be well cooked and grilled, not just warm."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild","cuisson":"In the embers (foil parcel)","tags":[],"ingredients":["1 beef steak (or thick bacon) in large dice","4 button mushrooms, halved","1 small knob of butter (or a drizzle of oil)","Coarse salt, black pepper","1 crushed garlic clove"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_ragout_taverne":{"titre":"The tavern stew","intro":"Slow carbs, fibre and protein: lasting energy against the cold of the night.","etapes":["The opening: open the tin of beans completely.","The cutting: slice the smoked sausage into thick rounds, add it to the beans and stir.","The heating: put the mess tin (or the open tin) on a flat stone at the edge of the embers, and let it simmer until it bubbles gently.","The melting: take it off the fire with gloves or improvised tongs, and throw the cheese cubes in.","Serving: let it melt for 1 minute. With a spoon, when the cheese turns stringy."],"attention":["Never heat a closed tin: it explodes. An open tin can be heated at the edge of the embers, but its inner coating doesn't like a fierce fire: it's best to pour the contents into the mess tin.","Never use a stone taken from water or very damp: the water trapped inside can make it burst in the fire."],"recette":{"jeu":"World of Warcraft and Skyrim","cuisson":"On a hot stone, in the tin or mess tin","tags":[],"ingredients":["1 tin of beans in tomato sauce (baked beans)","1 smoked sausage (Montbéliard or kielbasa)","50 g of hard cheese (Comté, Beaufort) in cubes"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by World of Warcraft and Skyrim. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_grilled_cheese":{"titre":"The shelter grilled cheese","intro":"Very high in calories: fat to restart the body's boiler in cold weather.","etapes":["The sandwich: put the cheese and bacon between the two slices of bread.","The wrapping: wrap the sandwich firmly in foil.","The press: heat a flat, clean, dry stone at the edge of the fire. It must be very hot.","The cooking: put the sandwich on a nice flat bed of embers, then the hot stone on top, like a panini press.","The result: 3 to 5 minutes each side. The bread should be crispy and the cheese completely melted."],"attention":["Never use a stone taken from water or very damp: the water trapped inside can make it burst in the fire.","Handle the stone with two sticks or thick gloves."],"recette":{"jeu":"Fallout","cuisson":"Pressed under a hot stone","tags":[],"ingredients":["2 large slices of country bread","3 thick slices of melting cheese (raclette, cheddar or reblochon)","2 slices of bacon or cured ham"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Fallout. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_chaudron_ermite":{"titre":"The hermit's cauldron","intro":"Hydration, minerals and instant comfort.","etapes":["The searing: in the mess tin, sauté the mushrooms in the oil over small flames until golden.","The broth: crumble the stock cube over them and pour in a big glass of water (250 ml).","The reduction: let it boil for 5 minutes over the embers.","The binding: off the heat, add the cheese and stir hard until you get a creamy sauce.","The trick: pour it over a potato baked in the embers, or eat it as a thick soup with bread."],"attention":["Shop-bought mushrooms only. Never cook a wild mushroom unless you're absolutely sure: some are deadly."],"recette":{"jeu":"Genshin Impact","cuisson":"In the mess tin (hung or on a tripod)","tags":[],"ingredients":["1 handful of shop-bought mushrooms (fresh, or dried and rehydrated)","1 stock cube (chicken or vegetable)","2 portions of spreadable cheese (like Laughing Cow)","1 drizzle of oil"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Genshin Impact. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_pain_elmoth":{"titre":"Elmoth's travel bread","intro":"Compact carbs: keeps in the pack and gets eaten on the move the next day.","etapes":["The kneading: in the mess tin or the flour bag, add the salt, sugar, and the water little by little. Mix by hand until you get a firm dough that no longer sticks.","The shape: divide the dough and flatten it into thin flatbreads, 1 cm at most so they cook through.","The natural oven: brush the ash off a flat stone heated at the edge of the fire. It must be scorching.","The cooking: put the flatbreads straight onto the stone, without fat.","The timing: 5 to 8 minutes each side. The flatbread should sound hollow. Eat it hot, or wrap it in a cloth for the road."],"attention":["Never use a stone taken from water or very damp: the water trapped inside can make it burst in the fire."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Lord of the Rings and fantasy","cuisson":"On a flat stone (dry cooking)","tags":[],"ingredients":["100 g of wheat flour (weighed at home in a sealed bag)","1 pinch of salt","1 teaspoon of sugar or honey","Lukewarm water, by eye"],"niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Lord of the Rings and fantasy. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_cafe_cowboy":{"titre":"Cowboy coffee","intro":"Caffeine against tiredness, sugar and magnesium from the chocolate.","etapes":["The boil: bring the water to a full boil in the mess tin.","The brewing: take it off the heat, throw the ground coffee into the water and stir once.","The settling: let it stand 3 to 4 minutes without touching it, out of the wind. The grounds sink and settle at the bottom.","The secret: put the square of chocolate at the bottom of your mug.","Serving: pour very gently, without shaking the mess tin, to leave the grounds at the bottom. Stir to melt the chocolate."],"attention":["A drop of cold water poured onto the coffee helps the grounds sink. Don't drink the bottom."],"recette":{"jeu":"Red Dead Redemption","cuisson":"Settled in the mess tin","tags":[],"ingredients":["2 tablespoons of ground coffee (not instant)","300 ml of drinking water","1 square of dark chocolate (or a little sugar)"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Red Dead Redemption. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_onigiri":{"titre":"The samurai's rations (onigiri)","intro":"Slow carbs: a full stamina gauge before a long walk.","etapes":["The gentle fire: rice and water in the mess tin, lid closed with a stone on top. Bring to the boil over the embers, then move the tin back and leave for 15 minutes on very low heat, never opening it.","The rest: off the heat, let it steam for 10 minutes, still covered.","The shaping: let it cool slightly, wet your hands with salted water. Take a handful of rice, make a hollow and slip in a spoonful of flaked tuna.","The triangle: press hard between your hands to form a compact triangle. Wrap the base in seaweed to hold it without sticky fingers."],"attention":["Cooked rice and opened tuna don't keep: eat the onigiri the same day, and not after a hot day in the pack."],"recette":{"jeu":"Ghost of Tsushima and Sekiro","cuisson":"In the mess tin (compact rice)","tags":[],"ingredients":["100 g of short-grain rice (sushi rice), washed at home","150 ml of water","1 pinch of salt","1 small tin of tuna in water (or chopped cooked bacon)","1 sheet of nori seaweed (optional)"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Ghost of Tsushima and Sekiro. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_soupe_monstre":{"titre":"Seraphin's monster soup","intro":"Rehydration, collagen and minerals: the best defence bonus against frost.","etapes":["The searing: brown the meat at the bottom of the hot mess tin, with a little fat.","The deluge: just cover with water, and add the onion, carrot and crumbled stock cube.","The long cook: put the mess tin on a tripod or two stable stones, over medium embers. Let it simmer 1 h to 1 h 30 while you put up the shelter. The gentler and longer, the more tender the meat.","Tasting: serve piping hot in the mug. Add water if it reduces too much."],"attention":["Shop-bought onion: wild garlic and “wild onions” have deadly look-alikes (lily of the valley, autumn crocus). See the species sheets.","Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear."],"recette":{"jeu":"Monster Hunter and Dragon Quest","cuisson":"Long broth, by the fire","tags":[],"ingredients":["200 g of braising meat (beef, shop-bought game or pork shank)","1 large onion in quarters","1 carrot in thick rounds","1 beef stock cube","Water"],"niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Monster Hunter and Dragon Quest. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_galette_stalker":{"titre":"The Zone flatbread (survival blini)","intro":"Compact energy, bottom-of-the-pack ingredients that keep a long time.","etapes":["The mix: in the mess tin, mix the flakes and flour, then add the lukewarm water little by little until you get a thick dough, like modelling clay. Salt and pepper.","The flatbreads: form balls, then flatten them into thick flatbreads the size of your palm.","The fire test: heat the oil in the pan or the mess-tin lid, on the embers.","The frying: 3 to 4 minutes each side, until you get a golden, crispy crust.","Serving: hot, ideally with a piece of corned beef, for a real tunnel survivor's meal."],"attention":["Hot oil over the fire: keep the pan very stable, and never any water in it."],"recette":{"jeu":"S.T.A.L.K.E.R. and Metro 2033","cuisson":"In a bivouac pan or the mess-tin lid","tags":[],"ingredients":["1 sachet of instant mash flakes","1 tablespoon of flour","Lukewarm water","1 tablespoon of oil or lard","Salt, pepper"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by S.T.A.L.K.E.R. and Metro 2033. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_porridge":{"titre":"The pioneer's porridge","intro":"Low glycaemic index: steady energy for hours of walking in the cold.","etapes":["The soaking: in the cold mess tin, mix the oats, dried fruit and water.","The bubbling: over medium embers, gently bring to the boil, stirring constantly so the bottom doesn't stick.","The thickening: as soon as it boils and thickens (3 to 5 minutes), take it off the heat.","The finish: add the honey, cover for 2 minutes so the dried fruit plumps up. It warms you right from waking up."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Long Dark","cuisson":"In the mess tin (storm breakfast)","tags":[],"ingredients":["50 g of fine oat flakes","200 ml of water (or melted snow, boiled)","1 handful of dried fruit (raisins, cranberries, almonds)","1 teaspoon of honey or brown sugar"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Long Dark. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_lembas":{"titre":"The woodsmen's lembas","intro":"Dense carbs: the trappers' bread par excellence.","etapes":["The ration dough: in a freezer bag or the mess tin, mix flour, powdered milk and salt. Add a splash of water and knead until you get a soft, elastic ball.","The support: cut a green-wood branch (hazel or birch) the size of a broom handle, and strip the bark off the last 20 centimetres.","The winding: stretch the dough into a long, thin rope and wind it in a spiral around the stripped part, pressing the ends down firmly.","The cooking: above the embers, not in the flames, turning regularly.","The result: in 10 to 15 minutes, the bread puffs up, browns and comes away by itself. Fill the hole with a square of chocolate or cheese."],"attention":["No yew, cherry laurel or unknown wood: they're poisonous. Hazel, birch or willow."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Lord of the Rings and Green Hell","cuisson":"On a green-wood branch (direct fire)","tags":["No washing-up","Craft","Hiking"],"ingredients":["100 g of flour with a pinch of baking powder","1 tablespoon of powdered milk","1 pinch of salt","Water, by eye"],"niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Lord of the Rings and Green Hell. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_ragout_goule":{"titre":"The outback ghoul stew","intro":"Salt against cramps, protein that keeps a long time.","etapes":["The base: soften the diced onion in a little fat at the bottom of the mess tin, until translucent.","The meat: add the block of corned beef and mash it with the spoon.","The bubbling: pour in the water and let it simmer 5 minutes over the embers.","The thickener: crumble 3 or 4 crackers into it. They soak up the juice and give a thick, rustic mash.","Eating: piping hot. +50% energy gauge."],"recette":{"jeu":"Fallout and Project Zomboid","cuisson":"In the mess tin (emergency ration)","tags":["Ration tin","Rustic","Anti-cold"],"ingredients":["1 tin of corned beef, 200 g","1 small packet of salted crackers","½ onion (dried or fresh)","1 cup of water","A little oil or fat"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Fallout and Project Zomboid. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_jerky":{"titre":"The hunter's drying (campfire jerky)","intro":"Pure protein, almost fat-free: a snack that keeps in the pack.","etapes":["The salting: salt and spice the strips on all sides, and let them rest 30 minutes on a clean surface.","The safety sear: dip the strips in boiling water for 1 minute, or sear them quickly on the grill. The meat must be heated through before drying.","The drying rack: build a small green-wood tripod, or stretch cords 60 cm above dying embers.","The hanging: thread the strips onto green-wood skewers and hang them in the heat.","The smoking: no flames, just gentle heat and hardwood smoke (oak, beech), for 3 to 4 hours.","The result: the meat turns dark and dry, but stays supple. Store it in a clean, closed bag."],"attention":["Campfire drying isn't industrial preserving: eat this jerky within 2 to 3 days, and throw it away if it smells bad or turns damp.","Shop-bought beef only: wild game can carry parasites that drying doesn't kill."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Long Dark and ARK: Survival Evolved","cuisson":"Drying and smoking over the embers","tags":["Preserving","Craft","Wood fire"],"ingredients":["200 g of lean beef (topside) in 5 mm strips","Coarse salt, black pepper","Garlic powder or chilli (optional)"],"niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Long Dark and ARK: Survival Evolved. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_crispy_boite":{"titre":"The hidden-tin crumble","intro":"Fast sugars and potassium: morale and mental energy pick up at once.","etapes":["The venting: open the tin and pour off half the syrup (drink it, it's an energy shot).","The crumble: crumble the biscuits over the fruit, add the cinnamon.","The fire: put the open tin (or the mess tin) firmly on a nest of small embers.","The caramelising: let it bubble gently for 10 minutes. The syrup reduces and the biscuit forms a crust.","Serving: take it off with a tool or thick cloth, and let it cool for 2 minutes before eating with a spoon."],"attention":["Never heat a closed tin: it explodes. An open tin can be heated at the edge of the embers, but its inner coating doesn't like a fierce fire: it's best to pour the contents into the mess tin."],"recette":{"jeu":"DayZ and Escape from Tarkov","cuisson":"In the tin or mess tin, on the embers","tags":["Ration tin","No washing-up","Dessert"],"ingredients":["1 tin of fruit in syrup (pineapple, peaches or apricots)","2 or 3 dry biscuits (speculoos, butter biscuits)","1 pinch of cinnamon (optional)"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by DayZ and Escape from Tarkov. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_elixir_mana":{"titre":"The mana elixir (forest tea)","intro":"Vitamin C and warmth: real comfort when it's cold.","etapes":["The harvest: rinse the needles and chop them roughly with the knife to release the aromas.","The heating: bring the water to a full boil.","The steeping: take it off the heat, add the needles and ginger, and cover.","The rest: 10 to 12 minutes out of the wind. The liquid turns golden.","The strain: pour into the mug, holding back the needles, then add the honey."],"attention":["Yew is deadly and looks like a fir: flat, soft needles, dark green on top, no citrus smell, and red berries. See its species sheet. Also avoid thuja and ornamental conifers.","Not advised for pregnant women: some pine needles are not recommended during pregnancy."],"recette":{"jeu":"Don't Starve and Skyrim","cuisson":"Steeped in the mess tin (foraged)","tags":["Foraging","Hydration","Zero calories"],"ingredients":["1 handful of young pine or fir needles, identified with certainty","1 piece of ginger (brought along), or young bramble shoots","1 tablespoon of honey","500 ml of water"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Don't Starve and Skyrim. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_fondue_pelerin":{"titre":"The pilgrim's fondue","intro":"Fat and protein galore: the perfect dish for a night below zero.","etapes":["The aroma: rub the bottom and sides of the mess tin with the halved garlic clove, and leave it at the bottom.","The start: pour in the wine (or apple juice) and bring to a simmer over gentle embers.","The cascade: add the cheese in three goes, stirring constantly in figures of eight.","The smoothness: keep the heat very gentle. The cheese must melt without burning or splitting. Move the tin back if it gets too hot.","The action: spear the bread on the end of a wooden skewer and dip it in the tin, in the middle of the group."],"attention":["Cooked wine keeps a little alcohol: use the apple juice version for children."],"recette":{"jeu":"Steep and Horizon","cuisson":"In the mess tin (shared warmth)","tags":["High calorie","Rustic","Comfort"],"ingredients":["150 g of pressed cheese (Comté, Abondance or Emmental) grated or in cubes","50 ml of dry white wine (in a small flask), or tart apple juice","1 garlic clove","Stale bread croutons or crackers"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Steep and Horizon. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_galettes_ration":{"titre":"The vault hunters' ration cakes","intro":"Oats: slow energy, iron and fibre. Ultra-light to carry.","etapes":["The shelter mix: in the mess tin, mix the oats, powdered milk, salt and raisins.","The hydration: add the water spoon by spoon until you get a very compact, sticky dough.","The compacting: shape small, very flat, dense cakes the size of a bank card.","The heating: put them on a scorching flat stone, brushed and dry, or on the upturned mess-tin lid set on the embers.","The result: 6 to 8 minutes each side, until they're dry and rigid. Well dried, they keep about a week in a sealed bag."],"attention":["Never use a stone taken from water or very damp: the water trapped inside can make it burst in the fire."],"recette":{"jeu":"Borderlands and Fallout","cuisson":"On a stone or mess-tin lid (dry cooking)","tags":["Pack staple","Emergency","Craft"],"ingredients":["80 g of oat flour (or flakes crushed with the knife)","1 tablespoon of powdered milk","1 handful of raisins","1 pinch of salt","Water, by eye"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Borderlands and Fallout. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_bouillon_sainte":{"titre":"The Saint's vitality broth","intro":"Water, salt and minerals against dehydration, and warmth for the whole body.","etapes":["The blanching: bring the water to the boil and add the stock cube.","The sacrifice: plunge the nettles and herbs into the simmering broth. Cooking removes the nettle's sting in under a minute.","The reduction: let it simmer 10 minutes over the embers, until the leaves are very tender.","The crushing: off the heat, crush the leaves against the side with a spoon.","Serving: piping hot in your mug, before an effort or before sleeping."],"attention":["Only pick what you identify with certainty (see the species sheets), away from roads, treated fields and places where dogs go.","Sorrel: rich in oxalic acid, eat it in moderation, and avoid it if you have kidney stones."],"recette":{"jeu":"Elden Ring and Dark Souls","cuisson":"In the mess tin (herb broth)","tags":["Foraging","Hydration","Vegetarian"],"ingredients":["1 handful of nettle tops (picked with gloves or a bag)","1 handful of dandelion or wild sorrel leaves, identified with certainty","1 vegetable stock cube","400 ml of water"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Elden Ring and Dark Souls. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_brocheton_pirate":{"titre":"The pirate's skewer","intro":"Lean protein, and a morale bonus for the whole camp.","etapes":["The weapon: cut a long, thin green hazel branch. Strip the bark from the last 15 centimetres and carve a point.","The skewering: thread alternately a piece of fish or chicken, a petal of onion and a square of bacon.","The lock: leave a knot in the branch or cut a notch at the base so nothing slides off.","The cooking: hold the skewer 20 cm above the embers and turn it often: the bacon fat melts and coats the flesh.","The timing: 12 to 15 minutes. The onion should blacken at the edges, and the meat be cooked through."],"attention":["Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear.","Cod holds badly on a branch: use thick cubes, or chicken."],"recette":{"jeu":"Sea of Thieves and Assassin's Creed IV: Black Flag","cuisson":"On a green-wood branch (direct fire)","tags":["No washing-up","Rustic"],"ingredients":["1 fillet of firm white fish (cod, pollock) or 1 chicken breast, in large cubes","½ onion in wide petals","1 slice of smoked bacon in squares","Salt, pepper"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Sea of Thieves and Assassin's Creed IV: Black Flag. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_pemmican":{"titre":"Great plains pemmican","intro":"Maximum energy density: protein, fat and berries in a small block.","etapes":["The powder: at home, crush or blend the dried meat into a coarse powder, like sawdust.","The binder: add the crushed berries.","The blend: pour in the melted fat, warm but not hot, then knead until you get a very dense, even paste.","The mould: shape small balls or spread the paste out to cut into bars.","Storage: let it set somewhere cool, then wrap it in baking paper. Dry and cool, it keeps several weeks."],"attention":["Use shop-bought, thoroughly dry dried meat: pemmican only keeps if everything is perfectly dry. Throw it away if it smells rancid or turns damp.","Very fatty and very filling: a few mouthfuls are enough."],"recette":{"jeu":"RimWorld and The Oregon Trail","cuisson":"No cooking in the field (prepared at home)","tags":["Emergency","Preserving","High calorie"],"ingredients":["50 g of shop-bought dried meat (beef jerky), ground to a powder","50 g of lard, beef tallow or clarified butter (ghee), melted","30 g of dried berries (cranberries, blueberries), crushed"],"niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by RimWorld and The Oregon Trail. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_pochee_espace":{"titre":"The space ration pouch","intro":"A hot meal in 5 minutes, with almost no washing-up.","etapes":["The crushing: without opening the noodle packet, crush it between your hands to break the noodles into small pieces.","The loading: pour the noodles, the dried soup and the drained fish into your mug or mess tin.","The reactor: cover with boiling water, a centimetre above the food.","The containment: close with the lid and wedge it between two stones, or in a hat, to keep the heat in for 5 minutes.","The extraction: stir and eat, with chopsticks carved from dead wood if you have no spoon."],"attention":["Don't pour boiling water into the noodle packet: it isn't made for that, it warps and can burn your fingers. Only freeze-dried meal pouches are designed for boiling water."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Outer Worlds and Starfield","cuisson":"With boiling water, lid closed","tags":["No washing-up","Ration tin","Quick"],"ingredients":["1 packet of instant noodles (ramen type)","1 sachet of dried soup (vegetable or miso)","1 small tin of tuna or crab meat","Boiling water"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Outer Worlds and Starfield. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_etouffee_trappeur":{"titre":"The trapper's braise","intro":"Melt-in-the-mouth meat cooked in its own steam, easy to digest.","etapes":["The mattress: spread out a double layer of foil.","The bed: put the onion and garlic in the centre.","The coating: coat the meat with mustard, lay it on the onions, salt and pepper.","The braise: close an airtight dome-shaped parcel, leaving a little air for the steam.","The burial: dig a hollow in the hot ashes at the foot of the fire, put the parcel in and cover with ash and a few embers. 25 to 30 minutes without touching it.","The check: open it and cut the biggest piece. If it's still pink, close it and put it back for 10 minutes."],"attention":["Poultry and pork must be cooked through: cut the biggest piece, the flesh must no longer be pink and the juices must run clear."],"recette":{"jeu":"World of Warcraft","cuisson":"In a foil parcel under the ash","tags":["Slow","Comfort"],"ingredients":["1 chicken breast (or shop-bought rabbit fillet) in large strips","½ onion, sliced","1 garlic clove, sliced","1 tablespoon of strong mustard (in a mini tube)","Salt, pepper","Aluminium foil"],"niveau":"Level 3 · Master trapper","niv":3},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by World of Warcraft. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_kari_sud":{"titre":"The South Sea curry","intro":"Spices warm you up and mask the taste of purified water.","etapes":["The spicy base: pour the sardine oil into the hot mess tin and toast the curry for 30 seconds over the embers.","The liquid: add the coconut milk and mix well. Bring to a simmer.","The plunge: add the whole sardines and the rice (or the crackers to thicken).","The simmering: 5 to 7 minutes over a very gentle heat, at the edge of the fire.","Serving: an adventurer's dish, rich and fragrant, in the middle of the cold forest."],"attention":["Curry burns quickly: 30 seconds is enough, otherwise it turns bitter."],"recette":{"jeu":"Uncharted and Far Cry","cuisson":"In the mess tin (spicy one-pot)","tags":["Spicy","Anti-cold","Rustic"],"ingredients":["1 small carton of coconut milk (200 ml)","1 teaspoon of curry powder (or curry paste)","1 tin of sardines in oil","1 handful of pre-cooked rice (or crumbled crackers)"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Uncharted and Far Cry. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_biscuits_abri":{"titre":"The shelter's safety biscuits","intro":"Concentrated carbs to replace bread gone soft with damp.","etapes":["The dough: mix flour, salt, bicarbonate and fat in the mess tin. Add the water little by little until you get a firm but pliable dough.","The shaping: form walnut-sized balls and flatten them with your thumb into small, thick discs.","The setup: line the bottom of the cold mess tin with foil and put the biscuits on it.","The steam: close the lid tightly, with a heavy stone on top, and put the mess tin on very gentle embers.","The cooking: 15 minutes covered. They rise a little and harden without burning. Delicious dipped in a broth."],"attention":["No foil? Fresh hazel or lime leaves will do, only if you recognise them with certainty."],"recette":{"jeu":"Fallout","cuisson":"In the mess tin (steamed under the lid)","tags":["Pack staple","Craft","Vegetarian"],"ingredients":["80 g of wheat flour","2 tablespoons of water, more if needed","1 pinch of bicarbonate of soda (or baking powder)","1 pinch of salt","1 small knob of lard or oil"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by Fallout. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_bouillie_necrophage":{"titre":"The necrophage porridge","intro":"A quick recharge after a long walk or a big climb.","etapes":["The heating: heat the salted water until it starts to bubble.","The plunge: pour in the flakes all at once and stir straight away to avoid lumps.","The fusion: after 2 minutes, when the porridge thickens, add the pieces of bar.","The mixing: take it off the heat and stir until the chocolate and caramel marble the porridge.","The effect: a luxury dessert that recharges your vigour gauge."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Witcher","cuisson":"In the mess tin (makeshift mix)","tags":["Ration tin","Quick","Dessert"],"ingredients":["40 g of fine oat flakes","1 chocolate bar (Snickers type) or cereal bar, in pieces","200 ml of water (or reconstituted milk)","1 pinch of salt"],"niveau":"Level 1 · Novice","niv":1},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Witcher. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"rj_crumble_balmora":{"titre":"The Balmora dunes crumble","intro":"Fruit, nuts and sugar: warm comfort before the night.","etapes":["The compote: in the bottom of your stainless-steel mug, put the diced fruit, honey and butter.","The reduction: put the mug on a hot stone or a grill above gentle embers. 10 minutes, stirring, until the fruit is translucent and caramelised.","The crumbling: in a bag or between your hands, crush the biscuits and nuts into coarse crumbs.","The lid: sprinkle these crumbs over the hot fruit, without stirring.","The finishing touch: leave another 3 minutes at the edge of the fire so the top turns crispy. With a teaspoon, under the shelter."],"attention":["A titanium or stainless-steel mug can go on the fire. Not a plastic or double-walled cup: it melts or can warp."],"recette":{"jeu":"The Elder Scrolls III: Morrowind","cuisson":"In the mug or mess tin (indirect fire)","tags":["Craft","Dessert","Comfort"],"ingredients":["1 apple or 1 pear in small dice","3 crackers or dry biscuits","1 handful of mixed nuts (almonds, hazelnuts, walnuts)","1 tablespoon of honey or sugar","1 small knob of butter"],"niveau":"Level 2 · Apprentice","niv":2},"source":"Original Bivaska recipe, inspired by The Elder Scrolls III: Morrowind. Unofficial: Bivaska is not affiliated with or endorsed by any publisher of the games mentioned"},"isolation_sol":{"titre":"Ground insulation: understanding the R-value","intro":"At night, it's the ground that steals most of your heat, not the air: your weight crushes the sleeping bag's insulation underneath you, so it no longer insulates. The mat is what insulates you from the ground, and its R-value says how much.","etapes":["The R-value measures the mat's resistance to heat flow. The higher it is, the better you're insulated from the cold ground.","Since 2020, the major brands measure the R-value with the same method (ASTM F3340 standard). These values can be compared. The old “in-house” values can't.","R-values add up: an R 2 foam pad under an R 4 inflatable gives R 6. Put the foam underneath: it protects the inflatable from thorns and still insulates you if it punctures.","Guide for the night temperature: above 10 °C, R 2 is enough; 5 to 10 °C, aim for R 3; 0 to 5 °C, R 3.5 to 4; −5 to 0 °C, R 4.5; −10 to −5 °C, R 5.5; below that, R 6 and more.","Add about 1 if you feel the cold, and 1 more on wet ground or snow.","The sleeping bag completes it: its “comfort” temperature is where a person who feels the cold sleeps well, its “limit” temperature is where a warm sleeper sleeps curled up without feeling cold. Below the limit, there's a risk of hypothermia.","Without a good enough mat: a thick bed of very dry dead leaves or bracken (30 cm before compressing), conifer branches, your empty pack and your rope under your legs. Less effective than a real mat, but much better than nothing.","Use Bivaska's calculator (Learn > Field tools > R-value): it adds up your gear and compares it with the night forecast at your bivouac spot."],"attention":["An inflatable mat without inner insulation (about R 1) is freezing as soon as the night drops below 10 °C, even with a good bag.","A survival blanket laid on the ground hardly insulates from the cold ground at all: it blocks moisture, not conduction.","A wet sleeping bag loses much of its warmth, especially down: keep it dry in a dry bag."],"source":"ASTM F3340 standard (measuring the thermal resistance of mats) and EN/ISO 23537 standards (sleeping bags). Written for Bivaska."},"crise_72h":{"titre":"Being ready for 72 hours","intro":"In a major crisis (general power cut, flood, storm, accident), the emergency services can't help everyone at once. The European Union asks every household to be able to manage on its own for at least 72 hours.","etapes":["Get informed: sign up to your country's alert system and find the official sources (Bivaska > Ready 72 h > Country).","Make a plan with your household: who to call, where to meet if separated, what to do about children at school and vulnerable people.","Build a stock to stay at home for 72 hours: water (3 L per person per day), food that can be eaten without cooking, light, radio, medicines, cash.","Separately, prepare a light evacuation bag, ready to grab if you're told to leave: papers, medicines, water, charger, clothes.","Build up your stock little by little, with each shop: no need to buy everything at once or expensive gear.","Rotate: use what is nearing its date and replace it. Bivaska reminds you of dates in the Stock tab.","Think of elderly, ill or lonely neighbours: in a crisis, mutual help saves lives."],"attention":["Keep the stock somewhere easy to reach, known to the whole household.","A kit forgotten at the back of the cellar is useless: check it twice a year, for example when the clocks change."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_panne":{"titre":"General power cut","intro":"A long outage cuts the heating, the fridge, sometimes water, the mobile network and payments. The greatest danger often comes from makeshift solutions.","etapes":["Unplug sensitive appliances (computer, TV) to avoid damage when the power comes back. Leave one lamp switched on so you know when it returns.","Keep the fridge and freezer closed: a full freezer stays cold for about 48 hours, half full for about 24 hours.","Listen to the battery radio for instructions. Save your phone: power-saving mode, low brightness, aeroplane mode when you don't need it.","If the cold sets in: gather in a single room, close the doors, pile on layers, blankets and a hat.","If a water cut is announced, fill bottles and the bath (for the toilet and hygiene, not for drinking).","Check on vulnerable neighbours and people who depend on a medical device."],"attention":["Never a barbecue, camping stove, brazier or generator indoors or in a garage: carbon monoxide kills without a smell. Headaches, nausea, drowsiness in several people at once: get into fresh air and call 112.","Candles: never unattended. Prefer torches."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_inondation":{"titre":"Flood","intro":"Water sometimes rises very fast, and the current carries away more than you think. The golden rule: never go into moving water.","etapes":["As soon as there's an alert: move valuables, papers and medicines upstairs; keep your evacuation bag within reach.","If water threatens the house and it's safe to do so, turn off the electricity and gas.","If you're told to evacuate, leave at once by the route indicated. Tell your loved ones.","If you're trapped, go upstairs (not into an attic with no way out) and signal: torch, sheet at the window, whistle, 112.","Afterwards: only go back when the authorities allow it, don't switch anything back on before it's checked, throw away food that touched the water, and follow the instructions about tap water.","Take photos of the damage for the insurance before cleaning up."],"attention":["15 cm of moving water is enough to knock an adult over, 30 cm to carry away a car. Don't walk or drive in a flooded area.","Never go down into a cellar or underground car park during a flood."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_tempete":{"titre":"Storm and violent winds","intro":"During a storm, most injuries are caused by falling trees, branches and objects blown by the wind.","etapes":["Before: bring in or tie down anything that can blow away (garden furniture, bins, trampoline), close shutters and windows.","Stay indoors, away from windows. Postpone journeys.","Don't go into the forest or under trees, and don't bivouac: that's where the danger is greatest, even after the peak of the wind.","Afterwards: never touch a fallen power line, move away and call 112.","Report trees fallen on the road and help neighbours if it's safe."],"attention":["After a storm, broken branches can stay hanging in the trees and fall several days later."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_canicule":{"titre":"Heatwave","intro":"Heat kills every summer in Europe, especially elderly people, babies, the sick and those who work outdoors.","etapes":["Drink regularly without waiting to be thirsty. Avoid alcohol.","Close shutters and windows during the day, ventilate at night when it's cooler.","Cool down: showers, damp cloths, the coolest room, air-conditioned places.","Avoid effort during the hot hours. Check on lonely or elderly people at least once a day."],"attention":["Never leave a child, a vulnerable person or an animal in a car, even for a few minutes."],"alerte":["Hot, dry skin, confusion, feeling faint, very high temperature: this is heatstroke, a life-threatening emergency. Call 112 and cool the person while you wait."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_abri":{"titre":"Nuclear or chemical accident: sheltering in place","intro":"In a toxic or radioactive cloud, the safest thing is almost always to stay inside a closed building. The authorities tell you what to do through alerts, radio and TV.","etapes":["Go into the nearest building and stay there.","Close doors and windows, switch off ventilation and air conditioning.","Listen to the radio or the alert messages, and follow the instructions exactly.","Don't go and fetch the children from school: the school will shelter them.","Avoid making calls to leave the network free for the emergency services: send your loved ones an SMS instead.","Iodine tablets: only when the authorities ask. They only protect the thyroid against radioactive iodine, mainly in children, young people and pregnant or breastfeeding women. In Belgium, they're available from pharmacies (information on risquenucleaire.be)."],"attention":["Don't go out “to have a look”: stay sheltered until the authorities announce the end of the alert."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_evacuer":{"titre":"Evacuating: leaving quickly and well","intro":"If the authorities ask you to evacuate, every minute counts. Having your bag ready and a meeting point makes all the difference.","etapes":["Take your evacuation bag, your medicines, your papers and your pets.","If you're asked to and it's safe: turn off the gas, water and electricity, and lock the house.","Follow the route given by the authorities, even if it seems longer.","Tell your loved ones where you're going, and register at the reception centre if you're sent there.","If the household is separated, go to the meeting point set in your family plan."],"attention":["Don't go back for belongings until access is allowed."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_sansreseau":{"titre":"No network and no card payments","intro":"A power cut or a cyberattack can cut phone, internet and payments all at once. What's on paper and in your pocket becomes precious.","etapes":["Keep cash in small notes and coins.","Keep on paper: important numbers, your family plan and the address of the meeting point.","Listen to the battery radio: it's the channel planned for when everything else fails.","SMS often get through when calls don't. Keep messages short.","112 keeps priority on the networks: in an emergency, try anyway, even without credit or a SIM card.","Bivaska works without a network: saved map, sheets, SOS, your plan and your stock."],"attention":["Recharge your power banks and your phone as soon as the power comes back, in case of another cut."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the European Commission (72 h self-sufficiency, 2026), the Belgian Crisis Centre and civil protection services. In a crisis, the authorities' instructions take precedence over this sheet."},"crise_eau_logement":{"titre":"The hidden water in your home","intro":"If tap water is cut off or declared unsafe, your home often holds dozens of litres of usable water. You just need to know where, and which to avoid.","etapes":["Hot water tank (often 100 to 200 litres): first turn off its electricity or gas, close the cold water inlet, open a hot tap in the house to let air in, then drain from the bottom tap into a clean container. Let it cool: risk of scalding.","Pipes: close the main stopcock, open the highest tap in the house, then collect the water at the lowest tap.","Toilet cistern (not the bowl), if there's no tablet or product in it: make it safe before drinking it.","Freezer ice cubes, liquid from tinned vegetables and fruit: drinkable.","Make safe any water you're not sure about: boil for at least one minute, or disinfect (see “Making water safe to drink”).","Don't ration water: drink what you need today and look for more tomorrow. Keep pool or bath water for hygiene and the toilet."],"attention":["Never water from radiators or central heating: it contains chemicals (antifreeze, anti-corrosion).","Don't switch an empty hot water tank back on: the element burns out. Refill it before restoring the power.","Pool, waterbed or aquarium water: never to be drunk, even filtered."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"crise_couper":{"titre":"Turning off gas, water and electricity","intro":"In a flood, an evacuation, an earthquake or if you smell gas, knowing how to shut off the home's supplies prevents fires, electrocution and damage. Find them now, not on the day.","etapes":["Electricity: switch off the main switch on the fuse box (often near the meter). Feet dry, never if water is touching the fuse box.","Water: close the main stopcock, usually near the water meter, in the cellar, garage or under the sink.","Gas: close the valve at the meter, often a quarter turn. Handle perpendicular to the pipe = closed.","Once the gas is off, don't turn it back on yourself: call the network operator or a professional, who will check the installation.","Walk round once with the whole household, put a label on each valve and note where they are in your family plan (Ready 72 h > Plan)."],"attention":["Only turn off the gas if there's a leak, an instruction from the authorities or an evacuation: putting it back into service requires a professional."],"alerte":["Smell of gas: don't switch anything on or off (no lamp, switch or doorbell), don't use your phone indoors, open the windows, get out, and call the gas operator's number or 112 from outside."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"crise_calfeutrer":{"titre":"Sealing a room","intro":"In a toxic cloud, fire smoke or radioactive fallout, the authorities may ask you to stay indoors. A well-sealed room greatly reduces what you breathe.","etapes":["Choose an inside room with as few windows as possible, big enough for the whole household.","Chemical: prefer an upper floor, as many toxic gases are heavier than air. Radioactivity: prefer the centre of the building or the cellar.","Close doors and windows, switch off ventilation, air conditioning, the cooker hood and warm-air heating.","Tape around windows and doors, and put plastic over air vents. Slip a damp cloth under the door.","Take with you: water, battery radio, torch, phone and charger, medicines, something to sit on.","Listen to the radio and wait for the authorities to announce the end of the alert before opening up and airing the room."],"attention":["A sealed room is only meant for a few hours: follow the authorities' instructions on duration.","Don't use a candle, stove or combustion heater in a closed room."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"crise_troc":{"titre":"Bartering, rationing and discretion","intro":"If payments and shops stop for several days, what you have and what you can do become your currency. Rationing too hard and drawing attention are the two mistakes to avoid.","etapes":["Never ration water. For food, a healthy adult can last a long time on about half their usual ration, if they drink enough.","Children, pregnant or breastfeeding women, the sick and the elderly keep their full ration.","Eat first what goes off: fridge, then freezer, then reserves.","What trades well: water and purification tablets, batteries, lighters and matches, common medicines and plasters, coffee, sugar, salt, soap, toilet paper, tools and skills (repairing, treating, cooking).","Your skills are often worth more than your things: offer a service rather than showing your stock.","Discretion: don't talk about your reserves on social media, don't show everything you have, keep lighting discreet at night.","Mutual help: organise with a few trusted neighbours (childcare, shared cooking, taking turns to get news)."],"attention":["Prescription medicines aren't for trading: a treatment taken without medical advice can be dangerous.","Tell the authorities about isolated or dependent people you know."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"crise_radio":{"titre":"Radio: staying informed and communicating without a network","intro":"When the internet and mobile network go down, radio remains the channel the authorities plan to use to inform people. Walkie-talkies let you stay in touch over a few kilometres.","etapes":["Stay informed: listen to your country's public radio on a battery or wind-up radio (Belgium: RTBF, VRT; France: franceinfo and local stations; Luxembourg, Netherlands, Switzerland: the national public radio; UK: BBC local radio). Find their FM frequencies at home today.","PMR446 walkie-talkies: 16 licence-free channels in Europe (446.00625 to 446.19375 MHz), 0.5 W. Real range: a few hundred metres in town, 1 to 3 km in open country, much more from one hilltop to another.","Agree in advance on a channel and a sub-code within the household and neighbourhood, and on listening times (for example 5 minutes on the hour) to save batteries.","CB (27 MHz, licence-free): channel 9 is often used as an emergency calling channel and 19 by truckers, with no guarantee that anyone is listening.","Keep it short: who you are, where you are, what you need. Repeat important information twice.","Calculate the maximum range between two points with the “Radio range” tool (Learn > Field tools)."],"attention":["No PMR or CB channel is monitored by the emergency services: in an emergency, always try 112 first.","Marine VHF (channel 16, 156.800 MHz) is reserved for boats and requires a licence, except for a genuine distress call.","Most long-wave stations (RTBF, France Inter) no longer broadcast: use FM or digital radio (DAB+)."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"crise_nord_soleil":{"titre":"Finding north with the sun and the time","intro":"Without a compass or GPS, the sun gives north to within a few degrees, provided you know the time. Bivaska's “Find north” tool does the calculation for you, even without a network.","etapes":["Open Learn > Field tools > Find north: Bivaska calculates where the sun is now, for your region and the phone's time.","Face the sun: the tool tells you how far to turn to face north.","Without a phone, in Belgium and France: the sun is due south between 12:30 and 1:30 pm in winter, between 1:30 and 2:30 pm in summer (later the further west you are). At that moment, your shadow points north.","The sun only rises due east and sets due west at the equinoxes: in winter it rises in the south-east; in summer, in the north-east.","Check with a second method when you can: stick and shadow, the North Star at night."],"attention":["Never look straight at the sun, even briefly."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"ps_rcp":{"titre":"Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)","intro":"A person who doesn't respond and isn't breathing normally is in cardiac arrest. Each minute without compressions cuts the chance of survival by about 10%: don't wait to be sure, start.","etapes":["Check there's no danger to you. Gently shake the shoulders and speak loudly: do they respond?","If they don't respond: gently tilt the head back, lift the chin, and look for normal breathing for no more than 10 seconds. Rare, noisy breaths (gasps) don't count.","Not breathing normally: call 112 (speaker on), ask someone to fetch a defibrillator (AED).","Hands one on top of the other in the centre of the chest, arms straight: push down 5 to 6 cm, at a rate of 100 to 120 per minute, letting the chest come back up.","If you're trained: 30 compressions then 2 rescue breaths. Otherwise, compressions only, without stopping.","As soon as a defibrillator arrives, switch it on and follow its voice. Keep going until the emergency services take over, the person wakes up, or you're exhausted."],"attention":["A few hours' training (Red Cross, civil protection) changes everything: in the field, you don't learn these skills from an app."],"isole":["Alone, with no help reachable: CPR alone rarely saves without a defibrillator. Keep going anyway as long as you can, swapping with another person every 2 minutes if there are several of you.","If the person starts breathing normally again: recovery position and constant monitoring."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"crise_stock_long":{"titre":"A stock that lasts for years","intro":"Some dry foods keep for years, sometimes decades, if they stay cool, dry, out of the light and well sealed. They're the basis of a reserve you don't have to keep renewing.","etapes":["Keep almost indefinitely, well sealed and dry: salt, white sugar, honey (it can crystallise: that's normal, warm it gently), white vinegar and cider vinegar.","Up to 30 years, but only in long-term packaging (bucket or sealed pouch with an oxygen absorber): white rice, oat flakes, whole wheat and maize grains.","Several years, well sealed: dry pasta, lentils, dried beans and peas (the older they get, the longer they take to cook), cornflour, bicarbonate of soda, instant coffee, skimmed milk powder (quality drops over time), dried vegetables.","Shorter: oils and fats (they go rancid: renew them every 1 to 2 years), whole spices (2 to 4 years to keep their flavour), brown rice and wholemeal flour (a few months: their fat goes rancid).","The four rules: cool, dry, dark, well sealed. An opened packet or ordinary shop packaging lasts much less than the durations above.","Write the purchase date on each packet, put the oldest at the front and eat them first: the reserve renews itself. Dates are tracked in Ready 72 h > Stock."],"attention":["No honey for a baby under one year old (risk of infant botulism).","Throw away anything that smells rancid, has taken on moisture, is mouldy or has insects in it.","Also plan water and a way to cook: rice, pasta and pulses need a lot."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the USDA (United States Department of Agriculture) and Utah State University Extension on long-term food storage."},"crise_lampe_huile":{"titre":"A cooking-oil lamp","intro":"During a power cut, a jar, a cotton wick and cooking oil give a steady light for several hours. Vegetable oil burns poorly without a wick: it doesn't ignite easily, which makes this lamp safer than an improvised paraffin lamp.","etapes":["Take a thick glass jar (preserving jar type) with a metal lid.","Drill a 6 to 10 mm hole in the centre of the lid. Ideally, fix a wick holder or a large washer on it to hold the wick upright.","Use a pure cotton wick (cotton cord or string, never synthetic: synthetics melt). Thread it through the hole; it must reach the bottom.","Fill the jar two-thirds with cooking oil (sunflower, rapeseed, olive…), screw on the lid.","Let the wick soak for a few minutes, then light it. It should stick out about 1 cm: longer and the flame smokes; shorter and it goes out.","A thin wick lasts longer, a thick wick gives more light. Trim the charred tip now and then."],"attention":["Never leave it burning unattended, or while you sleep.","Put it on a stable, heat-resistant surface, away from curtains, fabric and children.","Ventilate the room: any flame uses oxygen and produces a little soot.","To put it out, smother the flame with a lid. Never pour water on burning oil.","The lid and the top of the jar get hot: let them cool before touching or refilling."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of civil protection and crisis services (EU, Belgium, France, FEMA). In a crisis, follow the authorities' instructions."},"crise_toilettes":{"titre":"Toilets and hygiene without running water","intro":"When water or sewers stop working, dirty hands and badly managed excrement make you ill faster than hunger. A few simple rules prevent diarrhoea and infections.","etapes":["Dry toilet: a bucket with a lid (or the toilet bowl emptied of water), a strong bin bag inside, and a material to cover after each use: sawdust, cold wood ash, cat litter, dry soil.","Two-bucket method if possible: one for urine (much less dirty, it can be diluted and poured on uncultivated ground, away from wells), one for faeces. Separating them greatly reduces smells.","Close the bag when it's a third full, double it, and store it outside in a closed bin, in the shade, until collections resume.","If the sewers overflow or the authorities ask, stop flushing: dirty water could come back up into your home or your neighbours'.","Hands: wash them after the toilet and before eating or cooking, even with very little water. A container with a tap or a pierced bottle over a basin, a little soap: 20 seconds of rubbing. Hand gel helps if hands aren't visibly dirty.","Body wash: a flannel and a basin of lukewarm water are enough; start with the face, finish with the feet and intimate areas. Change underwear as often as possible.","Periods: keep a reserve of disposable products for several cycles. A menstrual cup or washable pads mean you won't run out; wash them in cold then boiling water if possible.","Washing-up: scrape first, then wash in a basin of hot soapy water and rinse in disinfected or boiled water. Let it air-dry."],"attention":["Diarrhoea in several members of the household: step up handwashing even more and give the sick person their own toilet if possible.","Never dump human waste into a stream or near a well."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on official recommendations (WHO, Red Cross, civil protection, USDA, CDC). If in doubt about someone's health, call 112."},"crise_rehydratation":{"titre":"Diarrhoea and dehydration: the home-made solution","intro":"In a crisis, doubtful water and difficult hygiene cause diarrhoea. The real danger is dehydration, especially in babies, children and elderly people. A rehydration solution can be made with water, sugar and salt.","etapes":["The best option: oral rehydration salts (ORS) sachets from the pharmacy. Put some in your kit, especially if you have young children.","Without sachets, the WHO recipe: 1 litre of drinking water (boiled then cooled), 6 level teaspoons of sugar and half a level teaspoon of salt. Stir until completely dissolved.","Measure carefully: too much salt is dangerous, especially for a child. Taste it: the solution should be no saltier than tears.","Give it in small sips, often, even if there's vomiting (one spoonful every 1 to 2 minutes). An adult drinks at least a glass after each liquid stool.","Keep eating simple foods as soon as possible (rice, pasta, banana, bread). A breastfed baby keeps feeding.","Make a fresh solution every day and throw away what's left after 24 hours."],"attention":["In any of these warning cases: call 112 or a doctor. The home-made solution helps you hold on, it doesn't treat the cause."],"alerte":["Baby under 6 months with diarrhoea or vomiting.","No wee for 6 to 8 hours, very dry mouth, sunken eyes, crying without tears, sunken fontanelle in a baby.","Unusual drowsiness, confusion, difficulty waking the person.","Blood in the stools, high fever, vomiting that prevents drinking anything at all.","Diarrhoea lasting more than 2 to 3 days in an adult, more than 24 hours in a young child."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on official recommendations (WHO, Red Cross, civil protection, USDA, CDC). If in doubt about someone's health, call 112."},"crise_fievre":{"titre":"Fever and minor ailments without a doctor","intro":"During a crisis, surgeries may be closed or overwhelmed. Knowing how to manage a fever or a common ailment, and above all recognising what can't wait, avoids burdening emergency services needlessly and waiting too long when it's serious.","etapes":["Fever: from 38 °C. It helps the body defend itself; it's treated mainly for comfort.","Uncover the person (no thick blankets), keep the room at a mild temperature, give plenty to drink, often.","Medicine: paracetamol is generally the first choice. Follow the leaflet exactly for the dose, the interval between doses and the daily maximum; for a child, the dose depends on their weight: follow the dosing on the box or the syringe.","Never give aspirin to a child or teenager without medical advice.","Cold, sore throat: rest, hot drinks, honey (not before one year old), nose rinses with salt water. They clear up on their own in about a week.","Small wounds: clean with clean water and soap, disinfect, cover with a clean dressing and change it every day. Watch for infection: spreading redness, heat, pus, fever.","Write down the time and dose of every medicine given: in a crisis, you forget quickly."],"attention":["These signs can't wait: call 112, even if the services are overloaded."],"alerte":["Baby under 3 months with 38 °C or more.","Purplish spots on the skin that don't fade when you press a glass on them.","Stiff neck, violent headache, confusion, seizures.","Difficulty breathing, bluish lips, chest pain.","Fever lasting more than 3 days, or above 40 °C that doesn't come down with treatment."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on official recommendations (WHO, Red Cross, civil protection, USDA, CDC). If in doubt about someone's health, call 112."},"crise_traitements":{"titre":"Daily treatments: not running out","intro":"Insulin, heart or blood-pressure medicines, epilepsy, asthma, thyroid, contraception… A sudden interruption can be more dangerous than the crisis itself. It needs preparing beforehand.","etapes":["Make a complete list of your treatments: name, dose, timing, prescribing doctor. Keep it in your kit, on paper, and in “My emergency info” (SOS window).","Ask your doctor and pharmacist how to keep a small supply in advance (one or two weeks). Never stockpile without their advice.","Rotate this supply: use the oldest boxes first, watch the expiry dates (Ready 72 h > Stock).","Insulin and medicines to keep cool: unopened vials go in the fridge; the one in use generally tolerates room temperature for a period stated on the leaflet. In a power cut, keep them in a cool box or insulated bag, never in direct contact with ice, never frozen.","Spare glasses, hearing aids and batteries, measuring equipment (glucose meter, blood-pressure monitor) and their consumables are part of the treatment.","If you depend on an electrical device (oxygen, ventilator, home dialysis): register with your electricity supplier, your medical provider and your local council, and plan with them what happens in a power cut."],"attention":["Never take someone else's treatment, and don't stop a treatment on your own: some (steroids, anti-epileptics, heart medicines) are dangerous to stop suddenly.","If you've missed a dose or are running out, call a pharmacist: in a crisis, duty pharmacies remain the best resource."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on official recommendations (WHO, Red Cross, civil protection, USDA, CDC). If in doubt about someone's health, call 112."},"crise_fragiles":{"titre":"Children, elderly and dependent people","intro":"A crisis weighs much more heavily on the youngest and most vulnerable. They need a suitable kit, and above all someone who thinks of them.","etapes":["Babies: infant formula for several days and bottled water to prepare it, bottles, nappies, wipes, cream, spare clothes, thermometer, rehydration sachets, paracetamol suited to their weight.","Children: their cuddly toy, a game or a book, snacks they like, a family photo with your number written on the back in their pocket.","Explain the situation in simple, calm words, without lying or dramatising. Children copy adults' calm.","Keep landmarks: mealtimes, bedtime, little rituals. Give them a role (watching the lamp, counting the bottles): feeling useful is reassuring.","Elderly people: treatments, glasses, hearing aids, walking aids, continence pads, a blanket. They feel thirst less and dehydrate quickly: get them to drink regularly.","They cope badly with heat and cold: in a heatwave, a cool place and water; in winter, several layers and a heated room.","Isolated people in your building or street: go and knock. Many victims of heatwaves and severe cold lived alone."],"attention":["Add everyone's needs in Ready 72 h > Kit (number of babies, children, adults): the list adapts."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on official recommendations (WHO, Red Cross, civil protection, USDA, CDC). If in doubt about someone's health, call 112."},"crise_animaux":{"titre":"Pets in a crisis","intro":"Pets are part of the household. Not having planned for them pushes people to stay in a dangerous house or go back into it. Better to organise their kit in advance.","etapes":["Food for at least 3 days, ideally a week, in a waterproof box, and their bowls.","Water: allow about 50 to 60 ml per kilo per day for a dog or cat, on top of the household's water.","Lead, collar or harness, carrier for cats and small animals, litter and bags.","Their medicines, their vaccination record, and a recent photo of you with the animal (to prove it's yours if it gets lost).","Check that its microchip is registered with your current phone number.","Find out in advance: not all emergency reception centres accept animals. Find a relative, a boarding kennel or a vet who could look after it.","If you have to evacuate, take it with you if possible. If you really must leave it, don't tie it up: it must be able to escape water or fire. Leave plenty of water and food, and a note on the door."],"attention":["A stressed animal may bite or run away: keep it on a lead or in a carrier during an evacuation, even if it's usually calm."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on official recommendations (WHO, Red Cross, civil protection, USDA, CDC). If in doubt about someone's health, call 112."},"crise_moral":{"titre":"Keeping your spirits up when it lasts","intro":"Fear, anger, tiredness, insomnia, difficulty concentrating: these are normal reactions to an abnormal situation. They usually pass with time, but a few habits help you hold on and help others.","etapes":["Breathe: breathe in through your nose counting to 4, breathe out slowly through your mouth counting to 6. Repeat for one to two minutes: your heart slows and your thoughts clear.","Keep a routine: times for getting up, meals, small tasks, bed. It gives you a grip on a situation you don't control.","Act on what you can: a list of three useful things for the day, and tick them off. Acting pushes back anxiety.","Sleep as much as possible, even in short bursts. An exhausted mind makes bad decisions.","Get information from official sources two or three times a day, then switch off. Rumours and a flood of horror images wear you out.","Stay connected: talk with your loved ones and neighbours, offer your help. Helping others is one of the best remedies against helplessness.","Avoid alcohol and sleeping pills taken without medical advice: they make anxiety and sleep worse.","Alone for a long time: talk out loud, keep a diary (Bivaska's logbook works for that too), set yourself small goals."],"attention":["In these cases, talk about it: to someone close, to a doctor, or to a helpline (Belgium: Télé-Accueil on 107; France: 3114; elsewhere: your national crisis or suicide prevention line). In immediate danger: 112."],"alerte":["Thoughts of dying or harming yourself.","No longer able to eat, sleep or look after yourself for several days.","Nightmares, startle reactions and anxiety that don't ease after a few weeks."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on official recommendations (WHO, Red Cross, civil protection, USDA, CDC). If in doubt about someone's health, call 112."},"crise_electricite":{"titre":"Long outage: electricity and the freezer","intro":"An outage of several days raises two questions: how to recharge the essentials (phone, lamps, radio) and what to do with the food in the fridge and freezer. Plus a deadly danger: carbon monoxide.","etapes":["Recharging: power banks charged in advance, a small solar panel with battery, wind-up torches and radio. Keep the phone in power-saving mode and switch off what you don't need.","The car can charge a phone, engine running, only outdoors, never in a garage even with the door open.","Generator: only in the open air, at least 6 metres from the house, exhaust pointing away from doors and windows. Never in a garage, a cellar, a garden shed or under a canopy.","Never plug a generator directly into a socket in the house: it can feed power back into the grid and kill a technician. Use suitable extension leads, or have a changeover switch installed by an electrician.","Fridge and freezer: keep the doors closed. A full freezer stays cold for about 48 hours, half full for about 24 hours. A closed fridge holds for about 4 hours.","After the outage: freezer food that still contains ice crystals, or has stayed at 4 °C or below, can be refrozen or cooked. Anything else from the fridge (meat, fish, milk, ready meals) that stayed above 4 °C for more than 2 hours goes in the bin.","Tip: a thermometer in the fridge and freezer, and frozen water bottles that fill the space and act as ice packs (then drinking water)."],"attention":["Carbon monoxide is invisible and odourless. Headaches, nausea, dizziness, drowsiness in several people at once: get into the open air immediately and call 112.","Never a barbecue, brazier or combustion space heater in a closed room, a tent or a garage.","If in doubt about a food, throw it away: food poisoning in the middle of a crisis is a real danger."],"source":"Written for Bivaska based on the recommendations of the USDA (food safety during power outages) and the CDC (generators and carbon monoxide)."},"fab_charbon":{"titre":"Making charcoal","intro":"Charcoal is wood heated away from air: it loses its water and gases and becomes almost pure carbon. It burns hot, without flame or smoke, and has many uses besides fire.","etapes":["Small quantity (the simplest method): fill a metal tin with a lid (biscuit tin, mess tin) with finger-sized pieces of dry, sound wood, without bark. Preferably hardwoods: beech, oak, hornbeam, ash.","Pierce a single small 2 to 3 mm hole in the lid, close the tin and put it in the middle of good embers.","Smoke, then sometimes a small flame, comes out of the hole: these are the wood gases. Let it be.","When nothing more comes out of the hole (often 20 to 40 minutes), take the tin out of the fire with a stick and plug the hole with earth or a small stone.","Let it cool COMPLETELY, lid closed, for at least an hour. If you open it too early, the air relights the charcoal.","Large quantity: the charcoal burners' clamp or pit (stacked wood, covered with earth, a few days of watching). It's a real craft: keep it for your own land, away from trees, with permission.","What it's for: cooking fuel that burns hot without smoke; char cloth for lighting a fire; a layer of crushed charcoal in a water filter (it improves taste and smell, but doesn't make water safe: always boil it); resin glue (see the sheet); drawing and marking; face camouflage.","Teeth: without toothpaste, rubbing your teeth with a chewed twig is enough. Powdered charcoal doesn't do better: it's abrasive and can wear enamel, and dentists advise against using it regularly."],"attention":["Home-made charcoal is NOT medical “activated” charcoal: it's useless against poisoning. In case of poisoning, call 112 or the poison centre (in Belgium: 070 245 245). Pharmacy activated charcoal is only taken on their advice.","A bag of warm charcoal can reignite by itself hours later: only store it completely cold, in a metal container.","Never painted, treated, varnished or chipboard wood: it gives off toxic fumes.","A charcoal fire or brazier gives off a lot of odourless carbon monoxide: never in a tent, a car or a closed room."],"source":"Written for Bivaska from traditional know-how, checked against current safety knowledge."},"fab_colle":{"titre":"Resin glue (pine pitch)","intro":"With pine or spruce resin, a little charcoal and wax, you get a hard, waterproof glue that you warm up to use: repairing a handle, gluing a point, filling a crack, waterproofing a seam.","etapes":["Collect resin that has already oozed and hardened on wounds in pine or spruce trunks. Don't wound a living tree to get it.","Melt it gently in an old tin can, set at the edge of the embers, not in the flames: resin is highly flammable.","Remove bits of bark and debris with a stick (or strain it through a cloth).","Add charcoal ground to a fine powder: about one part charcoal to three or four parts resin. It makes the glue stronger and less brittle.","To make it less brittle in the cold, add a little beeswax or fat (about one part in ten).","Mix well, then wind the warm glue onto the end of a small stick: you have a “glue stick”.","To glue: warm the stick near the embers, spread the glue on both pieces, join them and hold for a few seconds. Binding over the top makes the joint much stronger."],"attention":["Hot resin sticks to skin and burns like wax: handle it with a stick, never with your fingers.","If it catches fire, smother it with a lid or earth, never with water.","To clean your hands: a little oil or fat, then soap."],"source":"Written for Bivaska from traditional know-how, checked against current safety knowledge."},"fab_cendres":{"titre":"Ash water: washing without soap","intro":"Wood ash mixed with water gives “lye”: an alkaline water that removes grease. Our grandparents washed laundry, greasy dishes and floors this way. It's also the basis for making soap.","etapes":["Use white or grey ash from hardwood (beech, oak, ash), completely cold and sieved. Never ash from barbecue charcoal with firelighters, painted or treated wood, printed paper or plastic.","Put about one volume of ash for five to ten volumes of water in a bucket (not aluminium: lye attacks it).","Boil it or let it soak for a day, stirring now and then, then let it settle: the ash sinks to the bottom.","Gently pour off the clear water from the top, strained through a cloth. It feels a little slippery between the fingers: that's normal.","For laundry: soak it in this lukewarm water, scrub, then rinse in clear water. For greasy dishes: scrub with a little damp ash and rinse well."],"attention":["This water is caustic: it irritates the skin and can seriously damage the eyes. Wear gloves if you have them, and rinse immediately with plenty of water if splashed. In the eye: rinse for 15 minutes and get medical advice.","Never drink it and keep it out of children's reach, in a clearly labelled container.","It damages wool and silk: keep it for cotton and linen."],"source":"Written for Bivaska from traditional know-how, checked against current safety knowledge."},"fab_savon":{"titre":"Ash and fat soap","intro":"Soap comes from a reaction between a fat and an alkali. With very concentrated ash water and animal fat or oil, you get a soft, brown, rustic soap, but one that cleans. It's a slow and somewhat dangerous process: do it calmly, not in an emergency.","etapes":["Prepare a very concentrated ash water (see the sheet “Ash water”): pour the same water several times over fresh ash, or reduce it by boiling it for a long time.","Traditional strength test: a fresh egg should float at the surface with only a small part of its shell showing. If it sinks, the lye is too weak.","Prepare the fat: beef or mutton tallow, lard, or vegetable oil. Melt it, strain it to remove any bits.","In a large cast-iron or stainless-steel pot (never aluminium), slowly pour the lye into the hot fat, about two volumes of lye for one volume of fat, stirring constantly with a long stick.","Cook over a low fire, stirring, for one to several hours, until the mixture thickens like a cream and no longer separates into layers.","Pour into a mould (wooden box lined with cloth) and let it harden in the air for at least several weeks: the soap gets milder as it ages.","Before using it on your skin, test it on a small area of your arm: if it stings or reddens, it still contains too much alkali. Then keep it for laundry."],"attention":["Concentrated lye is very caustic: gloves, long sleeves and eye protection (glasses) are essential, children and animals kept away.","Never lean over the pot: splashes burn skin and eyes. In the eye: rinse for 15 minutes with clean water and call 112.","Home-made soap is unpredictable: without scales or pure lye, you can't guarantee it's gentle on the skin. Don't use it on a wound or on a baby."],"source":"Written for Bivaska from traditional know-how, checked against current safety knowledge."},"fab_saponaire":{"titre":"Washing with a plant: soapwort","intro":"Soapwort (Saponaria officinalis) contains saponins, substances that foam in water and clean gently. It's nature's simplest soap, with nothing dangerous to handle.","etapes":["Recognising it: a plant 30 to 80 cm tall, on path edges, banks and riversides. Upright stems, opposite leaves in an elongated oval with 3 clearly visible veins. In summer, clusters of pale pink five-petalled flowers, fragrant in the evening.","Pick a handful of leaves and stems (the root is even richer, but leave the plant in place).","Crush or cut them, then rub them vigorously in a little water between your hands: a light foam appears.","For more foam: boil a handful of the chopped plant in a litre of water for 15 to 20 minutes, let it cool and strain. This liquid washes hands, body and delicate laundry.","Rinse with clear water, away from streams."],"attention":["Don't drink or eat it: saponins irritate the digestive tract.","Keep it away from your eyes: it stings.","If you're not sure of the identification, don't use it: if in doubt, ash or plain water are safer.","Even “natural”, foam pollutes a stream: wash at least 60 metres from water."],"source":"Written for Bivaska from traditional know-how, checked against current safety knowledge."},"fab_bougie":{"titre":"Tallow or fat candle","intro":"Before paraffin, people lit their homes with tallow, melted animal fat. With fat from cooking and a bit of cotton, you can make a candle or a small lamp that gives light for several hours.","etapes":["Collect fat: beef or mutton tallow (the hardest), lard, or cooled cooking fat. Melt it gently and strain it through a cloth to remove bits of meat.","Wick: a strand of pure cotton (string, shoelace, twisted strip of cotton T-shirt). First dip it in the melted fat and let it harden: it will stand upright.","Candle: fix the wick to the bottom of a small metal pot or thick glass jar (a clean tin can, a jar), keep it upright with two twigs laid across the rim, then pour in the melted fat.","Let it set somewhere cool for several hours before lighting. Trim the wick to about 1 cm.","Quick lamp: a shell or small container filled with fat, a wick lying in it with its end sticking out over the rim."],"attention":["Tallow smokes a little and smells of cooking: air the room.","Never leave it burning unattended, or while you sleep, and put it on a surface that can't burn.","A candle doesn't heat a room: it gives light. To keep warm, wrap up; no improvised fire in a closed room.","The smell of fat attracts animals: on a bivouac, store leftovers away from the tent."],"source":"Written for Bivaska from traditional know-how, checked against current safety knowledge."}},"especes":{"chataignier":{"nom":"Sweet chestnut","statut":"Edible cooked","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"A nourishing fruit, rich in starch. Its look-alike, the horse chestnut, is poisonous.","identification":["Husk bristling with fine, long, very dense spines (like a hedgehog)","2 to 3 nuts per husk, flattened on one side, with a small hairy tip","Simple, long leaves (15–25 cm), edged with pointed teeth"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Horse chestnut (Aesculus hippocastanum)","latin":"Aesculus hippocastanum","niveau":"danger","diff":"Husk with a few short, widely spaced spikes, a single round, smooth nut without a tip, fan-shaped leaves with 5–7 leaflets."}],"usages":["Roasted on the embers (score the shell first so it doesn't burst) or boiled 20–30 min.","Raw, it is astringent and hard to digest."]},"hetre":{"nom":"Beechnut (beech)","statut":"Small amounts, roasted","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"Beech dominates many European forests. Its small triangular seeds can be eaten in moderation.","identification":["Smooth grey bark, straight trunk","Oval leaves with a wavy edge, slightly hairy at the margin","Four-valved husk containing 2 shiny three-sided seeds"],"usages":["Shell then roast: cooking reduces fagin, an irritant in large amounts.","Eat in small amounts (a handful)."]},"noisetier":{"nom":"Hazel","statut":"Edible","saison":"Sep. → Oct. (end of season)","resume":"Hazelnuts can still be found on the ground in early November, often already emptied by animals.","identification":["Multi-stemmed shrub, shiny light-brown bark","Rounded, double-toothed leaves, rough to the touch","Nut in a fringed green husk"],"usages":["Raw or roasted. A hazelnut with a small hole is empty (larva or rodent).","Preferably gather from the ground away from paths (animal droppings)."]},"eglantier":{"nom":"Dog rose (rose hip)","statut":"Edible, needs preparation","saison":"Oct. → Dec.","resume":"The fruit of the wild rose. Best after the first frosts, when it softens.","identification":["Thorny shrub with arching stems, hooked prickles","Oval, smooth, orange-red fruit, 1.5–2 cm, with the remains of the calyx at the tip","Compound leaves of 5 to 7 toothed leaflets"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Poisonous red berries (yew, bittersweet, holly, honeysuckle)","niveau":"danger","diff":"None has the oval shape of the rose hip with the dried calyx at the tip, nor the prickly stems. If in doubt, don't eat."}],"usages":["Open the fruit and remove the seeds and inner hairs: they irritate the mouth and digestive tract.","The pulp can be eaten raw when fully ripe, or as a tea (strain the hairs out carefully)."]},"prunellier":{"nom":"Blackthorn (sloe)","statut":"Edible, needs preparation","saison":"Oct. → Dec.","resume":"Small, very sour blue-black plums, softened by frost. Don't crunch the stones.","identification":["Very thorny shrub (long, hard spines on the twigs)","Round blue-black fruits, 1 cm, covered with a waxy bloom","Grows in hedges and forest edges"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Privet (Ligustrum vulgare)","latin":"Ligustrum vulgare","niveau":"danger","diff":"Shiny black berries in clusters, without a bluish bloom, on a shrub without thorns."}],"usages":["Pick after a frost (or put them in the freezer): the astringency decreases.","Don't crunch or swallow the stones in quantity (cyanogenic compounds)."]},"aubepine":{"nom":"Hawthorn (haw)","statut":"Edible pulp","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"Small, mealy red berries, very common at forest edges.","identification":["Thorny shrub, leaves deeply cut into 3 to 7 lobes","Oval red berries, 1 cm, in clusters, a single hard stone"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Poisonous red berries (yew, bittersweet, holly)","niveau":"danger","diff":"Check the hawthorn's lobed leaves and thorns. No lobed leaves = no hawthorn."}],"usages":["Pulp raw or cooked, mealy, slightly sweet taste.","Spit out the stone."]},"sorbier":{"nom":"Rowan","statut":"Edible cooked only","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"Clusters of bright orange berries. Raw, they irritate the stomach: always cook them.","identification":["Small tree, compound leaves of 11 to 15 toothed leaflets","Wide clusters of small orange-red berries (about 1 cm)"],"usages":["Cook (boil) before eating: cooking destroys the irritant parasorbic acid.","Bitter taste: best as a compote or mixed with other fruit."]},"gland":{"nom":"Acorn (oak)","statut":"After leaching","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"High in calories and plentiful, but full of tannins: inedible without long preparation.","identification":["Leaves with rounded lobes","Smooth brown nut in a scaly cup"],"usages":["Shell, crush, then soak, changing the water several times (or boil, renewing the water) until the bitterness has gone.","A long survival technique: rarely useful on a short bivouac."]},"ortie":{"nom":"Stinging nettle","statut":"Edible cooked","saison":"All year round (young shoots)","resume":"Very nutritious. The stinging hairs disappear with cooking.","identification":["Square stem covered with stinging hairs","Opposite leaves, elongated heart-shaped, edged with large teeth"],"sosies":[{"nom":"White dead-nettle","latin":"Lamium album","niveau":"ok","diff":"Looks like nettle but doesn't sting. Also edible."}],"usages":["Pick the top leaves with gloves or by pinching firmly.","In soup or blanched 1–2 min."]},"pissenlit":{"nom":"Dandelion","statut":"Edible","saison":"All year round","resume":"Rosette of toothed leaves: leaves and roots are edible.","identification":["Rosette at ground level, deeply toothed leaves","White latex when you break the leaf or the flower stalk","A single yellow flower per hollow stalk"],"usages":["Young leaves in salad (bitter in winter), roasted root.","Wash anything growing at ground level thoroughly (see Fox)."]},"oenanthe":{"nom":"Hemlock water-dropwort","statut":"Deadly","saison":"All year round","resume":"One of the most poisonous plants in Europe. Grows by water: beware along rivers and ditches.","identification":["Large umbellifer of banks and wet ditches","Roots in fleshy spindles, sap that turns yellow in the air","White flowers in umbels"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Wild carrot, parsnip, wild celery","latin":"Daucus carota","niveau":"danger","diff":"Simple rule: never eat a wild umbellifer (umbrella-shaped flowers) unless you're an expert. Several are deadly."}],"usages":["Avoid touching it with bare hands if possible, eat nothing."]},"if":{"nom":"Yew","statut":"Deadly","saison":"All year round","resume":"A conifer with cup-shaped red berries. Seed and foliage are highly poisonous.","identification":["Flat, dark green needles, without a resinous smell","Open, cup-shaped red “berry” with a dark seed in the centre"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Don't use its wood to skewer food."]},"digitale":{"nom":"Foxglove","statut":"Highly poisonous","saison":"Rosettes in winter","resume":"Common in forest clearings on acid soil. The whole plant is poisonous to the heart.","identification":["In winter: rosette of large, oval, soft and downy leaves, marked veins","In summer: spike of purple bell-shaped flowers"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Comfrey, great mullein (rosettes)","niveau":"caution","diff":"The rosettes look alike: don't eat any downy rosette not identified with certainty."}],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"douceamere":{"nom":"Bittersweet (woody nightshade)","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"Berries into winter","resume":"A climber of damp hedges and banks with shiny red berries, attractive and poisonous.","identification":["Climbing stem, often by water","Oval, bright red, shiny berries in hanging clusters"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"phalloide":{"nom":"Death cap","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Jul. → Nov.","resume":"Responsible for most fatal poisonings. Symptoms come late, when the liver is already affected.","identification":["Olive-green to yellowish cap, sometimes white, smooth","White gills, ring on the stem","White sac-like volva at the base (often buried: clear around the stem)"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Field mushroom, green brittlegills, knights","niveau":"danger","diff":"NEVER eat a mushroom identified from a photo or an app. Have it checked by a mycologist or a mycological society."}],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"tuemouches":{"nom":"Fly agaric","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"Aug. → Nov.","resume":"The red mushroom with white spots from fairy tales. Common under birch and spruce.","identification":["Bright red to orange cap, covered with white flakes (which rain can wash off)","White gills and stem, ring, bulbous base"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Caesar's mushroom (absent from Belgium)","latin":"Amanita caesarea","niveau":"caution","diff":"Yellow gills and stem. Don't try to tell them apart yourself."}],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"galere":{"nom":"Funeral bell","statut":"Deadly","saison":"All year round, especially in autumn","resume":"A small brown mushroom on dead wood, containing the same toxins as the death cap.","identification":["Small brown to tawny caps, in clusters on stumps","Small, fragile ring on the stem"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Sheathed woodtuft (edible)","latin":"Kuehneromyces mutabilis","niveau":"danger","diff":"A classic and deadly confusion, even for experienced foragers."}],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"cepe":{"nom":"Penny bun (cep)","statut":"Edible · have it checked","saison":"Aug. → Nov.","resume":"The famous bolete. Edible, but only checking by an expert makes it safe to eat.","identification":["Brown cap, like a bread bun","Sponge underside (tubes), white then yellow-green, doesn't turn blue","Swollen stem with a fine raised white net at the top"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Bitter bolete (Tylopilus felleus)","latin":"Tylopilus felleus","niveau":"caution","diff":"Tubes turning pink, dark net on the stem, very bitter taste."},{"nom":"Red-pored boletes","niveau":"danger","diff":"Red or orange pores, flesh turning blue: discard."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist before eating."]},"sanglier":{"nom":"Wild boar","statut":"Caution","saison":"All year round · hunting in autumn","resume":"Very present in European forests. Avoids humans, except a sow with piglets or a wounded animal.","identification":["Tracks: two-toed hooves with 2 dewclaws marked behind","Rooted-up ground, muddy wallows, rubbed trees"],"usages":["Don't leave food accessible at night (hang it or keep it in the closed tent).","In autumn, driven hunts are frequent: respect the “Hunting in progress” signs and the paths."]},"cerf":{"nom":"Red deer","statut":"Watching","saison":"All year round","resume":"The rut has just ended. Watch at dawn and dusk at forest edges.","identification":["Tracks: split hoof in an elongated heart shape, 6–9 cm","Rubbing marks on young trees, bark stripped"],"usages":["Keep your distance, never feed them."]},"renard":{"nom":"Red fox","statut":"Health risk","saison":"All year round","resume":"Can transmit a serious parasite (echinococcosis) through its droppings on low vegetation.","identification":["Tracks: 4 toes, oval and narrow print, in a straight line","Twisted droppings, often placed prominently on a stone"],"usages":["Cook or wash thoroughly anything picked at ground level (berries, leaves, mushrooms)."]},"tique":{"nom":"Tick","statut":"Lyme disease","saison":"Active above about 7 °C","resume":"Less active in November, but a mild spell is enough. Check yourself after every trip.","identification":["Small brown-black mite, in tall grass and bracken"],"usages":["Trousers tucked into socks, light-coloured clothing.","Remove with a tick remover, twisting, disinfect, note the date. See a doctor if a redness spreads."]},"castor":{"nom":"Eurasian beaver","statut":"Watching","saison":"All year round","resume":"Back in many European valleys. Discreet, active at dusk along the water.","identification":["Trees gnawed into a sharpened-pencil shape at the water's edge","Wood chips, slides leading to the river"],"usages":["Protected species: watch without disturbing, don't touch the dams."]},"aildesours":{"nom":"Wild garlic (ramsons)","statut":"Edible · deadly look-alikes","saison":"Mar. → May","resume":"Carpets of garlic-scented leaves in damp woodland in spring. Its look-alikes are among the most poisonous plants in our forests.","identification":["Broad, soft, shiny leaves, each on its own thin stalk, coming straight out of the ground","Underside of the leaf matt, upper side shiny","White star-shaped flowers grouped in a ball (April–May)","Strong garlic smell when crushed — but the smell stays on your fingers: sniffing each leaf separately isn't enough"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Lily of the valley (Convallaria majalis)","latin":"Convallaria majalis","niveau":"danger","diff":"Two or three leaves wrapped around each other on the same stalk, no garlic smell. Deadly."},{"nom":"Autumn crocus (Colchicum autumnale)","latin":"Colchicum autumnale","niveau":"danger","diff":"Leaves in a tight tuft coming from the same point, stiffer, no smell. Deadly."},{"nom":"Lords-and-ladies (Arum maculatum)","latin":"Arum maculatum","niveau":"danger","diff":"Arrow-shaped leaves with net-like veins, sometimes spotted with black."}],"usages":["Pick leaf by leaf, never by the handful.","Raw in pesto or salad, or cooked like spinach.","If in doubt about a single leaf: throw everything away."]},"mure":{"nom":"Blackberry (bramble)","statut":"Edible","saison":"Jul. → Sep.","resume":"The easiest wild fruit: no dangerous look-alike in Belgium.","identification":["Thorny bramble with long arching stems","Leaves with 3 or 5 toothed leaflets","Shiny black fruits made of small beads"],"usages":["Raw. Pick from higher up rather than at ground level (see Fox).","Avoid roadsides."]},"framboise":{"nom":"Wild raspberry","statut":"Edible","saison":"Jul. → Aug.","resume":"Common in forest clearings and felled areas.","identification":["Upright stems with small, fine prickles","Whitish underside to the leaves","Hollow red fruit that comes away from its base"],"usages":["Raw, wash it if picked low down."]},"myrtille":{"nom":"Bilberry","statut":"Edible","saison":"Jul. → Aug.","resume":"Covers the ground in acid forests and heaths. Wild bilberries turn your mouth blue.","identification":["Small shrub 20 to 50 cm tall with angular green stems","Small, oval, finely toothed leaves","Blue-black berries with a small crown at the top, purple flesh"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Bog bilberry (Vaccinium uliginosum)","latin":"Vaccinium uliginosum","niveau":"caution","diff":"Round brown stems, bluish untoothed leaves, white flesh. Avoid in quantity."}],"usages":["Raw. Wash or cook if picked at ground level (echinococcosis, see Fox)."]},"fraise":{"nom":"Wild strawberry","statut":"Edible","saison":"May → Jul.","resume":"Tiny, very fragrant strawberries on forest edges and banks.","identification":["Leaves with 3 toothed leaflets","White five-petalled flowers","Small conical red fruit, seeds on the surface"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Barren strawberry","latin":"Potentilla sterilis","niveau":"ok","diff":"Same look but a dry, non-fleshy fruit: harmless, just disappointing."}],"usages":["Raw, wash it (grows at ground level)."]},"sureau":{"nom":"Elder","statut":"Flowers raw · berries cooked","saison":"Flowers May–June, berries Aug.–Sep.","resume":"A hedgerow shrub. Fragrant flowers and rich berries, but raw berries and the leaves are poisonous.","identification":["Shrub with grey, corky bark, white pith in the twigs","Wide, flat umbels of cream flowers in May–June","Hanging clusters of black berries in late summer"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Dwarf elder (Sambucus ebulus)","latin":"Sambucus ebulus","niveau":"danger","diff":"A herbaceous plant that regrows each year (no wood), UPRIGHT berry clusters, unpleasant smell. Poisonous."},{"nom":"Red elder (Sambucus racemosa)","latin":"Sambucus racemosa","niveau":"caution","diff":"Red berries in oval clusters. Poisonous seeds: avoid."}],"usages":["Flowers: in fritters or steeped in water (cordial).","Berries: always cooked, without green stalks (jam, syrup).","Never the leaves or bark."]},"oseille":{"nom":"Wild sorrel","statut":"Edible in moderation","saison":"Apr. → Sep.","resume":"A sharp, lemony taste. Rich in oxalic acid: in small amounts.","identification":["Arrow-shaped leaves, lobes at the base pointing downwards","Distinctly sour taste","Meadows and path edges"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Lords-and-ladies (young leaves)","latin":"Arum maculatum","niveau":"danger","diff":"Thicker leaves, net-like veins, no sour taste, burns the mouth. Poisonous."}],"usages":["A few leaves in salad or soup.","Not advised in quantity (kidney stones, gout)."]},"plantain":{"nom":"Ribwort plantain","statut":"Edible","saison":"Apr. → Oct.","resume":"Everywhere in meadows and paths. Traditionally used by crushing a leaf on a nettle or insect sting.","identification":["Rosette of long, narrow leaves with 3 to 5 very marked parallel veins","Dense brown spikes on long bare stalks"],"usages":["Young leaves in salad or cooked.","Young spikes: taste of mushroom once pan-fried."]},"cresson":{"nom":"Watercress","statut":"Always cooked","saison":"All year round","resume":"Grows in clear streams. Raw, it can transmit liver fluke (a parasite) near pastures.","identification":["Hollow stems floating or creeping in running water","Compound leaves with rounded leaflets, peppery taste","Small white four-petalled flowers"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Water parsnip and fool's watercress (umbellifers)","latin":"Helosciadium nodiflorum","niveau":"danger","diff":"Toothed leaves, flowers in umbels. The umbellifer family includes deadly species by the water."}],"usages":["Cooked only (soup), never raw if it comes from a wild stream."]},"tilleul":{"nom":"Lime (linden)","statut":"Edible","saison":"Leaves Apr.–May, flowers June–July","resume":"Tender young leaves and flowers for tea.","identification":["Large tree with asymmetric, heart-shaped, toothed leaves","Fragrant cream flowers hanging beneath a strap-shaped bract"],"usages":["Young leaves in salad.","Dried flowers as a tea."]},"pin":{"nom":"Scots pine","statut":"Tea · beware of yew","saison":"All year round","resume":"Fresh needles make a tea rich in vitamin C. The only real danger: mistaking it for yew.","identification":["Needles in PAIRS, joined at the base in a small sheath","Orange bark at the top of the trunk","Small cones (pine cones)"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Yew (Taxus baccata)","latin":"Taxus baccata","niveau":"danger","diff":"Flat needles, single, one by one, dark green, no resin smell. Deadly."},{"nom":"Norway spruce (Picea abies)","latin":"Picea abies","niveau":"ok","diff":"Single but prickly needles, square in cross-section. Young May shoots are edible."}],"usages":["Steep a handful of chopped needles for 10 minutes in hot (not boiling) water.","Avoid during pregnancy."]},"bardane":{"nom":"Burdock","statut":"Root, cooked","saison":"Root Sep. → Mar.","resume":"Its clinging burrs inspired Velcro. The first-year root is eaten cooked.","identification":["Very large heart-shaped leaves, white woolly underside","Round fruits covered with hooks that cling to clothing","Long taproot"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Foxglove (rosette)","latin":"Digitalis purpurea","niveau":"danger","diff":"Smaller leaves, downy on top, without a white underside. Deadly."},{"nom":"Rhubarb (leaves)","latin":"Rheum rhabarbarum","niveau":"danger","diff":"Poisonous leaves, red stalk. Don't confuse them in the garden."}],"usages":["Root peeled, cut and boiled or sautéed.","Dig only with the owner's permission."]},"pomme":{"nom":"Crab apple","statut":"Edible","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"Small, very sour apples, best cooked.","identification":["Small tree, sometimes thorny","Small apples 2 to 4 cm, green to yellow"],"usages":["Cooked as a compote or on the fire.","Don't crunch the pips in quantity."]},"merisier":{"nom":"Wild cherry","statut":"Edible","saison":"Jun. → Jul.","resume":"Small sweet cherries at forest edges, often raided by birds.","identification":["Large tree with smooth red-brown bark that peels in horizontal strips","Red to black cherries on long stalks"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Cherry laurel (Prunus laurocerasus)","latin":"Prunus laurocerasus","niveau":"danger","diff":"Hedge shrub with thick, shiny evergreen leaves, fruit in upright clusters. Poisonous."}],"usages":["Raw. Don't crunch the stones."]},"chenopode":{"nom":"Fat hen","statut":"Edible cooked","saison":"May → Sep.","resume":"A weed of disturbed ground, the wild cousin of spinach.","identification":["Diamond-shaped, toothed leaves, covered with a white, mealy powder (especially underneath and at the top)","Small green flowers in clusters"],"usages":["Cooked like spinach.","Avoid treated crops and field edges."]},"mauve":{"nom":"Common mallow","statut":"Edible","saison":"May → Sep.","resume":"Mild, slightly mucilaginous leaves and flowers.","identification":["Rounded leaves with 5 to 7 shallow lobes","Large pink-mauve flowers streaked with dark veins"],"usages":["Flowers raw in salad, leaves cooked in soup."]},"lierreterrestre":{"nom":"Ground ivy","statut":"Aromatic","saison":"Mar. → Jun.","resume":"A small, very fragrant creeping plant, used like an aromatic herb.","identification":["Creeping square stems","Round, kidney-shaped, scalloped, opposite leaves","Violet tubular flowers in spring"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Common ivy (Hedera helix)","latin":"Hedera helix","niveau":"danger","diff":"Woody climbing plant with leathery, shiny leaves. Poisonous."}],"usages":["In small amounts to flavour a salad or an omelette."]},"muguet":{"nom":"Lily of the valley","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Apr. → Jun.","resume":"Deadly look-alike of wild garlic, growing in the same woodland.","identification":["2 to 3 leaves wrapped around each other at the base of the same stalk","Fragrant white bells in a one-sided cluster","Red berries in summer"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"colchique":{"nom":"Autumn crocus","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Leaves Mar.–May, flowers Sep.–Oct.","resume":"Its spring leaves are confused with wild garlic. Mauve flowers in autumn, without leaves.","identification":["In spring: large leaves in a tight tuft coming from the same point, with a capsule in the centre","In autumn: mauve tulip-like flowers sitting on the ground"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"arum":{"nom":"Lords-and-ladies (cuckoo pint)","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"All year round","resume":"Common in damp woodland. Burns the mouth and throat, red berries attractive to children.","identification":["Arrow-shaped leaves, net-like veins, sometimes spotted with black","Pale green hood-shaped flower around a purple finger","Spike of bright red berries in summer"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"belladone":{"nom":"Deadly nightshade","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Berries Jul. → Sep.","resume":"Forest clearings and chalky edges. A few berries can kill a child.","identification":["Bushy plant up to 1.5 m","Brown-purple bell-shaped flowers","Shiny black berry like a cherry, sitting on a five-pointed green star"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Bilberry","latin":"Vaccinium myrtillus","niveau":"ok","diff":"A small shrub at ground level, matt blue berries with a crown. Deadly nightshade is a tall plant."}],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"cigue":{"nom":"Hemlock","statut":"Deadly","saison":"All year round","resume":"The most famous umbellifer. Waste ground, path edges and ditches.","identification":["Tall, smooth stem spotted with purple, hairless","Very finely divided leaves like parsley","Unpleasant mousy smell when crushed"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Wild carrot, cow parsley","latin":"Anthriscus sylvestris","niveau":"danger","diff":"Absolute rule: no wild umbellifer is eaten without an expert."}],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after contact."]},"berce":{"nom":"Giant hogweed","statut":"Severe burns","saison":"May → Sep.","resume":"A giant invasive plant along waterways. Its sap + sunlight cause severe burns and lasting scars.","identification":["Giant 2 to 4 m tall, thick stem spotted with purple","Huge, deeply divided leaves","White umbels 50 cm across"],"usages":["Don't touch. If contact: wash with soap and water, cover the skin and keep it out of the sun for 48 h, see a doctor if blisters appear."]},"aconit":{"nom":"Monkshood (aconite)","statut":"Deadly","saison":"All year round","resume":"One of the most poisonous plants in Europe, even through prolonged touch. Damp meadows and stream banks.","identification":["Leaves deeply cut into strips","Blue-violet helmet-shaped flowers (June–August)"],"usages":["Don't pick, don't handle with bare hands."]},"houx":{"nom":"Holly","statut":"Poisonous berries","saison":"Berries Oct. → Feb.","resume":"Its red winter berries cause vomiting and diarrhoea.","identification":["Shrub with shiny, prickly evergreen leaves","Bright red berries in winter"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"fusain":{"nom":"Spindle","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"Fruits Sep. → Nov.","resume":"Bright pink “bishop's hat” fruits, very decorative and poisonous.","identification":["Green twigs with 4 corky angles","Pink four-lobed capsules opening on orange seeds"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"bryone":{"nom":"White bryony","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"Berries Aug. → Oct.","resume":"A hedgerow climber with red berries and a large root (“devil's turnip”).","identification":["Climbing vine with spring-like tendrils","Five-lobed, rough leaves","Small red berries in clusters"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"laurier":{"nom":"Cherry laurel","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"All year round","resume":"A very common hedge plant that has escaped into forests. Leaves and seeds release cyanide when crushed.","identification":["Thick, shiny evergreen leaves, smell of bitter almond when crushed","Black fruits in upright clusters"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Don't use its branches to cook food."]},"girolle":{"nom":"Chanterelle","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Jun. → Oct.","resume":"A highly prized egg-yolk-yellow mushroom, often in groups under beech and oak.","identification":["Uniform egg-yolk yellow, irregular funnel-shaped cap","Underside with thick, forked RIDGES running down the stem (no true gills)","Firm white flesh, apricot smell"],"sosies":[{"nom":"False chanterelle (Hygrophoropsis aurantiaca)","latin":"Hygrophoropsis aurantiaca","niveau":"caution","diff":"Brighter orange, true thin crowded gills, soft flesh. Indigestible."},{"nom":"Jack-o'-lantern (Omphalotus olearius)","latin":"Omphalotus olearius","niveau":"danger","diff":"Orange, in clumps on wood or stumps, true gills. Severe digestive upset."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"piedmouton":{"nom":"Hedgehog mushroom","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Aug. → Nov.","resume":"Recognisable by its underside covered in spines, with no known poisonous look-alike here.","identification":["Cream to pale orange, irregular cap","Underside covered in small, fragile SPINES instead of gills","Brittle white flesh"],"usages":["Get it checked before eating."]},"coulemelle":{"nom":"Parasol mushroom","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Jul. → Oct.","resume":"A large umbrella mushroom of meadows and edges. Its small cousins, the Lepiotas, are deadly.","identification":["Cap 15 to 30 cm covered with brown scales, a bump in the centre","Tall stem with brown snakeskin pattern and a double ring that slides","Free white gills"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Small Lepiotas (Lepiota brunneoincarnata…)","latin":"Lepiota brunneoincarnata","niveau":"danger","diff":"Cap LESS than 10 cm, with pink to brown scales. Deadly: never a small Lepiota."},{"nom":"Panther cap (Amanita pantherina)","latin":"Amanita pantherina","niveau":"danger","diff":"Smooth brown cap with white flakes, a rimmed volva at the base. Poisonous."}],"usages":["Only eat caps over 15 cm, and after an expert has checked them."]},"trompette":{"nom":"Horn of plenty (black trumpet)","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Aug. → Nov.","resume":"Despite its French name (“trumpet of death”), an excellent edible. Black, trumpet-shaped, under beech.","identification":["Grey-black funnel, hollow down to the stem","Smooth or wrinkled outside, no gills","Grows in troops among dead leaves"],"usages":["Get it checked before eating."]},"pleurote":{"nom":"Oyster mushroom","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Oct. → Mar.","resume":"Grows in tiers on dead broadleaf wood, even in winter.","identification":["Grey-blue to brown oyster-shaped caps, with little or no stem","White gills running down the stem","In overlapping clusters on trunks and stumps"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Sulphur tuft (Hypholoma fasciculare)","latin":"Hypholoma fasciculare","niveau":"danger","diff":"Sulphur-yellow caps in clumps on stumps, greenish gills, bitter taste. Poisonous."}],"usages":["Get it checked before eating."]},"vesse":{"nom":"Giant puffball","statut":"Edible when young · get it checked","saison":"Jun. → Oct.","resume":"A white ball that can reach the size of a football. The rule: always cut it in half.","identification":["Large smooth white ball, no visible stem","Uniform white flesh, like mozzarella, when young"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Young Amanita “in the egg”","latin":"Amanita phalloides","niveau":"danger","diff":"Cut in half, it shows the outline of a mushroom (cap, gills, stem) inside. Deadly."}],"usages":["Cut every specimen in half: the flesh must be white and uniform.","Get it checked before eating."]},"morille":{"nom":"Morel","statut":"Edible cooked · get it checked","saison":"Mar. → May","resume":"A spring mushroom with a cap pitted like a sponge. Poisonous raw.","identification":["Honeycomb cap, joined to the stem","Completely hollow when cut in half"],"sosies":[{"nom":"False morel (Gyromitra esculenta)","latin":"Gyromitra esculenta","niveau":"danger","diff":"Brain-like cap (folds), chambered inside, not hollow. Deadly."}],"usages":["Always well cooked, never raw.","Get it checked before eating."]},"boletbai":{"nom":"Bay bolete","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Aug. → Nov.","resume":"Very common under spruce and pine.","identification":["Chestnut-brown cap, sticky in wet weather","Yellow pores that turn blue when touched","Light brown stem without a net pattern"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Bitter bolete (Tylopilus felleus)","latin":"Tylopilus felleus","niveau":"caution","diff":"Pores that turn pink, dark net on the stem, very bitter taste."}],"usages":["Get it checked before eating."]},"panthere":{"nom":"Panther cap","statut":"Seriously poisonous","saison":"Jul. → Oct.","resume":"Causes agitation, confusion and coma. Gets confused with edibles.","identification":["Smooth brown cap dotted with white flakes","White gills, smooth ring","Bulb at the base with a clear rim"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"vireuse":{"nom":"Destroying angel","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Jul. → Oct.","resume":"The “angel of death”: entirely white, as deadly as the death cap.","identification":["All white, cap often conical","Shaggy stem, fragile ring","White sac-like volva at the base"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Field mushroom, white Agaricus","latin":"Agaricus campestris","niveau":"danger","diff":"White means nothing: a white mushroom is not necessarily edible."}],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"cortinaire":{"nom":"Fool's webcap","statut":"Deadly (kidneys)","saison":"Aug. → Oct.","resume":"Destroys the kidneys. Symptoms may only appear after several days.","identification":["Tawny-orange mushroom, silky cap","Rust-coloured gills","Cobweb-like veil remains (cortina) on young ones"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"entolome":{"nom":"Livid pinkgill","statut":"Seriously poisonous","saison":"Aug. → Oct.","resume":"A large pale grey mushroom, often picked by mistake.","identification":["Grey-cream cap, wavy edge","Yellowish gills turning salmon pink","Smell of flour"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"hypholome":{"nom":"Sulphur tuft","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"All year round","resume":"The most common sulphur-yellow mushroom on stumps.","identification":["Sulphur-yellow caps, more orange in the centre, in large clumps","Yellow-green gills turning blackish","Very bitter taste"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"vipere":{"nom":"Adder","statut":"Rare · bite","saison":"Mar. → Oct.","resume":"The only viper of northern Europe. Discreet and rare, it only bites if handled or stepped on.","identification":["Stocky snake 50 to 65 cm long","Continuous dark zigzag along the back","Vertical slit pupil"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Grass snake (Natrix helvetica)","latin":"Natrix helvetica","niveau":"ok","diff":"Longer and slimmer, yellow and black collar behind the head, round pupil. Harmless."}],"usages":["If bitten: stay calm, lie down, keep the limb still, remove rings and watch, disinfect.","Don't suck, cut or apply a tourniquet. Call 112."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, only in the south of Namur province (near Givet) and a reintroduction area in Liège province. Not known in the Ourthe valley.","FR":"Found mainly in the northern half, the Massif Central, the Jura and the Alps. Don't confuse it with the asp viper, further south."}},"loup":{"nom":"Grey wolf","statut":"Rare · discreet","saison":"All year round","resume":"Back in much of Western Europe. It avoids humans.","identification":["Bigger than a German shepherd, grey-beige coat, dark back","Tracks: 4 toes, oval print 10 cm long, in a straight line"],"usages":["Never feed or approach. Keep your dog on a lead.","Report a sighting to your region's nature services (see the country note)."],"notes":{"BE":"Has returned naturally to Wallonia, with several areas of permanent presence. Report a sighting to the Réseau Loup (Service public de Wallonie).","FR":"Well established in the Alps, the Jura and the Massif Central, and spreading elsewhere.","CH":"Several packs in the Alps and the Jura."}},"chevreuil":{"nom":"Roe deer","statut":"Watching","saison":"All year round","resume":"The most common deer. Barks when disturbed.","identification":["Small deer, reddish in summer, grey-brown in winter","White patch on the rump (the “mirror”)","Tracks: small split hoof 4 to 5 cm"],"usages":["A fawn on its own is not abandoned: never touch it."]},"blaireau":{"nom":"Badger","statut":"Watching","saison":"All year round","resume":"Nocturnal, lives in clans in large setts.","identification":["Black-and-white striped head","Tracks: 5 toes in a line with long claws","Latrines: small pits where it leaves its droppings"],"usages":["Don't camp near an active sett."]},"salamandre":{"nom":"Fire salamander","statut":"Don't touch","saison":"Mar. → Oct.","resume":"Black and yellow, comes out on rainy nights in damp forests. A protected species threatened by a deadly fungus carried on hands and boots.","identification":["Black amphibian with large yellow spots","Slow, moves at night in wet weather"],"usages":["Don't handle it: its skin secretes an irritant, and you can pass a disease on to it.","Clean and dry your boots between two sites."]},"processionnaire":{"nom":"Oak processionary caterpillar","statut":"Stinging hairs","saison":"May → Aug.","resume":"Its microscopic hairs cause itching, eye irritation and sometimes breathing difficulties, even without direct contact.","identification":["Hairy grey caterpillars in lines or clusters on oak trunks","Whitish silky nests on the bark"],"usages":["Don't approach the nests, don't camp under an infested oak.","If contact: don't rub, rinse with water, change clothes."]},"frelon":{"nom":"Wasps and hornets","statut":"Stings","saison":"May → Oct.","resume":"Stings are painful, and dangerous in case of allergy or multiple stings.","identification":["Hornet: large (3 cm), reddish-brown and yellow","Wasp: yellow and black, narrow waist"],"usages":["Don't leave sweet food open.","If the face swells, breathing gets difficult or you feel faint after a sting: call 112."]},"rosedespres":{"nom":"Field mushroom","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Jul. → Oct.","resume":"The pasture mushroom, wild cousin of the button mushroom. Its confusions with white Amanitas are among the most serious.","identification":["Silky white cap","PINK gills when young, then chocolate brown (never white)","Single ring, NO volva at the base","In grazed meadows, not in forests"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Destroying angel / death cap","latin":"Amanita virosa","niveau":"danger","diff":"WHITE gills that stay white, sac-like volva at the base (dig out the stem). Deadly."},{"nom":"Yellow stainer","latin":"Agaricus xanthodermus","niveau":"danger","diff":"Turns bright yellow when you scratch the base of the stem, smell of ink or chemicals. Poisonous."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"coprin":{"nom":"Shaggy ink cap","statut":"Edible very young · get it checked","saison":"May → Nov.","resume":"Tall, scaly, shell-shaped white cap on banks and lawns. It dissolves into black ink within hours.","identification":["Cylindrical white cap covered in woolly scales","White gills turning pink, then liquid black","Movable ring on the stem"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Common ink cap","latin":"Coprinopsis atramentaria","niveau":"danger","diff":"Smooth grey bell-shaped cap. Poisonous with alcohol up to 72 h after the meal."}],"usages":["Only very young, gills still white, eaten the same day.","Get it checked before eating."]},"piedbleu":{"nom":"Wood blewit","statut":"Edible cooked · get it checked","saison":"Oct. → Dec.","resume":"A late-season violet mushroom in dead leaves and hedges.","identification":["Lilac to violet-brown cap, smooth","Lilac, crowded gills","Violet stem, without ring or veil","Fruity smell"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Violet webcaps","latin":"Cortinarius traganus","niveau":"danger","diff":"Cobweb-like veil remains (cortina) under the cap, gills turning rust. Some webcaps destroy the kidneys."}],"usages":["Always well cooked.","Get it checked before eating."]},"mousseron":{"nom":"St George's mushroom","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Apr. → Jun.","resume":"A white spring mushroom, often in rings in the grass, with a strong smell of flour.","identification":["Thick, fleshy cream cap","Very crowded white gills","Strong smell of fresh flour","Grows in spring, in rings or lines"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Deadly fibrecap","latin":"Inocybe erubescens","niveau":"danger","diff":"Fibrous conical cap that turns red, fruity smell. Seriously poisonous (muscarine)."},{"nom":"Livid pinkgill","latin":"Entoloma sinuatum","niveau":"danger","diff":"Yellowish gills turning salmon pink. Seriously poisonous."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"fauxmousseron":{"nom":"Fairy ring champignon","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"May → Nov.","resume":"A small beige lawn mushroom, growing in “fairy rings”.","identification":["Small beige cap with a bump","Widely spaced cream gills","Thin, tough stem: it twists without breaking","In rings on lawns"],"sosies":[{"nom":"White Clitocybes","latin":"Clitocybe rivulosa","niveau":"danger","diff":"Same lawns, powdery white cap, crowded gills running down the stem. Poisonous (muscarine)."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"armillaire":{"nom":"Honey fungus","statut":"Experts · well cooked","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"Huge honey-yellow clumps on stumps and trees. Poorly tolerated by many people, even cooked.","identification":["Tight clumps on wood or at the foot of trees","Honey-coloured caps dotted with small dark tufts","Cottony ring on the stem"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Funeral bell","latin":"Galerina marginata","niveau":"danger","diff":"Smaller, in small clumps, without dark tufts. Deadly."},{"nom":"Sulphur tuft","latin":"Hypholoma fasciculare","niveau":"danger","diff":"Sulphur yellow, greenish gills, no ring, very bitter. Poisonous."}],"usages":["For experienced foragers only: never raw, always well cooked, in small amounts.","Get it checked before eating."]},"collybie":{"nom":"Velvet shank","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Nov. → Mar.","resume":"One of the few mushrooms of deep winter, on dead wood, even after frost.","identification":["Small orange caps, sticky and shiny","VELVETY stem, brown-black towards the base","In clumps on broadleaf wood, in winter"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Funeral bell","latin":"Galerina marginata","niveau":"danger","diff":"Small ring on the stem, stem not velvety, matt cap. Deadly: a classic winter confusion."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"oreillejudas":{"nom":"Jelly ear","statut":"Edible cooked · get it checked","saison":"All year round","resume":"A brown, gelatinous ear-shaped fungus, mostly on elder.","identification":["Gelatinous, rubbery texture","Brown ear shape, velvety on top","On dead or living elder branches"],"usages":["Always cooked.","Get it checked before eating."]},"polypore":{"nom":"Chicken of the woods","statut":"Experts · well cooked","saison":"May → Sep.","resume":"Large bright orange and yellow brackets on trunks. The “chicken of the woods”.","identification":["Overlapping bright orange brackets edged with yellow","Sulphur-yellow underside full of pores","Tender flesh when young"],"usages":["Only young and well cooked, in small amounts (intolerance is common).","Never if it grows on yew, black locust or a conifer.","Get it checked before eating."]},"languedeboeuf":{"nom":"Beefsteak fungus","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Aug. → Oct.","resume":"A surprising blood-red bracket on old oaks and sweet chestnuts.","identification":["Bracket red like a tongue or a liver, moist","Marbled red flesh that oozes red juice","Cream underside with small separate tubes"],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"chanterelletube":{"nom":"Trumpet chanterelle (winter chanterelle)","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Sep. → Dec.","resume":"A small brown chanterelle with a yellow stem, in troops among the moss of conifer forests.","identification":["Brown funnel-shaped cap, often pierced in the centre","Underside with grey-yellow ridges (no true gills)","Hollow yellow stem"],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"russulecharbon":{"nom":"Charcoal burner","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Jul. → Oct.","resume":"One of the most common brittlegills under broadleaf trees.","identification":["Violet to greenish, multicoloured cap","Supple white gills, greasy to the touch (they don't break)","White flesh that breaks like chalk"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Acrid brittlegills and Amanitas","latin":"Russula emetica","niveau":"danger","diff":"Several brittlegills are poisonous, and a green death cap can get mixed in. Have each specimen checked."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist or a mycological society before eating."]},"rougissante":{"nom":"Blusher","statut":"Experts only","saison":"Jun. → Oct.","resume":"Edible when well cooked, but it's an Amanita: confusing it with the panther cap is far too easy.","identification":["Pinkish-brown cap with grey-beige flakes","Flesh that slowly turns RED where damaged or nibbled by slugs","Striated ring"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Panther cap","latin":"Amanita pantherina","niveau":"danger","diff":"Pure white flakes, flesh that doesn't redden, smooth ring. Seriously poisonous."}],"usages":["Not advised for anyone except experienced mycologists.","Poisonous raw."]},"printaniere":{"nom":"Spring amanita","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Apr. → Jun.","resume":"An entirely white deadly Amanita… in spring, when you don't expect it.","identification":["All white, smooth cap","White gills, ring, sac-like volva at the base"],"sosies":[{"nom":"St George's mushroom","latin":"Calocybe gambosa","niveau":"caution","diff":"No ring or volva, smell of flour. Always dig out the base of the stem."}],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"lepiote":{"nom":"Deadly dapperling","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Jul. → Oct.","resume":"A small Lepiota of parks and edges, with the same toxins as the death cap.","identification":["Small cap 2 to 5 cm, concentric pinkish-brown scales","Free white gills","Stem banded with pinkish zones"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Parasol mushroom","latin":"Macrolepiota procera","niveau":"caution","diff":"Cap over 15 cm, sliding double ring. Rule: no Lepiota under 10 cm."}],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"gyromitre":{"nom":"False morel","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Mar. → May","resume":"The “false morel” of conifer woods. Its toxins also evaporate during cooking.","identification":["Red-brown cap folded like a brain","Chambered inside, not hollow"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Morel","latin":"Morchella esculenta","niveau":"caution","diff":"Cap with regular pits, completely hollow when cut."}],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"omphalote":{"nom":"Jack-o'-lantern","statut":"Seriously poisonous","saison":"Jul. → Nov.","resume":"Large bright orange clumps at the foot of oaks and olive trees, confused with the chanterelle.","identification":["Bright orange clumps on wood or buried stumps","True crowded gills running down the stem"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Chanterelle","latin":"Cantharellus cibarius","niveau":"caution","diff":"Thick, forked ridges (no gills), grows singly on the ground, never in a large clump on wood."}],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"paxille":{"nom":"Brown rollrim","statut":"Deadly (repeated eating)","saison":"Jul. → Nov.","resume":"Long eaten, it can trigger a deadly immune reaction after several meals.","identification":["Brown cap with its edge ROLLED inwards","Yellowish gills that turn brown when touched and come off easily","Very common under birch"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"equestre":{"nom":"Yellow knight","statut":"Seriously poisonous","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"The “man on horseback”, once prized: repeated meals destroy the muscles.","identification":["Greenish-yellow cap, browner centre","Sulphur-yellow gills","Under pines, on sandy soil"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"tigre":{"nom":"Tiger tricholoma","statut":"Seriously poisonous","saison":"Aug. → Oct.","resume":"A large grey mushroom causing severe digestive poisoning.","identification":["Grey cap covered with dark scales in a tiger pattern","Cream-white gills, smell of flour"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"clitoblanc":{"nom":"Fool's funnel","statut":"Poisonous (muscarine)","saison":"Jul. → Nov.","resume":"A small white lawn mushroom, growing among fairy ring champignons.","identification":["White to cream cap, as if dusted with frost","Crowded gills running down the stem"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Symptoms: sweating, tears, salivation within the hour: call the poison centre."]},"noirdencre":{"nom":"Common ink cap","statut":"Poisonous with alcohol","saison":"Apr. → Nov.","resume":"Eaten with alcohol (or up to 72 h before or after), it causes hot flushes, palpitations and fainting.","identification":["Grey bell-shaped, pleated cap, in clumps","Gills that dissolve into black ink"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"emetique":{"nom":"The sickener","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"Jul. → Nov.","resume":"A bright red brittlegill of damp forests, very acrid, causes vomiting and diarrhoea.","identification":["Bright red, smooth, shiny cap","Pure white gills and stem","Brittle, very hot-tasting flesh"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"satan":{"nom":"Devil's bolete","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"Jul. → Sep.","resume":"A large, stocky bolete of chalky soils, with red pores and a whitish cap.","identification":["Greyish-white cap","Blood-red pores","Swollen red stem with a net pattern","Flesh that turns blue, unpleasant smell as it ages"],"usages":["Simple rule: reject all boletes with red pores."]},"psilocybe":{"nom":"Liberty cap","statut":"Hallucinogenic · illegal","saison":"Sep. → Nov.","resume":"A small meadow mushroom, hallucinogenic. Possessing it is illegal, and poisonings regularly end up in A&E.","identification":["Small, pointed conical cap, beige to brown","Very thin, wavy stem","In damp grazed meadows"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Wash your hands after handling."]},"arbousier":{"nom":"Strawberry tree","statut":"Edible in moderation","saison":"Oct. → Dec.","resume":"The “tree strawberries” of the maquis. When fully ripe, they are sweet and mealy.","identification":["Shrub with shiny, finely toothed evergreen leaves","Round, rough fruits, yellow then bright red","White bell-shaped flowers at the same time as the fruit"],"usages":["Only fully red, soft fruits.","In small amounts: overripe, they ferment slightly."]},"figuebarbarie":{"nom":"Prickly pear","statut":"Edible · invisible spines","saison":"Aug. → Oct.","resume":"A cactus with flat “pads”. Its fruits are delicious but covered in tiny spines that stick everywhere.","identification":["Fleshy oval pad-shaped stems","Oval green to red fruits on the edge of the pads","Tufts of tiny spines (glochids) on the fruits"],"usages":["Never touch with bare hands: use tongs or a thick cloth.","Roll the fruits on the ground with a stick, then peel them with a knife."]},"pinparasol":{"nom":"Stone pine (pine nuts)","statut":"Edible","saison":"All year round","resume":"Its large cones release pine nuts, very nourishing.","identification":["Characteristic umbrella-shaped crown","Long needles in pairs","Large, heavy cones containing hard-shelled seeds"],"usages":["Warm the cone near the fire so it opens, then crack the shell of the pine nuts."]},"thym":{"nom":"Thyme","statut":"Aromatic","saison":"All year round","resume":"A small, very fragrant shrub of the garrigue.","identification":["Sub-shrub 10 to 30 cm, woody stems","Tiny rolled grey leaves","Strong characteristic smell when crushed"],"usages":["As a tea or to flavour a dish.","Pick a few sprigs without uprooting the plant."]},"romarin":{"nom":"Rosemary","statut":"Aromatic","saison":"All year round","resume":"An aromatic shrub of the garrigue and coasts.","identification":["Shrub with needle-like leaves, dark green above, white below, rolled edges","Pale blue flowers","Camphor-like smell"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Yew (Taxus baccata)","latin":"Taxus baccata","niveau":"danger","diff":"Flat needles, plain green on both sides, no smell. Deadly."}],"usages":["As a tea or to flavour grilled meat."]},"asperge":{"nom":"Wild asparagus","statut":"Edible · poisonous look-alike","saison":"Mar. → May","resume":"Thin bitter shoots, much sought after in spring in the maquis.","identification":["Spiny bush with prickly needle-like “leaves”","In spring, thin green shoots with tight tips coming from the base"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Black bryony (Dioscorea communis)","latin":"Dioscorea communis","niveau":"danger","diff":"Climbing shoots with no spiny bush around them, tip that coils. Poisonous, very irritant."}],"usages":["Cook the shoots like asparagus (omelette)."]},"laurierrose":{"nom":"Oleander","statut":"Deadly","saison":"All year round","resume":"An ornamental shrub found everywhere in the south of France, deadly for the heart. Even the smoke from its wood is dangerous.","identification":["Narrow, leathery leaves, grouped in threes","Large pink, white or red flowers in bunches"],"usages":["Eat nothing. Never use it as a skewer for food or burn it."]},"datura":{"nom":"Thorn apple (jimsonweed)","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Jun. → Oct.","resume":"A weed of fields and waste ground, spreading. Its seeds have already contaminated crops.","identification":["Large white trumpet-shaped flowers","Spiny capsule fruit the size of a walnut","Unpleasant smell"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"ricin":{"nom":"Castor oil plant","statut":"Deadly","saison":"All year round","resume":"Its mottled seeds contain one of the most powerful plant toxins.","identification":["Large palmate leaves with 5–11 lobes, often reddish","Spiny capsule fruits containing shiny mottled seeds"],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"lactaire":{"nom":"Saffron milk cap","statut":"Edible · get it checked","saison":"Sep. → Dec.","resume":"The “saffron” of pine woods: it oozes an orange milk.","identification":["Orange cap with concentric rings","Carrot-ORANGE milk where broken, turning green","Under pines"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Woolly milk cap (Lactarius torminosus)","latin":"Lactarius torminosus","niveau":"danger","diff":"Hairy cap edge, very acrid WHITE milk. Poisonous."}],"usages":["Have every harvest checked by a mycologist before eating."]},"aspic":{"nom":"Asp viper","statut":"Bite","saison":"Mar. → Oct.","resume":"The most common viper in France. It likes sunny scree, low walls and edges.","identification":["Stocky snake 60 to 75 cm long, slightly upturned snout","Back markings in bars or a broken zigzag","Vertical pupil"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Grass snakes","latin":"Natrix helvetica","niveau":"ok","diff":"Slimmer, round pupil, large scales on the head. Harmless."}],"usages":["If bitten: stay calm, lie down, keep the limb still, remove rings and watch.","Don't suck, cut or apply a tourniquet. Call 112."]},"processionpin":{"nom":"Pine processionary caterpillar","statut":"Stinging hairs","saison":"Jan. → Apr.","resume":"Moves in long lines on the ground at the end of winter. Dangerous for dogs and children.","identification":["Hairy orange-brown caterpillars following each other in single file","White silky ball-shaped nests at the tips of pine branches"],"usages":["Don't touch the caterpillars or the nests, keep dogs on a lead.","If contact: don't rub, rinse with water, change clothes, see a doctor if the eyes or mouth are affected."]},"veratre":{"nom":"White false hellebore","statut":"Deadly","saison":"Apr. → Sep.","resume":"Grows in the same mountain pastures as great yellow gentian. The confusion is deadly and happens every year.","identification":["Large leaves pleated like an accordion, ALTERNATE (one by one, in a spiral) along the stem","Downy underside to the leaves","Clusters of white-green flowers in summer"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Great yellow gentian (Gentiana lutea)","latin":"Gentiana lutea","niveau":"caution","diff":"Leaves OPPOSITE in pairs, smooth underneath, yellow flowers. Protected in many regions: don't pick."}],"usages":["Eat nothing."]},"airelle":{"nom":"Lingonberry (cowberry)","statut":"Edible","saison":"Aug. → Oct.","resume":"Small, tart red berries of heaths and mountain forests.","identification":["Small shrub with leathery, shiny evergreen leaves, with black dots underneath","Shiny red berries in clusters"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Bearberry (Arctostaphylos uva-ursi)","latin":"Arctostaphylos uva-ursi","niveau":"caution","diff":"Leaves without black dots underneath, mealy, tasteless berries. Avoid."}],"usages":["Raw (tart) or cooked as a compote."]},"epilobe":{"nom":"Rosebay willowherb (fireweed)","statut":"Edible","saison":"Apr. → Jun. (shoots)","resume":"Tall pink spikes in forest clearings, felled areas and mountain glades.","identification":["Upright stems up to 1.5 m","Narrow lance-shaped leaves, veins that join in loops near the edge","Clusters of bright pink flowers in summer"],"usages":["Young shoots cooked like asparagus.","Dried leaves as a tea."]},"chamois":{"nom":"Chamois","statut":"Watching","saison":"All year round","resume":"Agile on steep slopes, it whistles when it's worried.","identification":["Brown coat in summer, almost black in winter","White and black face mask","Small black horns curved into a hook"],"usages":["Watch from a distance, without making it flee down the slopes, especially in winter when every effort exhausts it."]},"marmotte":{"nom":"Alpine marmot","statut":"Watching","saison":"Apr. → Oct.","resume":"Whistles to warn the others. Hibernates from October to April.","identification":["Large grey-brown rodent sitting near its burrow","Sharp warning whistle"],"usages":["Don't feed them: human food makes them ill."]},"patou":{"nom":"Livestock guardian dogs","statut":"How to behave","saison":"May → Oct.","resume":"Large white dogs guarding flocks in mountain pastures. They protect the sheep, not the hikers.","identification":["Large white or cream dog, often alone in the middle of or near a flock","Barks and comes towards walkers"],"usages":["Go well around the flock. Get off your bike and walk.","Stop calmly if it approaches, don't shout, don't threaten it with a stick, don't stare at it.","Let it identify you, then move on slowly without turning your back abruptly.","Keep your own dog on a lead, close to you."]},"salicorne":{"nom":"Samphire (glasswort)","statut":"Edible","saison":"Jun. → Aug.","resume":"A crunchy, naturally salty plant of salt marshes.","identification":["Small fleshy plant with no visible leaves, stems jointed in segments","Grows in the mud of salt marshes, flooded at high tide"],"usages":["Raw or blanched, without adding salt.","Picking is often regulated or banned in nature reserves: check first."]},"argousier":{"nom":"Sea buckthorn","statut":"Edible","saison":"Sep. → Dec.","resume":"A spiny dune shrub with orange berries very rich in vitamin C.","identification":["Very spiny shrub, narrow silvery leaves","Bright orange berries packed along the twigs"],"usages":["Very sour berries: as juice or cooked.","Cutting a twig damages the shrub: pick by hand, with gloves."]},"vive":{"nom":"Lesser weever (venomous fish)","statut":"Very painful sting","saison":"Jun. → Sep.","resume":"A small fish buried in the sand of beaches at low tide. Its dorsal spines inject a very painful venom.","identification":["Small yellow-brown fish 10 to 15 cm, buried in the sand","Black, spiny dorsal fin"],"usages":["Wear water shoes on sand at low tide.","If stung: put the foot in the hottest water you can bear (without scalding) for 30 minutes, because heat destroys the venom. See a doctor if you feel faint or the swelling is large."]},"meduse":{"nom":"Jellyfish","statut":"Stings","saison":"Jun. → Sep.","resume":"Some cause sharp stings, even when washed up on the beach.","identification":["Gelatinous umbrella-shaped body, long tentacles","Never touch a stranded jellyfish"],"usages":["Get out of the water, rinse with seawater (not fresh water, which bursts the stinging cells).","Remove the tentacles with a card or tweezers, without rubbing.","Call 112 if breathing becomes difficult or you feel faint."]},"massette":{"nom":"Bulrush (cattail)","statut":"Edible · clean water","saison":"Shoots Apr.–Jun., rhizomes all year round","resume":"The “cigar reed” of ponds and ditches. Almost the whole plant is edible, but it also concentrates water pollutants.","identification":["Long, thick, spongy strap-like leaves, sheathed inside each other at the base","Brown sausage-shaped spike at the top of a long stem in summer","Grows with its feet in the water, in large colonies"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Yellow flag iris (Iris pseudacorus)","latin":"Iris pseudacorus","niveau":"danger","diff":"Flat leaves arranged in a fan, stiffer, no spongy sheath; large yellow flowers in spring. Poisonous."}],"usages":["Young spring shoots: the white heart, raw or cooked.","Rhizomes: peeled and cooked, rich in starch.","Only in clean water, away from roads, fields and outflows."]},"iris":{"nom":"Yellow flag iris","statut":"Poisonous","saison":"All year round","resume":"Grows in the same places as the bulrush, and gets confused with it when not in flower.","identification":["Flat, stiff leaves in a fan","Large yellow flowers in May–June","Thick rhizome"],"usages":["Eat nothing. The sap can irritate the skin."]},"truite":{"nom":"Brown trout","statut":"Edible cooked · regulated","saison":"Season opens early March (Wallonia)","resume":"The wild trout of cool streams and rivers like the Ourthe. Excellent cooked, but tightly controlled by fishing rules.","identification":["Streamlined body, brown-green back","Black spots and red spots ringed with pale on the flanks","Small adipose fin (no rays) between the dorsal fin and the tail","Lives in clear, cool, well-oxygenated water"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Atlantic salmon (young, parr)","latin":"Salmo salar","niveau":"danger","diff":"More forked tail, a row of dark marks on the flanks (parr marks), no ringed red spots. Protected: release it."},{"nom":"Rainbow trout","latin":"Oncorhynchus mykiss","niveau":"ok","diff":"Pinkish-purple band along the flank, small black spots right onto the tail. A stocked farmed fish, edible."}],"usages":["Barbless hooks are required in many sectors of Wallonia: check before fishing.","Cook right through: the flesh turns opaque and flakes.","A trout keeps a few hours in the cool: gutted, in the shade, wrapped in damp grass."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"perche":{"nom":"Perch","statut":"Edible cooked · spines","saison":"All year round (depending on rules)","resume":"One of the best freshwater fish, common in still water and slow rivers. Beware the spines of the dorsal fin.","identification":["Dark vertical stripes on a greenish body","Large spiny dorsal fin with a black spot at the back","Orange-red belly fins"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Ruffe","latin":"Gymnocephalus cernua","niveau":"ok","diff":"Smaller, no clear stripes, covered in small dots. Also edible."}],"usages":["Grab the perch by folding the dorsal fin back with your hand, so you don't get pricked.","May carry a parasitic worm (fish tapeworm): never raw, always well cooked.","Easy to scale right after catching."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"brochet":{"nom":"Pike","statut":"Edible cooked · teeth and bones","saison":"Depending on rules (closed in spring)","resume":"The big predator of still waters. Its flesh is good, but it has sharp teeth and many Y-shaped bones.","identification":["Very elongated body, duck-bill head","Green flanks marbled with pale spots","Dorsal and anal fins set right at the back","Mouth full of pointed teeth"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Zander","latin":"Sander lucioperca","niveau":"ok","diff":"Two dorsal fins, one spiny, large glassy eyes, striped grey flanks."}],"usages":["Never put your fingers in its mouth: use pliers or a stick to unhook it.","May carry the fish tapeworm: always well cooked, never as tartare or raw-marinated.","Big fish build up more pollutants: prefer medium-sized ones.","In Wallonia, keeping pike is restricted in some areas: check the rules."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"sandre":{"nom":"Zander","statut":"Edible cooked · spines","saison":"Depending on rules (closed in spring)","resume":"A predator of slow rivers, canals and murky lakes. Firm white flesh, highly prized.","identification":["Elongated grey-green body with blurred dark bands","Two dorsal fins, the first spiny","Large glassy eyes and a few big teeth (fangs)"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Perch","latin":"Perca fluviatilis","niveau":"ok","diff":"Stockier, clear stripes, orange belly fins."}],"usages":["Watch out for the dorsal spines and the fangs.","Cook well, like all freshwater fish."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"gardon":{"nom":"Roach","statut":"Edible cooked · bones","saison":"All year round (depending on rules)","resume":"The most common “white fish”. Easy to catch, edible, but full of small bones.","identification":["Small silvery fish with a blue-green back","Eye with a red iris","Reddish belly and anal fins"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Rudd","latin":"Scardinius erythrophthalmus","niveau":"ok","diff":"Brighter red fins, upturned mouth. Also edible."}],"usages":["The small bones soften when deep-fried very hot or cooked for a long time.","Like all white fish: never raw, some carry liver parasites."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"chevesne":{"nom":"Chub","statut":"Edible cooked · bones","saison":"All year round (depending on rules)","resume":"A large, stocky white fish, very common in the Ourthe and Ardennes rivers. Not much prized because of its bones.","identification":["Thick body, large broad head","Large black-edged scales, in a net pattern","Convex, reddish-orange anal fin"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Dace","latin":"Leuciscus leuciscus","niveau":"ok","diff":"Slimmer, small head, concave (hollowed) anal fin."}],"usages":["Fillet it and score the fillets finely every 3 mm before cooking: the bones become unnoticeable.","Cook well."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"carpe":{"nom":"Carp","statut":"Edible · pollutants if large","saison":"All year round (depending on rules)","resume":"A large fish of still, muddy waters. Old specimens root in the mud and build up pollutants.","identification":["Large golden to bronze body","Four barbels around the mouth","Large scales (or few scales in mirror carp)"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Crucian carp","latin":"Carassius carassius","niveau":"ok","diff":"Smaller, no barbels."}],"usages":["Prefer small carp: large ones (over 5 kg) build up more pollutants.","Remove the skin, the belly fat and the brown line along the flank, where the muddy taste and pollutants concentrate.","Night fishing for carp needs a specific permit in Wallonia."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"barbeau":{"nom":"Barbel","statut":"Edible flesh · poisonous roe","saison":"Depending on rules","resume":"A bottom fish of flowing rivers, like the Ourthe. Its flesh is edible, but its roe is poisonous.","identification":["Elongated body, flat belly","Mouth under the head with four barbels","Brown-green back, reddish fins"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Gudgeon","latin":"Gobio gobio","niveau":"ok","diff":"Very small (under 15 cm), only two barbels, dark spots on the flanks."}],"usages":["NEVER eat barbel roe, especially in spring: it causes vomiting and diarrhoea.","Gut it well and remove the whole roe sac.","Lots of bones: best as scored fillets or in soup."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"silure":{"nom":"Wels catfish","statut":"Edible · large ones polluted","saison":"Depending on rules","resume":"The giant of our rivers, more and more common in the Meuse and its tributaries. Young, it's good; old, it concentrates pollutants.","identification":["Very long scaleless, slimy body","Wide mouth with six barbels, two of them very long","Tiny fin on the back, very long anal fin"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Black bullhead","latin":"Ameiurus melas","niveau":"caution","diff":"Much smaller (under 35 cm), eight barbels, hard spines on the fins."}],"usages":["Prefer fish under 1 m.","Remove the skin and fat.","Careful: a big catfish is very powerful, never wrap the line around your hand."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"poissonchat":{"nom":"Black bullhead","statut":"Edible · painful spines","saison":"All year round (invasive species)","resume":"A small introduced North American fish, invasive in still waters. Edible, but its spines sting badly.","identification":["Scaleless body, brown-black, broad head","Eight barbels","A hard spine at the front of the dorsal fin and of each pectoral fin"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Wels catfish (young)","latin":"Silurus glanis","niveau":"caution","diff":"Six barbels, no spine on the back, very long anal fin."}],"usages":["Grab it behind the pectoral spines, or with a thick cloth.","A sting is very painful and gets infected easily: see the fiche on spine and tooth wounds.","Invasive species: never release it alive elsewhere or carry it alive."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"anguille":{"nom":"European eel","statut":"Release it · heavily polluted","saison":"Must be released (Wallonia)","resume":"A critically endangered species. In Wallonia, every eel caught must be put back. It is also one of the fish most loaded with pollutants (PCBs, dioxins).","identification":["Snake-like body, no belly fins","Dorsal, tail and anal fins fused into a single fringe","Slimy skin, small head with small eyes"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Brook lamprey","latin":"Lampetra planeri","niveau":"danger","diff":"No jaw but a sucker mouth, seven round holes behind the eye. Protected."}],"usages":["Unhook it in the water and release it straight away.","Its raw blood irritates eyes and wounds: rinse your hands after touching it.","Even elsewhere, don't eat it: eels in our rivers often exceed pollutant limits."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"saumon":{"nom":"Atlantic salmon","statut":"Protected · release it","saison":"Catching banned (Belgium)","resume":"It vanished from the Meuse in the 20th century and is being reintroduced. Every fish counts: it must be released at once.","identification":["Young (parr): a row of dark marks on the flanks, clearly forked tail","Adult: silvery body, small black cross-shaped spots above the lateral line","Small adipose fin on the back"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Brown trout","latin":"Salmo trutta","niveau":"ok","diff":"Red spots ringed with pale, less forked tail."}],"usages":["Release it by holding it in the water, facing the current, until it swims off on its own.","Reporting a catch to the Maison wallonne de la Pêche helps monitor the reintroduction."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"ombre":{"nom":"Grayling","statut":"Strictly protected · release it","saison":"Fishing very restricted","resume":"The “fish with the big sail”, a sign of very pure water. Its populations are fragile: release it.","identification":["Very large, colourful sail-shaped dorsal fin","Silvery-grey body with a few black spots at the front","Small adipose fin"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Brown trout","latin":"Salmo trutta","niveau":"ok","diff":"Small dorsal fin, red spots."}],"usages":["Grayling rules are very strict and change from one sector to another: if in doubt, release it."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"chabot":{"nom":"Bullhead (miller's thumb)","statut":"Protected · release it","saison":"Protected all year round","resume":"A small big-headed bottom fish, hidden under the stones of clean streams. Protected in Europe.","identification":["Under 15 cm, very large flattened head","Large fan-shaped pectoral fins","Marbled brown scaleless body, lives under pebbles"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Stone loach","latin":"Barbatula barbatula","niveau":"info","diff":"Long, slim body, six barbels, small head."}],"usages":["Don't turn over stream stones to look for it: they shelter it and many larvae."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"ecrevisse_rouge":{"nom":"Noble crayfish","statut":"Protected · release it","saison":"Protected all year round","resume":"The native crayfish, now very rare because of diseases brought by American crayfish. Strictly protected.","identification":["Large dark brown crayfish","Bright red underside to the claws","Broad, grainy claws"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Signal crayfish","latin":"Pacifastacus leniusculus","niveau":"ok","diff":"White or turquoise patch at the claw joint, smooth claws. Invasive."}],"usages":["Release it on the spot immediately.","After handling American crayfish, dry boots and gear before going into another stream: you could carry crayfish plague."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"ecrevisse_signal":{"nom":"Signal crayfish","statut":"Edible cooked · invasive","saison":"All year round (local rules)","resume":"An introduced, invasive American crayfish, carrying a disease deadly to the native crayfish. Edible and excellent cooked.","identification":["Large bluish-brown crayfish","White or turquoise patch on the claw joint","Red underside to the claws"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Noble crayfish","latin":"Astacus astacus","niveau":"danger","diff":"No pale patch on the claws, grainy claws. Protected: release it."},{"nom":"Spiny-cheek crayfish","latin":"Faxonius limosus","niveau":"ok","diff":"Smaller, rusty patches on the sides of the abdomen, orange-tipped claws."}],"usages":["Always well cooked (5 to 10 minutes in boiling water): it can carry parasites.","Remove the black gut from the tail before eating.","Carrying it alive is banned: an invasive species at EU level.","Catching crayfish is regulated: check the local rules."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"grandevive":{"nom":"Greater weever","statut":"Venomous","saison":"All year round","resume":"The bigger cousin of the lesser weever, sometimes caught on a line from beaches or boats. Its spines stay venomous even after the fish is dead.","identification":["Up to 40 cm, elongated yellow-brown body","Fine dark oblique stripes on the flanks","Short, black, spiny first dorsal fin"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Lesser weever","latin":"Echiichthys vipera","niveau":"danger","diff":"Smaller and stockier, lives buried near the shore."}],"usages":["The spines stay dangerous on a dead fish or on the fish counter: handle with a thick cloth.","Edible once the spines have been removed by someone experienced."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"rascasse":{"nom":"Scorpionfish","statut":"Venomous spines","saison":"All year round","resume":"A Mediterranean rock fish, a master of camouflage. Very good to eat (bouillabaisse), but its spines are venomous.","identification":["Stocky body, mottled red-brown, covered in skin flaps","Large spiny head, big eyes","Dorsal fin bristling with spines"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Greater weever","latin":"Trachinus draco","niveau":"danger","diff":"Long, smooth body, lives on sand."}],"usages":["Never grab with bare hands: thick gloves or a cloth.","Sting: very hot water, as for the weever."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}},"vairon":{"nom":"Minnow","statut":"Small fish to respect","saison":"All year round","resume":"A tiny fish of clear streams, in shoals. A sign of good water quality, and food for trout.","identification":["Under 10 cm, in tight shoals","Flanks mottled with dark patches, belly sometimes red in spring","Very small scales"],"sosies":[{"nom":"Roach (young)","latin":"Rutilus rutilus","niveau":"ok","diff":"Silvery, red eye, large scales."}],"usages":["Don't use it as live bait if the species isn't on the list of allowed baitfish: check the rules.","Too small to be worth eating, except in real distress, fried whole and well cooked."],"notes":{"BE":"In Wallonia, a fishing permit is required on public rivers, canals and lakes. Open seasons, minimum sizes and quotas depend on the species: check the rules of the Maison wallonne de la Pêche."}}}};
